module hc.selfhost.checker;

// The self-hosted checker, phase 1 -- a genuine type checker (literal/operator/call/assignment
// type rules, a flat function-scoped variable environment, accumulated error messages) walking
// the `Expr`/`Stmt`/`FnDecl` AST `Parser.hotc` produces. Deliberately NOT attempting move/borrow
// checking in this phase -- the real `Checker.kt`'s move-tracking (conservative branch merges,
// capture analysis for lambdas, drop ordering) is a whole separate, much harder feature on top
// of plain type checking, and type checking alone is already a real, self-contained milestone
// worth proving out on its own first. Logged as its own follow-up, not silently skipped.
//
// **Structs now real types**: `Ty` gained a `TyStruct { name }` variant (its first *field-
// carrying* variant -- everything else here is a plain unit tag), backed by a real `structs:
// Registry<StructInfo>` field/type table. `register_struct` (called once per `StructDecl` by the
// driver before any fn body is checked, mirroring how `fn_sigs` gets pre-registered) populates
// it; `FieldAccess`/`FieldAssign`/`StructLit` all resolve against it for real instead of the old
// unconditional `TyUnknown`. Any *other* unrecognized type name (a genuine typo, not a struct)
// now also becomes a `TyStruct` tag rather than the old silently-trusted `TyUnknown` -- surfacing
// it as a real mismatch wherever it's used inconsistently, which is strictly more correct, not a
// scope cut.
//
// **Struct methods now real too**: a flat `methods: Registry<FnSig>` (keyed `"StructName#method"`)
// backs `register_method`/`check_method` (called once per impl-block method, mirroring
// `register_struct`) -- `check_method` binds `self` into the variable environment (the struct's
// own type) before checking params/body. `check_method_call` resolves against this table first,
// falling back to the old unconditional `TyUnknown` only for receivers with no registered method
// by that name (covers `String`'s real built-in methods, resolved concretely only in
// `Codegen.hotc`, and genuine typos alike -- a real, disclosed imprecision, not a false-negative
// risk: it costs a missing error message on a typo, never rejects something that should compile).
// Nested struct-typed fields and struct-typed fn params/return types both already work (verified
// in `Codegen.hotc`, not a checker-side change -- `ty_from_name`'s `TyStruct` fallback and
// `find_field_type`'s lookups are already fully general).
//
// **Scope cut, still disclosed**: no interface types (at the time this note was written -- see
// below for what's since landed).
//
// **Real lexical scoping** (added later, "Phase 1" of a small move/borrow-checking roadmap -- see
// README's own "Compile-time safety roadmap" section): variables used to live in one flat,
// whole-function `self.vars` table, so a shadowed `let` inside an `if`/`while` silently reused the
// same slot instead of properly scoping. `TyChecker.var_scopes` (a real stack of enclosing scopes)
// plus `push_scope`/`pop_scope`/`lookup_var` fix this -- see that field's own header for the full
// design. (Enums and static `impl` methods are real now -- see below.)
//
// **Generic enums now real too** (`enum Option<T> { ... }`, mirroring generic structs'
// `Base$Arg`-name on-demand monomorphization): `GenericEnumTemplate` parallels `GenericTemplate`,
// `register_enum` gained a `type_param` param branching into it exactly like `register_struct`
// does, and `ensure_instantiated` gained a second lookup (struct templates, then enum templates)
// substituting `T` across every variant's fields. The one genuinely new problem generic enums
// raise that generic structs don't: a *plain* generic struct's own concrete name already IS its
// instantiation name (`Box<Int> { ... }` directly constructs `"Box$Int"`), but an enum variant's
// bare name (`"Some"`) carries no such information -- two different concrete instantiations of the
// same generic enum used in one program (`Option<Int>` and `Option<String>`) would silently
// collide in the old bare-name-keyed `variant_owner` table (`Registry<T>.get()` returns the FIRST
// match, no shadowing). Fixed two ways: (1) construction sites for a generic enum's variants must
// use the explicit qualified form `Base<Arg>::Variant { ... }` (parsed into `Expr::StructLit`'s
// new `generic_base` field, `Ast.hc`), which `check_struct_lit` resolves directly, bypassing
// `variant_owner` entirely; (2) `check_match`'s variant-ownership check now consults a new
// `variant_owner_q: Registry<Bool>` keyed `"Owner#Variant"` (populated for every enum, plain and
// generic alike) instead of the bare table, since `match` already identifies the subject's own
// concrete type name with no ambiguity. Real bug found building this: `check_match` never called
// `ensure_instantiated` on its subject's type (unlike `check_field_access`/`check_method_call`,
// which already did) -- a generic-enum-typed match subject in a function checked *before* any
// function that actually constructs that instantiation failed every arm with a spurious "'Some' is
// not a variant of 'Option$Int'", since nothing had monomorphized it yet. Fixed by adding the same
// `ensure_instantiated` call `check_match` was missing. Bare unqualified construction (`Some {
// ... }`) still resolves fine for a *non*-generic enum's variants, unchanged.
//
// **Real finding building this file**: `Ty` (an enum) isn't `Copy` -- comparing the SAME value
// against multiple candidates (`if lt == TyInt ... else if lt == TyUnknown ...`) moves it on the
// first comparison, making the second a compile error. The clean fix, used throughout below: call
// `ty_name(t)` (returning a `String`, which IS `Copy`) exactly once per `Ty` value, then do every
// subsequent comparison against that name instead of the enum value itself.

// `Ty`/`FnSig`/`StructInfo`/`GenericTemplate`/`GenericEnumTemplate`/`ExternMethodInfo`, plus
// `ty_from_name`/`ty_name`/`empty_ty_vec`/`param_types_of`, now all live in `Types.hotc` --
// shared with `Codegen.hotc`, which needs this same vocabulary to re-derive a generic-fn
// instantiation's own signature (see that file's own header). Split out for the same single-
// responsibility reason `Ast.hotc`/`Lexer.hotc`/`Parser.hotc` are already their own files: this
// file owns the type-CHECKING algorithm, not the definition of what a type IS.

pub struct TyChecker {
    fn_sigs: Registry<FnSig>,
    // `vars` is only the CURRENT (innermost) lexical scope's own bindings -- `var_scopes` holds
    // every ENCLOSING scope, innermost-last, pushed/popped by `push_scope`/`pop_scope` (called by
    // `check_block` around every `{ ... }`, and by anything else that needs a temporarily-scoped
    // binding, e.g. a lambda literal's own params). A read (`lookup_var`) checks `vars` first, then
    // walks `var_scopes` from the end backwards, so an outer local stays visible inside a nested
    // block unless shadowed, and a nested block's own bindings vanish once that block ends -- real
    // lexical scoping, replacing what used to be one flat, function-wide table (see this file's own
    // header for why that was the original, deliberate simplification).
    vars: Registry<Ty>,
    var_scopes: Vec<Registry<Ty>>,
    structs: Registry<StructInfo>,
    // Keyed by "StructName#methodName" -- a flat map instead of a nested per-struct one, same
    // simplification `find_field_type`'s linear scan makes for field lookups.
    methods: Registry<FnSig>,
    // variant name -> owning enum name. An enum's OWN field table is registered into `structs`
    // under the enum's own name (the union of every variant's fields, see `register_enum`) --
    // `structs` doesn't distinguish "real struct" from "enum, field-lookup purposes" at all, only
    // this table does, and only for telling a `StructLit` construction site (`Name { ... }`)
    // whether `Name` is a plain struct or an enum variant.
    variant_owner: Registry<String>,
    // "OwnerName#VariantName" -> existence, populated for EVERY enum (plain and generic mono
    // instantiations alike) -- `check_match`'s own variant-ownership check uses this instead of
    // `variant_owner` above, since `variant_owner` alone would collide across multiple concrete
    // instantiations of the same generic enum (see `Ast.hc`'s own `EnumDecl` header).
    variant_owner_q: Registry<Bool>,
    generic_templates: Registry<GenericTemplate>,
    generic_enum_templates: Registry<GenericEnumTemplate>,
    // Every monomorphized instantiation name (`"Box$Int"`) actually constructed/accessed while
    // checking the program, in first-use order -- `compile_program` reads this back afterward to
    // know exactly which concrete classes `gen_struct` needs to emit (see `Codegen.hotc`'s own
    // header), since nothing else records "which instantiations does this program really use."
    mono_needed: Vec<String>,
    // extern class alias name -> its real binary name (existence check only -- `Codegen.hotc`
    // needs the actual binary name too, but rebuilds that table itself from the same original
    // `externs: Vec<ExternClassDecl>` rather than reading it back from here, same pattern structs/
    // methods/enums already use).
    externs: Registry<String>,
    // Keyed by "Alias#methodName" -- covers constructors and static methods (`Expr::StaticCall`)
    // and instance methods (`Expr::MethodCall`) alike.
    extern_methods: Registry<ExternMethodInfo>,
    // Keyed by "Alias#fieldName" -- covers both static (`Expr::ExternStaticFieldGet`/`Set`) and
    // instance (`Expr::FieldAccess`/`FieldAssign`, once `on` resolves to a known extern alias
    // instead of a struct) fields alike.
    extern_fields: Registry<ExternFieldInfo>,
    // struct name -> the extern-class alias it `extends`, present only for a real `extends`
    // struct (see `Ast.hc`'s own `StructDecl.extends` header). `check_static_call`'s `::new`
    // fallback is the only reader.
    struct_extends: Registry<String>,
    // Top-level fn name -> `true` for every `@must_use`-marked fn (see `Ast.hc`'s own
    // `Program.must_use_fn_names` header). `check_stmt`'s `ExprStmt` arm is the only reader --
    // a bare `Expr.Call` to one of these, with nothing consuming its return value, is a real error.
    must_use_fns: Registry<Bool>,
    // Top-level fn name -> its own declared param names, in order -- see README's own "Named
    // arguments" section. `resolve_named_args_chk`'s own header. Scoped to plain top-level fns
    // only this pass (not struct methods, `extern class` methods, or generic templates) --
    // registered once, in `Codegen.hotc`'s own `compile_program` fn-registration loop, alongside
    // (not replacing) `fn_sigs`'s own registration there.
    fn_param_names: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    // Enum name -> its own variant names, declaration order -- `check_match` reads this to check
    // exhaustiveness (every variant covered by name, or a `_` wildcard arm present).
    enum_variants: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    // Keyed by "StructName#methodName", same flat convention `methods` (instance) uses -- called
    // via `Expr::StaticCall` (`StructName::method(args)`), extern classes checked first, this as
    // the fallback -- see `check_static_call`.
    static_methods: Registry<FnSig>,
    // A generic top-level fn declaration (`fn identity<T>(x: T) -> T { ... }`), kept separate from
    // `fn_sigs` (real, directly-callable signatures) until some call site's inferred type argument
    // asks for a concrete instantiation -- mirrors `generic_templates`/`generic_enum_templates`,
    // except the type argument is INFERRED at each call site rather than spelled out (see
    // `check_call`'s own header).
    generic_fn_templates: Registry<FnDecl>,
    // Every monomorphized FUNCTION instantiation name (`"identity$Int"`) actually called while
    // checking the program, in first-use order -- mirrors `mono_needed`, read back by
    // `compile_program` afterward to know which concrete fn bodies `Codegen.hotc` needs to emit.
    fn_mono_needed: Vec<String>,
    // An instance method declared inside a GENERIC struct's own `impl` block (`impl Box<T> { fn
    // get(&self) -> T { ... } }`), keyed "StructName#methodName" like `methods` above -- kept
    // separate until some method-call site's RECEIVER (already a concrete `"Box$Int"`-shaped
    // instantiation) asks for it. Unlike a generic top-level fn's own call-site inference (scanning
    // params for one whose type is bare `T`), a generic method's `T` needs no scanning at all --
    // the receiver's own mono name already names it directly (`mono_arg("Box$Int")` = `"Int"`), see
    // `ensure_method_instantiated`. Scope cut, disclosed: only instance methods (`&self`/`&mut
    // self`) get this treatment -- a `static fn` inside a generic struct's `impl` block is a rarer
    // shape (the real prelude's own `Vec<T>`/`Registry<T>` don't use one) and stays unsupported.
    generic_method_templates: Registry<FnDecl>,
    // Every monomorphized METHOD instantiation key (`"Box$Int#get"`) actually called while checking
    // the program -- mirrors `fn_mono_needed`, read back by `compile_program` afterward to know
    // which concrete method bodies `Codegen.hotc` needs to emit onto their owning mono struct's own
    // class file (not the main program class, unlike a plain generic fn -- see `Codegen.hotc`'s own
    // header).
    method_mono_needed: Vec<String>,
    // Top-level `pub static NAME: Type = expr;` name -> declared type -- unlike `vars` (reset
    // fresh per fn/method), this is registered once up front and stays visible everywhere, real
    // global-constant semantics. `check_ident` checks `vars` first so a local can still shadow a
    // same-named static.
    statics: Registry<Ty>,
    errors: Vec<String>,
    // How many `while` bodies deep the checker currently is -- `Break`/`Continue` are only legal
    // while this is > 0. Incremented/decremented around a `While`'s own body check; no stack of
    // labels needed since this subset never needs to know WHICH enclosing loop, only whether one
    // exists at all.
    loop_depth: Int,
    // The fn/method currently being checked own declared return type name (set fresh at the top
    // of `check_fn`/`check_method`, mirrors `Codegen.hotc`'s own `ret_ty` field) -- read by
    // `check_stmt`'s `Return` arm to supply `check_struct_lit`'s `expected_mono` hint for a bare
    // generic struct literal (`return Vec { ... };`, see that fn's own header).
    cur_ret_ty: String,
    // Bare variant name -> THAT variant's own field table alone (not the enum-wide union
    // `structs` registers it under). Real gap found self-compiling `Parser.hotc`/`Ast.hotc`:
    // `structs.register(enumName, ...)` concatenates EVERY variant's fields into one flat table
    // (disclosed already -- "field lookups... don't distinguish which variant a field actually
    // belongs" -- this file's own header), so `find_field_type`'s linear scan returns the FIRST
    // same-named field it finds across the WHOLE enum, regardless of which variant is actually
    // being constructed. Harmless as long as no two variants reuse a field name at different
    // types -- exactly what `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr` enum does (`IntLit.value: Int`, `Assign.value:
    // Expr`, `StringLit.value: String`, ...), so every one of those was silently validated
    // against `IntLit`'s `Int` (registered first) until this. `check_struct_lit` prefers this
    // table over the enum-wide union whenever the name being constructed is a known variant.
    variant_field_info: Registry<StructInfo>,
    // Bare variant name -> owning GENERIC enum's own BASE name (e.g. "Some"/"None" -> "Option"),
    // registered once at DECLARATION time (`register_enum`'s `is_generic` branch) -- unlike
    // `variant_owner` (which only ever learns about a generic enum's variants AFTER some concrete
    // instantiation happens, via `ensure_instantiated`'s own call back into `register_enum`), this
    // is populated immediately, so a bare `Some { ... }`/`None` construction site (real
    // `Parser.hotc` usage -- `none_expr`/`some_expr`-style helpers, all in return position) can
    // resolve its OWNER even before anything has triggered the FIRST-EVER instantiation of
    // `Option<T>` anywhere in the program. See `check_struct_lit`'s own header for how the actual
    // type argument then gets filled in (from `expected_mono`).
    generic_variant_owner: Registry<String>,
    // Interface name -> its own declared method table -- see `register_interface`/
    // `check_method_call`'s own "dyn" branch. `struct_interfaces` (struct name -> every interface
    // it directly implements -- a struct implementing more than one is real now, not the
    // one-interface limit `ImplDecl` used to disclose) is read by `is_arg_type_mismatch`/
    // `sealed_implementers`/`check_sealed_match`, and also shared with `Codegen.hotc`'s own
    // `compile_program` (which reuses this same `TyChecker` instance) for the struct's own real
    // JVM `implements` list.
    interfaces: Registry<Vec<InterfaceMethod>>,
    struct_interfaces: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    // interface name -> every OTHER interface it transitively `extends` (not including itself) --
    // `is_arg_type_mismatch`'s own reader, so passing a struct implementing `Child` where a
    // `&dyn Parent` is expected widens correctly through `Child`'s own inheritance chain instead of
    // just comparing the struct's ONE direct interface name against the expected one as bare
    // strings. See `Ast.hc`'s own `transitive_supertraits` header.
    interface_supertraits: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    // Existence set (struct name -> `true`) -- which struct names are `arena struct` declarations,
    // populated by `register_struct`'s own `is_arena` flag. See `StructDecl`'s own header for the
    // real, disclosed scope this whole feature covers.
    arena_structs: Registry<Bool>,
    // Existence set (interface name -> `true`) -- which interfaces were declared `sealed
    // interface Name { ... }`. Only a sealed interface can be `match`ed on at all -- see
    // `check_sealed_match`'s own header for why (exhaustiveness needs a closed, known-in-advance
    // set of implementors; a plain, non-sealed `interface` never promises that).
    sealed_interfaces: Registry<Bool>,
    // === Move checking (Phase 2 of the roadmap -- see README's own "Compile-time safety roadmap"
    // section) ===
    //
    // Per-param "was this declared `&`/`&mut`" flags, mirroring `fn_param_names`/`method_param_
    // names`'s own "keyed by fn name" / "keyed StructName#method" conventions -- `Ast.hc`'s own
    // `Param.is_ref` header explains why the parser only just started tracking this at all. A
    // by-ref param never moves its argument; a plain by-value one does. Deliberately scoped to
    // plain top-level fn calls and plain (non-extern, non-generic) struct method calls only --
    // extern calls, generic calls, and a method's own receiver (`self`'s ref-kind still isn't
    // tracked, see that field's header) are a disclosed cut: their arguments are never treated as
    // moves, so this errs toward under-detecting rather than rejecting code that should compile.
    fn_param_is_ref: Registry<Vec<Bool>>,
    method_param_is_ref: Registry<Vec<Bool>>,
    static_method_param_is_ref: Registry<Vec<Bool>>,
    // Which locals are currently moved-out-of, current scope only -- `var_scopes`'s exact same
    // "current frame + enclosing stack" split, kept as a SEPARATE parallel stack (`moved_scopes`)
    // rather than folded into `Ty` itself, so a variable's OWN declaring scope frame (found by
    // walking `var_scopes` the same way `lookup_var` does) is where its moved-flag actually lives
    // -- critical so that moving a variable declared in an OUTER scope, from INSIDE a nested
    // block, correctly stays moved after that block ends (not reset just because the block's own
    // scope popped). `mark_moved`/`unmark_moved`/`is_moved` are the real read/write path; `push_
    // scope`/`pop_scope` push/pop this in lockstep with `var_scopes`/`vars`, and `check_stmt`'s own
    // `If` arm additionally snapshots/restores/unions it around a conditional's two branches (see
    // `snapshot_moved_state`'s own header) since only ONE branch of an `if` actually runs.
    moved: Registry<Bool>,
    moved_scopes: Vec<Registry<Bool>>,
    // === Borrow checking (Phase 3 of the roadmap -- see README's own "Compile-time safety
    // roadmap" section, and its "`&mut` vs `&`" section for the full rule set this enforces) ===
    //
    // Per-param "was this declared `&mut` specifically" flags -- `fn_param_is_ref`'s own exact
    // shape/header, but for the shared/exclusive distinction move-checking never needed. A call
    // argument landing on an `is_mut_ref` param must itself be `mut_capable` (below); an argument
    // NOT landing on one must not alias one that IS, within the same call (the exclusivity rule).
    fn_param_is_mut_ref: Registry<Vec<Bool>>,
    method_param_is_mut_ref: Registry<Vec<Bool>>,
    static_method_param_is_mut_ref: Registry<Vec<Bool>>,
    // Which locals can currently have their OWN fields assigned into, or be re-borrowed `&mut` --
    // true for a `var` local, an `&mut`-declared param, or `self` in an `&mut self` method; false
    // for a `let` local, a plain by-value param (no `mut` qualifier exists for one), a `&`-borrowed
    // param, or `self` in a `&self`/bare-`self` method. Exact same "current scope + enclosing
    // stack, walked to find the owning frame" shape as `moved`/`moved_scopes` -- see that field's
    // own header -- because the SAME concern applies: a param's mutability doesn't change once
    // it's this fn's own local, but needs to be found regardless of which nested block asks.
    mut_capable: Registry<Bool>,
    mut_capable_scopes: Vec<Registry<Bool>>,
    // === ECS with ownership-derived scheduling (see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own design doc) ===
    //
    // Existence set (component name -> `true`) -- which struct-shaped declarations came from a
    // `component Name { ... }` (as opposed to an ordinary `struct`), so a `system`'s own `run`
    // params can be validated against it. `register_component` also registers the SAME name into
    // `self.structs` (a component's field layout works exactly like a struct's for every other
    // purpose -- field access, construction, `Codegen.hotc`'s own struct codegen once that's
    // wired up) -- this registry is the ONE extra fact needed on top of that.
    components: Registry<Bool>,
    // Same shape as `components` right above, but for `resource Name { ... }` -- a SEPARATE
    // registry (not folded into `components`) so a resource is never accidentally archetype-
    // matched by a system's own ordinary component-borrow check, and a component is never
    // accidentally treated as a world-wide singleton. See `Ast.hc`'s own `Program.resources`
    // header for why this exists at all (the real gap Marshmallow's ECS ring surfaced: no way to
    // get live external per-frame state -- a camera, input, delta time -- into a system).
    resources: Registry<Bool>,
    // Non-fatal diagnostics -- unlike `errors` (which abort codegen once any exist, see
    // `compile_program`'s own `has_real_errors` gate), a warning never blocks compilation. First
    // real use: the scheduling-conflict warning `check_systems` emits when two systems share a
    // component and at least one writes it (see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Resolved decisions" #6) --
    // named here generally rather than `schedule_warnings` since nothing else about this field is
    // ECS-specific; a future feature needing a genuine warning (not a hard error) has a place to
    // put it without inventing a second mechanism.
    warnings: Vec<String>,
    // The real execution order `check_systems` derives for every `system` in the program --
    // topologically sorted by `@after`/`@before` constraints, declaration order as the tie-break
    // for anything those constraints don't decide (see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Resolved decisions"
    // #4). Nothing reads this yet (`Codegen.hotc` doesn't generate anything for `system`/
    // `component` at all yet) -- computed and validated now (including real cycle detection) so
    // the codegen slice has real, already-checked data to consume instead of redoing this work.
    system_order: Vec<String>,
    // The SAME order as `system_order`, grouped: each inner `Vec<String>` is a set of systems
    // that can run in the same parallel batch (mutually ready AND pairwise non-conflicting --
    // see `check_systems`'s own header for the real algorithm). A group of size 1 just means
    // "nothing else was both ready and conflict-free at this point" -- `Codegen.hotc`'s own
    // `build_run_all_body_ecs` reads this to decide whether a batch is a direct sequential call
    // (size 1) or a real Phoenix-Flight-pool dispatch + join (size > 1).
    system_groups: Vec<Vec<String>>,
    // Every distinct "archetype signature" (the as-written, order-sensitive list of component
    // names) actually spawned anywhere in the program via `world.spawn(...)` -- see `check_method_
    // call_by_type`'s own `World#spawn` gate for how this gets populated, and `Codegen.hotc`'s own
    // archetype-synthesis pass for the only reader.
    archetype_signatures: Vec<Vec<String>>,
    // Every distinct component type ever named as `world.add(_, ThisType { ... })`'s own second
    // argument, anywhere in the program -- deduped. `Codegen.hotc`'s own archetype-transition
    // synthesis (see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Archetype-transition mechanics") uses this, alongside
    // `remove_component_types` below, to compute the FULL closure of archetype signatures the
    // program could ever actually need (every signature reachable from `archetype_signatures`'
    // own spawn-derived starting set by adding/removing any of these types) -- not just the ones
    // literally spawned, since a `world.add(...)`/`world.remove(...)` call site can move an
    // entity into an archetype nothing ever spawned into directly.
    add_component_types: Vec<String>,
    // The `world.remove(_, ThisType)` sibling of `add_component_types` -- same dedup, same
    // closure-computation reader, `ThisType` named as a bare identifier (there's no value to
    // construct for a removal) rather than a struct literal.
    remove_component_types: Vec<String>,
    // `world.get(id)` -- see ECS_IDEAS.md's own "Still open" #6 answer. Every DISTINCT component type
    // ever queried this way, anywhere in the program (deduplicated, declaration-order-ish but not
    // load-bearing) -- `Codegen.hotc`'s own `compile_program` is the only reader, synthesizing
    // exactly one `__world_get_<T>` helper fn per entry (see `build_world_get_fn_ecs`'s own
    // header) before the main fn-compilation loop runs, mirroring `synthesize_system_dispatch_fn_
    // ecs`'s own "materialize a real fn, reuse the ordinary compile pipeline" strategy.
    queried_component_types: Vec<String>,
}

pub fn checker_new() -> TyChecker {
    var sigs = registry_new("__init__", FnSig { param_types: empty_ty_vec(), ret_type: TyUnknown });
    var vars = registry_new("__init__", TyUnknown);
    var structs = registry_new("__init__", StructInfo { field_names: empty_str_vec2(), field_types: empty_str_vec2() });
    var methods = registry_new("__init__", FnSig { param_types: empty_ty_vec(), ret_type: TyUnknown });
    var variant_owner = registry_new("__init__", "__init__");
    var variant_owner_q = registry_new("__init__", true);
    var generic_templates = registry_new("__init__", GenericTemplate { type_param: "__init__", field_names: empty_str_vec2(), field_types: empty_str_vec2(), type_param2: none_string3() });
    var generic_enum_templates = registry_new("__init__", GenericEnumTemplate { type_param: "__init__", variants: empty_variant_vec3(), type_param2: none_string3() });
    var externs = registry_new("__init__", "__init__");
    var extern_methods = registry_new("__init__", ExternMethodInfo { param_types: empty_str_vec2(), ret_type: "Unit", is_static: false, is_ctor: false });
    var extern_fields = registry_new("__init__", ExternFieldInfo { type_name: "Unit", is_static: false });
    var struct_extends = registry_new("__init__", "__init__");
    var must_use_fns = registry_new("__init__", true);
    var fn_param_names = registry_new("__init__", empty_str_vec2());
    var enum_variants = registry_new("__init__", empty_str_vec2());
    var static_methods = registry_new("__init__", FnSig { param_types: empty_ty_vec(), ret_type: TyUnknown });
    var generic_fn_templates = registry_new("__init__", FnDecl { name: "__init__", params: empty_param_vec3(), ret_type: none_string3(), body: empty_stmt_vec3(), type_param: none_string3(), type_bounds: empty_str_vec2(), self_is_mut: false, type_param2: none_string3(), type_bounds2: empty_str_vec2() });
    var generic_method_templates = registry_new("__init__", FnDecl { name: "__init__", params: empty_param_vec3(), ret_type: none_string3(), body: empty_stmt_vec3(), type_param: none_string3(), type_bounds: empty_str_vec2(), self_is_mut: false, type_param2: none_string3(), type_bounds2: empty_str_vec2() });
    var mono = vec_of("__init__");
    var mono_needed = Vec { data: mono.data, len: 0 };
    var fmono = vec_of("__init__");
    var fn_mono_needed = Vec { data: fmono.data, len: 0 };
    var mmono = vec_of("__init__");
    var method_mono_needed = Vec { data: mmono.data, len: 0 };
    var statics = registry_new("__init__", TyUnknown);
    var errs = vec_of("__init__");
    errs.set(0, "");
    var variant_field_info = registry_new("__init__", StructInfo { field_names: empty_str_vec2(), field_types: empty_str_vec2() });
    var generic_variant_owner = registry_new("__init__", "__init__");
    var interfaces = registry_new("__init__", vec_of(InterfaceMethod { name: "__init__", params: empty_param_vec3(), ret_type: "Unit", body: none_stmt_vec() }));
    var struct_interfaces = registry_new("__init__", empty_str_vec2());
    var arena_structs = registry_new("__init__", true);
    var sealed_interfaces = registry_new("__init__", true);
    var interface_supertraits = registry_new("__init__", empty_str_vec2());
    var vscopes_seed = vec_of(vars);
    var var_scopes = Vec { data: vscopes_seed.data, len: 0 };
    var fn_param_is_ref = registry_new("__init__", empty_bool_vec_chk());
    var method_param_is_ref = registry_new("__init__", empty_bool_vec_chk());
    var static_method_param_is_ref = registry_new("__init__", empty_bool_vec_chk());
    var moved = registry_new("__init__", false);
    var mvscopes_seed = vec_of(moved);
    var moved_scopes = Vec { data: mvscopes_seed.data, len: 0 };
    var fn_param_is_mut_ref = registry_new("__init__", empty_bool_vec_chk());
    var method_param_is_mut_ref = registry_new("__init__", empty_bool_vec_chk());
    var static_method_param_is_mut_ref = registry_new("__init__", empty_bool_vec_chk());
    var mut_capable = registry_new("__init__", false);
    var mcscopes_seed = vec_of(mut_capable);
    var mut_capable_scopes = Vec { data: mcscopes_seed.data, len: 0 };
    var components = registry_new("__init__", true);
    var resources = registry_new("__init__", true);
    var warns = vec_of("__init__");
    warns.set(0, "");
    var system_order = empty_str_vec2();
    var system_groups = empty_str_vec_vec_ecs();
    var archetype_signatures = empty_str_vec_vec_ecs();
    var add_component_types = empty_str_vec2();
    var remove_component_types = empty_str_vec2();
    var queried_component_types = empty_str_vec2();
    return TyChecker { fn_sigs: sigs, vars: vars, var_scopes: var_scopes, structs: structs, methods: methods, variant_owner: variant_owner, variant_owner_q: variant_owner_q, generic_templates: generic_templates, generic_enum_templates: generic_enum_templates, mono_needed: mono_needed, externs: externs, extern_methods: extern_methods, extern_fields: extern_fields, struct_extends: struct_extends, must_use_fns: must_use_fns, fn_param_names: fn_param_names, enum_variants: enum_variants, static_methods: static_methods, generic_fn_templates: generic_fn_templates, fn_mono_needed: fn_mono_needed, generic_method_templates: generic_method_templates, method_mono_needed: method_mono_needed, statics: statics, errors: errs, loop_depth: 0, cur_ret_ty: "Unit", variant_field_info: variant_field_info, generic_variant_owner: generic_variant_owner, interfaces: interfaces, struct_interfaces: struct_interfaces, arena_structs: arena_structs, sealed_interfaces: sealed_interfaces, interface_supertraits: interface_supertraits, fn_param_is_ref: fn_param_is_ref, method_param_is_ref: method_param_is_ref, static_method_param_is_ref: static_method_param_is_ref, moved: moved, moved_scopes: moved_scopes, fn_param_is_mut_ref: fn_param_is_mut_ref, method_param_is_mut_ref: method_param_is_mut_ref, static_method_param_is_mut_ref: static_method_param_is_mut_ref, mut_capable: mut_capable, mut_capable_scopes: mut_capable_scopes, components: components, resources: resources, warnings: warns, system_order: system_order, system_groups: system_groups, archetype_signatures: archetype_signatures, add_component_types: add_component_types, remove_component_types: remove_component_types, queried_component_types: queried_component_types };
}

pub fn empty_bool_vec_chk() -> Vec<Bool> {
    var v = vec_of(true);
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

pub fn empty_str_vec2() -> Vec<String> {
    var v = vec_of("__init__");
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

pub fn param_type_names_of4(params: Vec<Param>) -> Vec<String> {
    var out = empty_str_vec2();
    var i = 0;
    while i < params.length() {
        out.push(params.get(i).type_name);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// Joins param/arg type names with "," into one key fragment -- used to build an extern method's
// full-signature registry key (see `register_extern`'s own header), so two same-arity overloads
// (Gson's `addProperty(String,String)` vs `addProperty(String,Number)`) get distinct keys.
pub fn join_type_names_chk(names: &Vec<String>) -> String {
    var out = "";
    var i = 0;
    while i < names.length() {
        if i > 0 { out = out + ","; }
        out = out + names.get(i);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

pub fn opt_string_or2(o: Option<String>, default_val: String) -> String {
    if let Some { value: v } = o {
        return v;
    }
    return default_val;
}

// Linear scan for a field's declared type name, "?" if the struct has no such field -- mirrors
// `Ty`'s own "?" sentinel for "unknown, don't error on it" so callers can reuse the same
// `!= "?"` guard pattern already established throughout this file.
// The ONE central place a struct/enum-variant field's own declared type gets read back for
// EVERY caller (a plain field access, a match arm's own bind-name lookup, ...) -- a ranged-Int
// field (`"Int<0..=100>"`, see `Parser.hotc`'s own `type_name_ref` header) is normalized back to
// plain `"Int"` right here, the single spot that makes "a ranged Int behaves as an ordinary Int
// everywhere else once the check passes" (the same promise this feature's own `Let`-statement
// version already keeps) hold for struct fields too, without needing every ordinary consumer of
// a field's type (arithmetic, comparisons, argument passing, ...) to separately learn to treat
// the ranged string as compatible with plain `Int`. The range is enforced ONLY at the one
// WRITE/construction boundary (`check_struct_lit`'s own matching branch) -- reading the field
// back never re-checks it (same "not tracked through computation" scope cut the `Let` version
// already discloses).
pub fn find_field_type(info: StructInfo, field: String) -> String {
    var i = 0;
    while i < info.field_names.length() {
        if info.field_names.get(i) == field {
            let ft = info.field_types.get(i);
            if is_ranged_int_type_chk(ft) { return "Int"; }
            return ft;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return "?";
}

// The RAW (un-normalized) twin of `find_field_type`, right above -- `check_struct_lit`'s own
// per-field construction loop is the ONE caller that needs the ranged-Int annotation still
// intact (to run its own compile-time literal check against the real declared bounds); every
// OTHER caller wants the normalized `"Int"` `find_field_type` itself already returns. A real bug
// found running a real struct-field example: `check_struct_lit` originally called the NORMALIZING
// `find_field_type` for its own `expected` and tried to detect "is this ranged" from THAT
// already-stripped string, which is always false by construction -- silently skipping the
// compile-time check entirely (the runtime check, unaffected since it reads the raw type
// straight from `Codegen.hotc`'s own separate `struct_ftypes` registry, still caught it, but only
// at runtime, defeating the whole point of the compile-time half).
pub fn find_field_type_raw(info: StructInfo, field: String) -> String {
    var i = 0;
    while i < info.field_names.length() {
        if info.field_names.get(i) == field {
            return info.field_types.get(i);
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return "?";
}

extern class JStringChk = "java.lang.String" {
    fn length(&self) -> Int;
    fn codePointAt(&self, index: Int) -> Int;
    fn substring(&self, start: Int, end: Int) -> String;
    fn indexOf(&self, s: String) -> Int;
}

// `recv.field` (property-style access) capitalizes `field`'s own first letter to build the real
// Java-bean method name (`"health"` -> `"Health"`, for `"get"`/`"is"`/`"set"` + that to prefix) --
// see README's own "Property-style access" section. No `String.toUpperCase()` in this file's own
// intrinsic set above, so built from `codePointAt`/`codepoint_to_string` directly (ASCII
// lowercase-to-uppercase is just "subtract 32" -- ordinary field names are ASCII identifiers,
// nothing here needs full Unicode case-folding).
pub fn capitalize_first_chk(s: String) -> String {
    if s.length() == 0 { return s; }
    let c0 = s.codePointAt(0);
    var cap0 = c0;
    if c0 >= 97 && c0 <= 122 { cap0 = c0 - 32; }
    return codepoint_to_string(cap0) + s.substring(1, s.length());
}

// Monomorphized generic instantiations are named `"Base$Arg"` -- `$` specifically because it can
// never appear in a real HC identifier (see `Parser.hotc`'s `type_name_ref` header), so this
// split is unambiguous with any real user-written name.
pub fn is_mono_name(name: String) -> Bool {
    return name.indexOf("$") >= 0;
}

pub fn mono_base(name: String) -> String {
    let idx = name.indexOf("$");
    return name.substring(0, idx);
}

pub fn mono_arg(name: String) -> String {
    let idx = name.indexOf("$");
    return name.substring(idx + 1, name.length());
}

// `Result<T, E>` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `EnumDecl.type_param2` header, and `ensure_instantiated`'s
// own enum branch for why a two-param mono name's arg-splitting needs its own logic instead of
// reusing `mono_arg` (which returns EVERYTHING after the first `$`, correct for a one-param
// template whose own single arg might itself be a nested mono name, but wrong here).
pub fn mono_arg1_of2(name: String) -> String {
    let idx1 = name.indexOf("$");
    let rest = name.substring(idx1 + 1, name.length());
    let idx2 = rest.indexOf("$");
    return rest.substring(0, idx2);
}
pub fn mono_arg2_of2(name: String) -> String {
    let idx1 = name.indexOf("$");
    let rest = name.substring(idx1 + 1, name.length());
    let idx2 = rest.indexOf("$");
    return rest.substring(idx2 + 1, rest.length());
}

// `true` iff `name` is a genuine TWO-argument mono name (`"Result$Int$String"`, not a one-param
// mono name like `"Box$Int"` whose own single arg happens to be a nested mono name) -- the `?`
// operator's own `check_try_op` needs this BEFORE calling `mono_arg1_of2`/`mono_arg2_of2`
// (neither of which detects the one-arg case safely on its own: `mono_arg2_of2("Box$Int")` would
// return `"Int"` again, not `""`, since there's no second `$` to bound it against).
pub fn is_two_part_mono_chk(name: String) -> Bool {
    let idx1 = name.indexOf("$");
    if idx1 < 0 { return false; }
    let rest = name.substring(idx1 + 1, name.length());
    return rest.indexOf("$") >= 0;
}

pub fn empty_param_vec3() -> Vec<Param> {
    var v = vec_of(Param { name: "_", type_name: "_", is_ref: false, is_mut_ref: false });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

pub fn empty_variant_vec3() -> Vec<EnumVariant> {
    var v = vec_of(EnumVariant { name: "_", fields: empty_param_vec3() });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

pub fn none_string3() -> Option<String> {
    return None;
}

pub fn some_string3(s: String) -> Option<String> {
    return Some { value: s };
}

pub fn empty_stmt_vec3() -> Vec<Stmt> {
    var v = vec_of(ExprStmt { expr: IntLit { value: 0 }, line: 0 });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

// Array types are represented as the plain string `"[ElemType]"` throughout this file (see
// `Parser.hotc`'s own `type_name_ref` header) -- these two helpers are the only place that
// convention actually gets parsed back apart.
pub fn is_array_type_name(name: String) -> Bool {
    if name.length() < 2 { return false; }
    return name.codePointAt(0) == 91 && name.codePointAt(name.length() - 1) == 93; // '[' / ']'
}

pub fn array_elem_type_name(name: String) -> String {
    return name.substring(1, name.length() - 1);
}

// `"SecurityManager?"` -- a nullable type, same "plain suffixed type-name string" convention
// `is_array_type_name`/`is_dyn_type_name_chk` above already use for their own prefixes/suffixes.
// See README's own "Nullable types" section: this pass scopes nullable to `TyStruct`-shaped
// (struct/extern-class) values only -- `strip_nullable` on a bare `"Int"`/`"Bool"`/etc is never
// called (nothing ever suffixes those), so it doesn't need to guard against them.
pub fn is_nullable_type_name(name: String) -> Bool {
    if name.length() < 2 { return false; }
    return name.codePointAt(name.length() - 1) == 63; // '?'
}

pub fn strip_nullable(name: String) -> String {
    return name.substring(0, name.length() - 1);
}

pub fn is_numeric_ty_name_chk(name: String) -> Bool {
    return name == "Int" || name == "Long" || name == "Float" || name == "Double" || name == "Char" || name == "Byte";
}

// Move-checking's own "never needs tracking" set -- same primitives/`String` every other Copy-ish
// convention in this compiler already treats as safe to freely duplicate (mirrors `Codegen.hotc`'s
// own `is_sendable_type_cg`'s primitive list, though NOT its `@sendable`-struct exception --
// nothing struct-shaped is Copy for move-checking purposes, sendable or not). `"?"` (an unknown/
// uninferred type) is ALSO treated as Copy -- trust it rather than block on a checker gap, same
// "when unsure, don't reject" honesty `is_arg_type_mismatch` already has elsewhere.
pub fn is_copy_ty_name_chk(name: String) -> Bool {
    return name == "Int" || name == "Bool" || name == "Long" || name == "Float" || name == "Double" || name == "Char" || name == "Byte" || name == "String" || name == "?";
}

// Does `body` unconditionally diverge (its own last statement is `return`/`throw`)? Same simple
// "just check the last statement" heuristic the narrowing logic elsewhere in this file already
// uses for the identical question -- not real reachability analysis (a `return` buried inside a
// nested `if` two levels down that actually covers every path isn't detected), but good enough to
// avoid the single most common false positive move-checking's own `If`-merge would otherwise hit:
// an `if special_case { ...moves x...; return y; } return x;` -- the `return x` is only ever
// reached on the path where the `if` body DIDN'T run, so it must not see that branch's own moves.
pub fn stmt_list_diverges_chk(body: &Vec<Stmt>) -> Bool {
    if body.length() == 0 { return false; }
    let last = body.get(body.length() - 1);
    match last {
        Return { value, line } => { return true; }
        Throw { value, line } => { return true; }
        _ => { return false; }
    }
}

// ECS scheduling helpers (see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own design doc, and `TyChecker.check_systems`'s
// own header for the real analysis these support).

// An empty `Vec<Vec<String>>` -- `check_systems`'s own per-system read/write-set accumulator,
// same "seed via a real value, then reset `len` to 0" convention every other `empty_*_vec`
// helper in this file already uses.
pub fn empty_str_vec_vec_ecs() -> Vec<Vec<String>> {
    var seed = vec_of(empty_str_vec2());
    return Vec { data: seed.data, len: 0 };
}

// Exact, order-sensitive equality between two `Vec<String>` -- `check_method_call_by_type`'s own
// `World#spawn` gate uses this to dedup archetype signatures (see that fn's own header for why
// this is order-sensitive rather than canonicalized/sorted).
pub fn str_vec_eq_ecs(a: &Vec<String>, b: &Vec<String>) -> Bool {
    if a.length() != b.length() { return false; }
    var i = 0;
    while i < a.length() {
        if a.get(i) != b.get(i) { return false; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

pub fn vec_str_vec_contains_ecs(list: &Vec<Vec<String>>, sig: &Vec<String>) -> Bool {
    var i = 0;
    while i < list.length() {
        if str_vec_eq_ecs(&list.get(i), &sig) { return true; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return false;
}

pub fn str_vec_contains_ecs(v: &Vec<String>, s: String) -> Bool {
    var i = 0;
    while i < v.length() {
        if v.get(i) == s { return true; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return false;
}

// A declared `Option<Transform>` type annotation is stored as `"Option$Transform"` -- see
// `Parser.hotc`'s own `type_name_ref` header: EVERY generic type annotation this compiler parses
// already gets built as the internal `"Base$Arg"` mono-name string directly (no separate angle-
// bracket-syntax `Ty` shape ever exists), the exact same convention `mono_base`/`mono_arg` (right
// above) already read/write for a MONOMORPHIZED struct/enum instantiation's own name -- this is
// genuinely the SAME shape, just reused here for the one thing this compiler doesn't already
// monomorphize at parse time (there's no `Option<Transform>` struct/enum declaration anywhere to
// instantiate -- `world.get`'s own RESULT type is assembled from a runtime query, not a real
// generic construction). `"Option$Transform"` -> `Some("Transform")`; anything not shaped like a
// single-type-argument `Option$...` (no declared type at all, a different generic, `Option` with
// zero/two+ type args) -> `None`.
pub fn option_inner_type_chk(s: String) -> Option<String> {
    if !is_mono_name(s) { return none_string3(); }
    if mono_base(s) != "Option" { return none_string3(); }
    return some_string3(mono_arg(s));
}

// `true` iff `name` was declared via `resource Name { ... }` (registered into `resources`, a
// `Registry<Bool>` -- see that registry's own header). A plain boolean wrapper around `Registry
// <T>.get`'s own `Option<T>` result, so `check_systems`'s own `else if` chain can test it
// directly instead of needing an `if let` inside a condition position.
pub fn resource_known_ecs(resources: &Registry<Bool>, name: String) -> Bool {
    let found = resources.get(name);
    if let Some { value: _rk } = found { return true; }
    return false;
}

// The FIRST component name found in `haystack` that also appears in `needle` -- used purely to
// name ONE concrete component in a conflict warning message; which one (if several) is
// unspecified, matching this checker's own established "report the first match" convention
// elsewhere (`find_field_type`, `Registry<T>.get`).
pub fn first_shared_component_ecs(haystack: &Vec<String>, needle: &Vec<String>) -> String {
    var i = 0;
    while i < haystack.length() {
        let c = haystack.get(i);
        if str_vec_contains_ecs(needle, c) { return c; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return "";
}

// Do two systems conflict? True iff there's a component at least one of them WRITES that the
// other one also touches (reads or writes) -- two readers of the same component never conflict.
// See `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Checker" section for why this, not a general alias analysis, is
// exactly the signal `&`/`&mut` on a `run` param already gives for free.
pub fn systems_conflict_ecs(reads_a: &Vec<String>, writes_a: &Vec<String>, reads_b: &Vec<String>, writes_b: &Vec<String>) -> Bool {
    var i = 0;
    while i < writes_a.length() {
        let c = writes_a.get(i);
        if str_vec_contains_ecs(reads_b, c) || str_vec_contains_ecs(writes_b, c) { return true; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    var j = 0;
    while j < writes_b.length() {
        let c2 = writes_b.get(j);
        if str_vec_contains_ecs(reads_a, c2) || str_vec_contains_ecs(writes_a, c2) { return true; }
        j = j + 1;
    }
    return false;
}

// `systems_conflict_ecs` OR'd with the real, general hazard component/resource analysis alone
// can never see: two `@main_thread` systems (see `Ast.hc`'s own `SystemDecl.has_main_thread`
// header) are forced to conflict with EACH OTHER unconditionally, even sharing zero components
// -- real motivating case: two independent rendering systems, no shared `&mut` component at
// all, still crash if scheduled onto separate real threads, since a GL context only ever works
// on the thread that created it. A `@main_thread` system conflicting with an ORDINARY one is
// fine and NOT forced here (only two `@main_thread` systems specifically are guaranteed unsafe
// together) -- an ordinary system already correctly conflicts or doesn't purely by its own real
// component access.
pub fn systems_conflict_or_main_thread_ecs(reads_a: &Vec<String>, writes_a: &Vec<String>, main_thread_a: Bool, reads_b: &Vec<String>, writes_b: &Vec<String>, main_thread_b: Bool) -> Bool {
    if main_thread_a && main_thread_b { return true; }
    return systems_conflict_ecs(reads_a, writes_a, reads_b, writes_b);
}

// Real, independent `Vec<Bool>` of `n` `false` entries -- `Registry<T>`'s own established "seed
// via `vec_of`, then reset `len` to 0 and push for real" convention doesn't fit here (this needs
// a KNOWN LENGTH up front, indexable by `.set()`, not built by pushing) -- built by pushing `n`
// times instead, the simplest correct way to get there.
pub fn bool_vec_of_len_ecs(n: Int) -> Vec<Bool> {
    var out = empty_bool_vec_chk();
    var i = 0;
    while i < n {
        out.push(false);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// A real, empty `Vec<Int>` -- the grouped topo-sort's own per-round "which original indices did
// I place into this group" accumulator.
pub fn empty_int_vec_ecs() -> Vec<Int> {
    var v = vec_of(0);
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

// Is the system at `idx` ready to be placed next -- its own `@after` target (if any) already
// placed, and no unplaced system's `@before` names it? See `TyChecker.check_systems`'s own header
// for the full topological-sort this drives.
pub fn system_ready_ecs(idx: Int, names: &Vec<String>, after: &Vec<Vec<String>>, before: &Vec<Vec<String>>, placed: &Vec<Bool>) -> Bool {
    let my_name = names.get(idx);
    // Multi-target `@after` -- EVERY named system must already be placed, not just one.
    let my_after = after.get(idx);
    var aj = 0;
    while aj < my_after.length() {
        let want = my_after.get(aj);
        var after_placed = false;
        var ai = 0;
        while ai < names.length() {
            if names.get(ai) == want {
                after_placed = placed.get(ai);
            }
            ai = ai + 1;
        }
        if !after_placed { return false; }
        aj = aj + 1;
    }
    // Multi-target `@before` -- any NOT-YET-PLACED other system whose own `before` list names
    // `my_name` blocks this one from going first.
    var bi = 0;
    while bi < names.length() {
        if !placed.get(bi) && bi != idx {
            let other_before = before.get(bi);
            var bj = 0;
            while bj < other_before.length() {
                if other_before.get(bj) == my_name { return false; }
                bj = bj + 1;
            }
        }
        bi = bi + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// A real, independent copy of a `Registry<Bool>`'s current entries -- needed because `Registry`
// (like every struct in this language) is a reference type: `let snap = some_registry;` only
// copies the REFERENCE, so mutating either one afterward mutates both. `check_stmt`'s own `If` arm
// needs a TRUE snapshot to restore from before checking the `else` branch (see `snapshot_moved_
// state`'s own header) -- `.entries` read directly, same "no `.keys()`/iteration method to reuse"
// reason `sealed_implementers` already reads a `Registry`'s own field directly elsewhere.
pub fn clone_bool_registry_chk(r: Registry<Bool>) -> Registry<Bool> {
    var out = registry_new("__init__", false);
    var i = 0;
    while i < r.entries.length() {
        let e = r.entries.get(i);
        out.register(e.key, e.value);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// Merges every `true` entry from `source` into `target` (in place -- `target` is a reference, see
// `clone_bool_registry_chk`'s own header) -- the "moved in either branch" union `check_stmt`'s own
// `If` arm needs once both branches have been checked independently. Only `true` entries are ever
// copied over: a name un-moved (reassigned) in one branch shouldn't un-move it if the OTHER branch
// left it moved.
pub fn or_true_entries_chk(target: Registry<Bool>, source: Registry<Bool>) {
    var i = 0;
    while i < source.entries.length() {
        let e = source.entries.get(i);
        if e.value {
            target.register(e.key, true);
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// The widening rule behind `check_binary`'s own arithmetic/comparison mixed-numeric-pair support
// -- see that fn's own header for the real gap this closes (bare `Int` game-code values, loop
// counters chief among them, mixed into `Float`/`Double` math). Returns the COMMON type both
// operands should be treated as, or `""` when the pair isn't numeric at all, or is the one
// numeric pair still deliberately NOT widened (`Int`+`Long`). `Codegen.hotc`'s own identical
// `numeric_widen_result_cg` is the duplicated codegen-side twin, same "checker and codegen don't
// share registries" split this compiler always uses.
pub fn numeric_widen_result(ln: String, rn: String) -> String {
    if !is_numeric_ty_name_chk(ln) || !is_numeric_ty_name_chk(rn) { return ""; }
    if ln == rn { return ln; }
    if ln == "Double" || rn == "Double" { return "Double"; }
    if ln == "Float" || rn == "Float" { return "Float"; }
    return "";
}

// The fixed operator-overload naming convention -- see `check_binary`'s own header. `""` for any
// op that isn't overloadable this pass (comparisons -- `<`/`<=`/`>`/`>=` -- deliberately aren't
// covered: full ordering would need a struct to implement all four, or a single `cmp` returning
// something like `Ordering`, either a bigger design than this pass's real motivating cases,
// `Vec3`-style arithmetic and value equality, actually needed). `Codegen.hotc`'s own identical
// `operator_method_name_cg` is the duplicated codegen-side twin.
pub fn operator_method_name(op: String) -> String {
    if op == "+" { return "add"; }
    if op == "-" { return "sub"; }
    if op == "*" { return "mul"; }
    if op == "/" { return "div"; }
    if op == "%" { return "rem"; }
    if op == "==" { return "eq"; }
    if op == "!=" { return "eq"; }
    return "";
}

// Substitutes a generic template's type parameter into one declared field type -- handles the
// plain exact-match case (`ftype == "T"` -> the concrete arg, e.g. `"Int"`), an array-of-the-type-
// parameter (`ftype == "[T]"` -> `"[Int]"`, needed for `Vec<T> { data: [T], len: Int }`'s own
// `data` field), AND, new this phase, a *different* generic instantiation naming the type
// parameter as its own sole type argument (`ftype == "Vec$T"` -> `"Vec$Int"`, needed for
// `Registry<T> { entries: Vec<T> }`'s own `entries` field -- see `ensure_instantiated`'s own
// header for the cascading half of this: substituting the field type alone isn't enough, the
// newly-produced nested name has to actually get monomorphized too). Scope cut, still disclosed:
// no deeper nesting than one level (`[[T]]`, or a field type that names `T` somewhere inside a
// *compound* type argument rather than as the argument in its entirety, e.g. a hypothetical
// `Vec<Box<T>>`, isn't substituted here -- `type_name_ref`'s own single-bare-identifier-argument
// scope cut means a generic type argument that's itself generic can't even be parsed yet, so this
// case can't arise from real source today regardless).
// Real bug found stress-testing this against a closer port of the real prelude's own
// `Registry<T> { entries: Vec<RegistryEntry<T>> }`: the ORIGINAL version of this function only
// substituted ONE level of mono-name nesting (`mono_arg(ftype) == type_param` required the entire
// argument portion to be the bare type parameter) -- `"Vec$RegistryEntry$T"` (three levels: `T`
// is the OUTER instantiation's argument's OWN argument) fell through completely unchanged, since
// `mono_arg("Vec$RegistryEntry$T")` is `"RegistryEntry$T"`, not `"T"`. Fixed by recursing into a
// mono-name's own argument portion instead of demanding an exact match there -- this strictly
// generalizes the old single-level check (recursing on a bare `"T"` just hits the exact-match
// branch immediately and returns `arg`, so every previously-working case behaves identically) while
// correctly handling arbitrarily deep chains: `substitute_field_type("Vec$RegistryEntry$T", "T",
// "Int")` now recurses into `"RegistryEntry$T"` -> `"RegistryEntry$Int"`, then wraps back into
// `"Vec$RegistryEntry$Int"`.
// A real bug found adding two-param generics (`struct Pair<K, V> { ... }` / `fn f<K, V>(...)`,
// see `Ast.hc`'s own `StructDecl.type_param2`/`FnDecl.type_param2` headers): the ORIGINAL version
// of this fn only ever substituted the LAST `"$"`-delimited segment of a mono name, silently
// leaving every earlier one untouched. Invisible for a genuinely single-param template (`"Vec$T"`
// has exactly one segment after the base, so "last" and "only" were the same thing) -- but a flat
// two-param mono name like `"Pair$K$V"` (built when a two-param generic FN's own body constructs
// `Pair<K, V> { ... }`, using its own still-abstract type param NAMES as the literal type
// arguments) has TWO. Substituting `K` first, the old recursive shape (`mono_base(ftype) + "$" +
// substitute_field_type(mono_arg(ftype), ...)`) treated the REMAINING `"K$V"` as itself a nested
// mono arg (`mono_base("K$V")` = `"K"`, `mono_arg("K$V")` = `"V"`) -- i.e. "K applied to V", which
// is wrong; it's really two independent siblings, not one type parameterized by the other. Only
// the LAST segment (`"V"`) was ever actually compared against `type_param`, so substituting `K`
// silently did nothing, and a SECOND sequential call substituting `V` -> `String` produced the
// real, observed bug: `"Pair$K$V"` -> `"Pair$K$String"`, `K` never replaced at all.
//
// Fixed by splitting the WHOLE mono name flat on every `"$"` and checking EACH segment
// independently against `type_param`, instead of recursing into "the rest" as if it were always
// exactly one more nested type application. Correct for the original single-param case too (one
// segment to check, same outcome as before) AND now for a flat multi-param name -- callers doing
// two SEQUENTIAL substitutions (`ensure_fn_instantiated`/`ensure_instantiated`'s own two-param
// branches: substitute `K` -> its concrete type first, leaving `V` untouched since it doesn't
// match; substitute `V` next, leaving the now-already-substituted `K` slot untouched since IT
// doesn't match `V` either) now produce the right final string either order.
pub fn substitute_field_type(ftype: String, type_param: String, arg: String) -> String {
    if is_array_type_name(ftype) {
        let elem = array_elem_type_name(ftype);
        return "[" + substitute_field_type(elem, type_param, arg) + "]";
    }
    if !is_mono_name(ftype) {
        if ftype == type_param { return arg; }
        return ftype;
    }
    var result = "";
    var remaining = ftype;
    var first = true;
    var keep_going = true;
    while keep_going {
        let idx = remaining.indexOf("$");
        var seg = remaining;
        var has_more = false;
        if idx >= 0 {
            seg = remaining.substring(0, idx);
            remaining = remaining.substring(idx + 1, remaining.length());
            has_more = true;
        }
        var out_seg = seg;
        if !first && seg == type_param {
            out_seg = arg;
        }
        if first {
            result = out_seg;
        } else {
            result = result + "$" + out_seg;
        }
        first = false;
        if !has_more { keep_going = false; }
    }
    return result;
}

// A substituted field type's own CASCADE target -- what `ensure_instantiated` should actually try
// to instantiate next. Real bug found alongside the nested-mono-name one above: `is_mono_name`
// just checks for `"$"` ANYWHERE in the string, so `"[RegistryEntry$Int]"` (an array-of-mono-name
// field, e.g. `Vec<T> { data: [T] }`'s own `data` after `T` substitutes to a mono name) reads as a
// mono name ITSELF -- feeding the whole bracketed string into `ensure_instantiated` would try to
// `mono_base`/`mono_arg` split `"[RegistryEntry$Int]"` (garbage: `"[RegistryEntry"`/`"Int]"`),
// silently no-op (neither `generic_templates` nor `generic_enum_templates` has an entry under that
// garbage base), and never actually cascade into `"RegistryEntry$Int"` at all. Unwrapping one
// array level first (the same "single array level" scope cut already disclosed elsewhere) fixes
// it: an array field's cascade target is its ELEMENT type, not the bracketed string itself.
pub fn cascade_target(ftype: String) -> String {
    if is_array_type_name(ftype) {
        return array_elem_type_name(ftype);
    }
    return ftype;
}

pub fn empty_expr_vec3() -> Vec<Expr> {
    var v = vec_of(IntLit { value: 0 });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

pub fn empty_matcharm_vec3() -> Vec<MatchArm> {
    var v = vec_of(MatchArm { variant_name: "_", field_names: empty_str_vec2(), bind_names: empty_str_vec2(), body: empty_stmt_vec3(), is_literal: false, literal: IntLit { value: 0 }, is_positional: false });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

// A real, whole-tree copy of `e`, substituting `type_param` for `arg` everywhere a TYPE NAME
// STRING appears inside it -- the only two spots that happens in this AST are `StructLit`'s own
// `name`/`generic_base` (`substitute_field_type` handles the same three shapes `ensure_fn_
// instantiated`'s param/return substitution already does: bare `"T"`, `"[T]"`, or a *different*
// generic instantiation naming `T` as its own sole argument, `"Vec$T"`) and `StaticCall`'s
// `type_name` (a generic struct's own static method, `T::wrap(x)`-shaped, though this can't arise
// from real source yet since only enum/struct construction sites currently support the qualified
// `Base<Arg>` form -- included for completeness/robustness regardless). This is the piece that
// closes the gap disclosed in `ensure_fn_instantiated`'s own header: a generic fn's body CAN now
// reference its own still-abstract `T` inside a nested generic construction site (`vec_of<T>`'s
// own `return Vec<T> { data: [first; 4], len: 1 };`, say) -- every other `Expr`/`Stmt` variant
// here just recurses into its own sub-expressions unchanged, no type name of its own to fix up.
pub fn subst_expr(e: &Expr, type_param: String, arg: String) -> Expr {
    match e {
        IntLit { value } => { return IntLit { value: value }; }
        FloatLit { text } => { return FloatLit { text: text }; }
        DoubleLit { text } => { return DoubleLit { text: text }; }
        LongLit { text } => { return LongLit { text: text }; }
        CharLit { value } => { return CharLit { value: value }; }
        StringLit { value } => { return StringLit { value: value }; }
        StringInterp { literals, exprs } => { return StringInterp { literals: literals, exprs: subst_expr_list(exprs, type_param, arg) }; }
        BoolLit { value } => { return BoolLit { value: value }; }
        Ident { name } => { return Ident { name: name }; }
        Binary { op, left, right } => { return Binary { op: op, left: subst_expr(left, type_param, arg), right: subst_expr(right, type_param, arg) }; }
        Unary { op, expr } => { return Unary { op: op, expr: subst_expr(expr, type_param, arg) }; }
        Call { callee, args } => { return Call { callee: callee, args: subst_expr_list(args, type_param, arg) }; }
        FieldAccess { obj, field } => { return FieldAccess { obj: subst_expr(obj, type_param, arg), field: field }; }
        MethodCall { recv, method, args } => { return MethodCall { recv: subst_expr(recv, type_param, arg), method: method, args: subst_expr_list(args, type_param, arg) }; }
        Assign { name, value } => { return Assign { name: name, value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg) }; }
        FieldAssign { obj, field, value } => { return FieldAssign { obj: subst_expr(obj, type_param, arg), field: field, value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg) }; }
        StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => {
            let new_name = substitute_field_type(name, type_param, arg);
            var new_gb = none_string3();
            if let Some { value: gb } = generic_base {
                new_gb = some_string3(substitute_field_type(gb, type_param, arg));
            }
            return StructLit { name: new_name, field_names: field_names, field_values: subst_expr_list(field_values, type_param, arg), generic_base: new_gb };
        }
        ArrayLit { elements } => { return ArrayLit { elements: subst_expr_list(elements, type_param, arg) }; }
        ArrayRepeat { value, count } => { return ArrayRepeat { value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg), count: subst_expr(count, type_param, arg) }; }
        ArenaNew { struct_name, count } => { return ArenaNew { struct_name: struct_name, count: subst_expr(count, type_param, arg) }; }
        Index { arr, index } => { return Index { arr: subst_expr(arr, type_param, arg), index: subst_expr(index, type_param, arg) }; }
        IndexAssign { arr, index, value } => { return IndexAssign { arr: subst_expr(arr, type_param, arg), index: subst_expr(index, type_param, arg), value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg) }; }
        Slice { arr, start, end, inclusive } => { return Slice { arr: subst_expr(arr, type_param, arg), start: subst_expr(start, type_param, arg), end: subst_expr(end, type_param, arg), inclusive: inclusive }; }
        IndexFieldGet { arr_name, index, field } => { return IndexFieldGet { arr_name: arr_name, index: subst_expr(index, type_param, arg), field: field }; }
        IndexFieldSet { arr_name, index, field, value } => { return IndexFieldSet { arr_name: arr_name, index: subst_expr(index, type_param, arg), field: field, value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg) }; }
        StaticCall { type_name, method, args } => { return StaticCall { type_name: substitute_field_type(type_name, type_param, arg), method: method, args: subst_expr_list(args, type_param, arg) }; }
        ExternStaticFieldGet { type_name, field } => { return ExternStaticFieldGet { type_name: type_name, field: field }; }
        ExternStaticFieldSet { type_name, field, value } => { return ExternStaticFieldSet { type_name: type_name, field: field, value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg) }; }
        Cast { expr, target_type } => { return Cast { expr: subst_expr(expr, type_param, arg), target_type: target_type }; }
        IsCheck { expr, target_type } => { return IsCheck { expr: subst_expr(expr, type_param, arg), target_type: target_type }; }
        Lambda { params, body } => { return Lambda { params: params, body: subst_expr(body, type_param, arg) }; }
        TryOp { expr } => { return TryOp { expr: subst_expr(expr, type_param, arg) }; }
        NamedArg { name, value } => { return NamedArg { name: name, value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg) }; }
        NullLit => { return NullLit; }
        SafeCall { recv, method, args } => { return SafeCall { recv: subst_expr(recv, type_param, arg), method: method, args: subst_expr_list(args, type_param, arg) }; }
        SafeFieldAccess { recv, field } => { return SafeFieldAccess { recv: subst_expr(recv, type_param, arg), field: field }; }
        Elvis { left, right } => { return Elvis { left: subst_expr(left, type_param, arg), right: subst_expr(right, type_param, arg) }; }
        Comprehension { result_expr, var_name, iter_expr, cond } => {
            var new_cond = none_expr3();
            if let Some { value: c } = cond { new_cond = some_expr3(subst_expr(c, type_param, arg)); }
            return Comprehension { result_expr: subst_expr(result_expr, type_param, arg), var_name: var_name, iter_expr: subst_expr(iter_expr, type_param, arg), cond: new_cond };
        }
    }
}

pub fn subst_expr_list(es: Vec<Expr>, type_param: String, arg: String) -> Vec<Expr> {
    var out = empty_expr_vec3();
    var i = 0;
    while i < es.length() {
        out.push(subst_expr(es.get(i), type_param, arg));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

pub fn subst_stmt(s: Stmt, type_param: String, arg: String) -> Stmt {
    match s {
        Let { name, mutable, init, declared_type, line } => { return Let { name: name, mutable: mutable, init: subst_expr(init, type_param, arg), declared_type: declared_type, line: line }; }
        ExprStmt { expr, line } => { return ExprStmt { expr: subst_expr(expr, type_param, arg), line: line }; }
        Return { value, line } => {
            var new_val = none_expr3();
            if let Some { value: v } = value { new_val = some_expr3(subst_expr(v, type_param, arg)); }
            return Return { value: new_val, line: line };
        }
        If { cond, then_body, else_body, line, narrow_name, narrow_is_eq } => {
            var new_else = none_stmt_list3();
            if let Some { value: eb } = else_body { new_else = some_stmt_list3(subst_stmt_list(eb, type_param, arg)); }
            return If { cond: subst_expr(cond, type_param, arg), then_body: subst_stmt_list(then_body, type_param, arg), else_body: new_else, line: line, narrow_name: narrow_name, narrow_is_eq: narrow_is_eq };
        }
        While { cond, body, line } => { return While { cond: subst_expr(cond, type_param, arg), body: subst_stmt_list(body, type_param, arg), line: line }; }
        For { var_name, is_range, range_start, range_end, inclusive, arr_expr, body, line } => {
            return For { var_name: var_name, is_range: is_range, range_start: subst_expr(range_start, type_param, arg), range_end: subst_expr(range_end, type_param, arg), inclusive: inclusive, arr_expr: subst_expr(arr_expr, type_param, arg), body: subst_stmt_list(body, type_param, arg), line: line };
        }
        Match { subject, arms, line } => { return Match { subject: subst_expr(subject, type_param, arg), arms: subst_arm_list(arms, type_param, arg), line: line }; }
        Throw { value, line } => { return Throw { value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg), line: line }; }
        Break { line } => { return Break { line: line }; }
        Continue { line } => { return Continue { line: line }; }
        Try { try_block, catches, line } => {
            return Try { try_block: subst_stmt_list(try_block, type_param, arg), catches: subst_catches(catches, type_param, arg), line: line };
        }
    }
}

pub fn subst_stmt_list(ss: Vec<Stmt>, type_param: String, arg: String) -> Vec<Stmt> {
    var out = empty_stmt_vec3();
    var i = 0;
    while i < ss.length() {
        out.push(subst_stmt(ss.get(i), type_param, arg));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

pub fn subst_catches(cs: Vec<CatchClause>, type_param: String, arg: String) -> Vec<CatchClause> {
    var out = empty_catchclause_vec3();
    var i = 0;
    while i < cs.length() {
        let c = cs.get(i);
        out.push(CatchClause { catch_var: c.catch_var, catch_type: c.catch_type, catch_block: subst_stmt_list(c.catch_block, type_param, arg) });
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

pub fn empty_catchclause_vec3() -> Vec<CatchClause> {
    var v = vec_of(CatchClause { catch_var: "_", catch_type: "_", catch_block: empty_stmt_vec3() });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

pub fn subst_arm_list(arms: Vec<MatchArm>, type_param: String, arg: String) -> Vec<MatchArm> {
    var out = empty_matcharm_vec3();
    var i = 0;
    while i < arms.length() {
        let a = arms.get(i);
        out.push(MatchArm { variant_name: a.variant_name, field_names: a.field_names, bind_names: a.bind_names, body: subst_stmt_list(a.body, type_param, arg), is_literal: a.is_literal, literal: subst_expr(a.literal, type_param, arg), is_positional: a.is_positional });
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

pub fn none_expr3() -> Option<Expr> {
    return None;
}

pub fn some_expr3(e: Expr) -> Option<Expr> {
    return Some { value: e };
}

pub fn none_stmt_list3() -> Option<Vec<Stmt>> {
    return None;
}

pub fn some_stmt_list3(ss: Vec<Stmt>) -> Option<Vec<Stmt>> {
    return Some { value: ss };
}

impl TyChecker {
    fn error(&mut self, msg: String) {
        self.errors.push(msg);
    }

    // === Expressions ===

    fn check_expr(&mut self, e: &Expr) -> Ty {
        match e {
            IntLit { value } => { return TyInt; }
            FloatLit { text } => { return TyFloat; }
            DoubleLit { text } => { return TyDouble; }
            LongLit { text } => { return TyLong; }
            CharLit { value } => { return TyChar; }
            StringLit { value } => { return TyStr; }
            StringInterp { literals, exprs } => { return self.check_string_interp(exprs); }
            BoolLit { value } => { return TyBool; }
            Ident { name } => { return self.check_ident(name); }
            Binary { op, left, right } => { return self.check_binary(op, left, right); }
            Unary { op, expr } => { return self.check_unary(op, expr); }
            Call { callee, args } => { return self.check_call(callee, args); }
            FieldAccess { obj, field } => { return self.check_field_access(obj, field); }
            MethodCall { recv, method, args } => { return self.check_method_call(recv, method, args); }
            Assign { name, value } => { return self.check_assign(name, value); }
            FieldAssign { obj, field, value } => { return self.check_field_assign(obj, field, value); }
            // `self.cur_ret_ty` is passed unconditionally as `expected_mono` -- `check_struct_lit`
            // only ever CONSULTS it as a fallback when the bare struct name doesn't resolve on its
            // own (a real, non-generic struct, or an already-mono generic name like "Vec$T"), so
            // passing it here even for expressions that aren't actually in return position is
            // harmless: it's simply ignored whenever it isn't needed. See that fn's own header --
            // real gap found self-compiling `Parser.hotc`, whose own helper fns all write `return
            // Vec { ... };` inside a `-> Vec<X>` fn.
            StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => { return self.check_struct_lit(name, field_names, field_values, generic_base, some_string3(self.cur_ret_ty)); }
            ArrayLit { elements } => { return self.check_array_lit(elements); }
            ArrayRepeat { value, count } => { return self.check_array_repeat(value, count); }
            ArenaNew { struct_name, count } => { return self.check_arena_new(struct_name, count); }
            Index { arr, index } => { return self.check_index(arr, index); }
            IndexAssign { arr, index, value } => { return self.check_index_assign(arr, index, value); }
            Slice { arr, start, end, inclusive } => { return self.check_slice(arr, start, end); }
            IndexFieldGet { arr_name, index, field } => { return self.check_index_field_get(arr_name, index, field); }
            IndexFieldSet { arr_name, index, field, value } => { return self.check_index_field_set(arr_name, index, field, value); }
            StaticCall { type_name, method, args } => { return self.check_static_call(type_name, method, args); }
            ExternStaticFieldGet { type_name, field } => { return self.check_extern_static_field_get(type_name, field); }
            ExternStaticFieldSet { type_name, field, value } => { return self.check_extern_static_field_set(type_name, field, value); }
            Cast { expr, target_type } => { return self.check_cast(expr, target_type); }
            IsCheck { expr, target_type } => { return self.check_is(expr, target_type); }
            // Reaching here means a lambda literal showed up somewhere OTHER than directly as a
            // call argument this file's own call-checking (`check_call`/`check_method_call`/
            // `check_static_call`) already special-cases -- `let f = || 5;`, a lambda passed to a
            // NON-extern-interface param, nested inside another expression, etc. A real, disclosed
            // scope cut (see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.Lambda` header): there's no general "lambda type"
            // to check it against outside that one context, so this is always a real error, not a
            // crash.
            Lambda { params, body } => {
                self.error("lambda literal is only allowed directly as a call argument whose target is a single-method 'extern interface'");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            TryOp { expr } => { return self.check_try_op(expr); }
            // Reaching here means a `name: value` argument was used somewhere OTHER than a
            // supported call shape's own `resolve_named_args_chk` already handled (mixed with a
            // positional arg, or a call target that isn't a plain top-level fn) -- see `Ast.hc`'s
            // own `Expr.NamedArg` header for the real, disclosed scope this pass covers.
            NamedArg { name, value } => {
                self.error("named argument '" + name + "' isn't supported here (only plain top-level fn calls accept named arguments this pass)");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            // See `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.NullLit` header -- `TyUnknown`, the same "compatible with
            // anything, no static check to run" escape hatch every other genuinely-untyped shape
            // already gets, rather than a dedicated "null type" this checker has no other use for.
            NullLit => { return TyUnknown; }
            SafeCall { recv, method, args } => { return self.check_safe_call(recv, method, args); }
            SafeFieldAccess { recv, field } => { return self.check_safe_field_access(recv, field); }
            Elvis { left, right } => { return self.check_elvis(left, right); }
            // Reached only OUTSIDE the special-cased `let x: Vec<T> = [...]` position (`check_stmt`'s
            // own `Let` handling intercepts a `Comprehension` init BEFORE it ever reaches this
            // generic dispatch, calling `check_comprehension` directly with a real expected type) --
            // see `Ast.hc`'s own `Comprehension` header for why a comprehension genuinely can't be
            // checked/compiled without one.
            Comprehension { result_expr, var_name, iter_expr, cond } => {
                self.error("list comprehensions need a declared result type this pass -- use 'let x: Vec<T> = [...]' instead of a bare expression");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
        }
    }

    // `expr as Type` -- `target_type` is trusted at face value, same "declared type is truth"
    // honesty every other declared type already gets in this checker (see `Ast.hc`'s own `Cast`
    // header for why cast-legality checking is out of scope this round). `expr` is still checked
    // for real (its own errors, e.g. an unknown variable inside it, still surface).
    fn check_cast(&mut self, expr: &Expr, target_type: String) -> Ty {
        self.check_expr(expr);
        return ty_from_name(target_type);
    }

    // `expr is Type` -- `check_cast`'s boolean-returning sibling, same "declared type is truth"
    // honesty (see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.IsCheck` header).
    fn check_is(&mut self, expr: &Expr, target_type: String) -> Ty {
        self.check_expr(expr);
        return TyBool;
    }

    fn check_static_call(&mut self, type_name: String, method: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        var arg_tys = self.check_args_lambda_aware(args);
        let key = type_name + "#" + method;
        let cls_found = self.externs.get(type_name);
        var cls_known = false;
        if let Some { value: b } = cls_found { cls_known = true; }
        if cls_known {
            // Try the precise, full-signature key first (arg types as actually inferred at this
            // call site), falling back to the arity-only key -- see `register_extern`'s own
            // header. Only the fallback exists for a call whose overload set has just one member
            // at this arity; the precise key exists too when it doesn't.
            var arg_type_names = empty_str_vec2();
            var ati = 0;
            while ati < arg_tys.length() {
                arg_type_names.push(ty_name(arg_tys.get(ati)));
                ati = ati + 1;
            }
            let sig_key = type_name + "#" + method + "#" + JString::valueOf(args.length()) + "#" + join_type_names_chk(arg_type_names);
            let extern_key = type_name + "#" + method + "#" + JString::valueOf(args.length());
            var m_found = self.extern_methods.get(sig_key);
            var m_known = false;
            if let Some { value: mv1 } = m_found { m_known = true; }
            if !m_known {
                m_found = self.extern_methods.get(extern_key);
            }
            if let Some { value: m } = m_found {
                let expected_count = m.param_types.length();
                let got_count = arg_tys.length();
                if expected_count != got_count {
                    self.error("'" + method + "' expects " + JString::valueOf(expected_count) + " args, got " + JString::valueOf(got_count));
                } else {
                    self.backfill_lambda_args(&args, &m.param_types, &mut arg_tys);
                    var i = 0;
                    while i < got_count {
                        let expected = m.param_types.get(i);
                        let got = arg_tys.get(i);
                        let gn = ty_name(got);
                        if expected != "?" && gn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(expected, gn) {
                            self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + method + "' expects " + expected + ", got " + gn);
                        }
                        i = i + 1;
                    }
                }
                if m.is_ctor { return ty_from_name(type_name); }
                if m.ret_type == "Self" { return ty_from_name(type_name); }
                return ty_from_name(m.ret_type);
            } else {
                self.error("unknown method '" + method + "' on extern class '" + type_name + "'");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
        }
        // Not a known extern class -- an `extends`-struct's own `::new(...)` is the other real
        // constructor shape (see `Ast.hc`'s own `StructDecl.extends` header): resolved against the
        // extended ALIAS's own ctor table (same lookup `cls_known` above just did), but returning
        // the STRUCT's own name, not the alias's -- `Codegen.hotc`'s own `gen_struct` compiles one
        // real `<init>` per alias ctor directly onto the struct's own class, so the struct's name is
        // the real, callable target here, not just a type-checking fiction.
        let sup_found = self.struct_extends.get(type_name);
        var sup_known = false;
        var sup_name = "";
        if let Some { value: sup } = sup_found { sup_known = true; sup_name = sup; }
        if sup_known {
            if method != "new" {
                self.error("'" + type_name + "' extends '" + sup_name + "' -- only '::new' is callable this way, not '" + method + "'");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            var sup_arg_type_names = empty_str_vec2();
            var sati = 0;
            while sati < arg_tys.length() {
                sup_arg_type_names.push(ty_name(arg_tys.get(sati)));
                sati = sati + 1;
            }
            let sup_sig_key = sup_name + "#new#" + JString::valueOf(args.length()) + "#" + join_type_names_chk(sup_arg_type_names);
            let sup_key = sup_name + "#new#" + JString::valueOf(args.length());
            var sup_m_found = self.extern_methods.get(sup_sig_key);
            var sup_m_known = false;
            if let Some { value: smv1 } = sup_m_found { sup_m_known = true; }
            if !sup_m_known {
                sup_m_found = self.extern_methods.get(sup_key);
            }
            if let Some { value: sm2 } = sup_m_found {
                let sexpected_count = sm2.param_types.length();
                let sgot_count = arg_tys.length();
                if sexpected_count != sgot_count {
                    self.error("'" + type_name + "::new' expects " + JString::valueOf(sexpected_count) + " args, got " + JString::valueOf(sgot_count));
                } else {
                    self.backfill_lambda_args(&args, &sm2.param_types, &mut arg_tys);
                    var si2 = 0;
                    while si2 < sgot_count {
                        let sexpected = sm2.param_types.get(si2);
                        let sgot = arg_tys.get(si2);
                        let sgn = ty_name(sgot);
                        if sexpected != "?" && sgn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(sexpected, sgn) {
                            self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(si2 + 1) + " to '" + type_name + "::new' expects " + sexpected + ", got " + sgn);
                        }
                        si2 = si2 + 1;
                    }
                }
                return ty_from_name(type_name);
            }
            self.error("no matching '" + sup_name + "' constructor for '" + type_name + "::new' with " + JString::valueOf(args.length()) + " arg(s)");
            return ty_from_name(type_name);
        }
        // Not a known extern class or extends-struct -- fall back to a struct static method.
        let sm_found = self.static_methods.get(key);
        if let Some { value: sig } = sm_found {
            let expected_count = sig.param_types.length();
            let got_count = arg_tys.length();
            if expected_count != got_count {
                self.error("'" + method + "' expects " + JString::valueOf(expected_count) + " args, got " + JString::valueOf(got_count));
            } else {
                // Move-check: same "is_ref param never moves its argument" rule `check_call` uses
                // -- see `TyChecker.static_method_param_is_ref`'s own header.
                let is_ref_found3 = self.static_method_param_is_ref.get(key);
                var is_ref_list3 = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                if let Some { value: irl3 } = is_ref_found3 { is_ref_list3 = irl3; }
                let is_mutref_found3 = self.static_method_param_is_mut_ref.get(key);
                var is_mutref_list3 = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                if let Some { value: imrl3 } = is_mutref_found3 { is_mutref_list3 = imrl3; }
                var borrow_names3 = empty_str_vec2();
                var borrow_is_mut3 = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                var borrow_present3 = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                var i = 0;
                while i < got_count {
                    let expected = sig.param_types.get(i);
                    let got = arg_tys.get(i);
                    let en = ty_name(expected);
                    let gn = ty_name(got);
                    if en != "?" && gn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(en, gn) {
                        self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + method + "' expects " + en + ", got " + gn);
                    }
                    var arg_is_ref3 = true;
                    if i < is_ref_list3.length() { arg_is_ref3 = is_ref_list3.get(i); }
                    if !arg_is_ref3 {
                        let ai4 = args.get(i);
                        self.move_check_consumed(&ai4, gn);
                    }
                    var arg_is_mut_ref3 = false;
                    if i < is_mutref_list3.length() { arg_is_mut_ref3 = is_mutref_list3.get(i); }
                    let ai5 = args.get(i);
                    match ai5 {
                        Ident { name } => {
                            borrow_names3.push(name);
                            borrow_is_mut3.push(arg_is_mut_ref3);
                            borrow_present3.push(true);
                        }
                        _ => {
                            borrow_names3.push("");
                            borrow_is_mut3.push(false);
                            borrow_present3.push(false);
                        }
                    }
                    i = i + 1;
                }
                self.check_borrow_rules(borrow_names3, borrow_is_mut3, borrow_present3, "call to '" + method + "'");
            }
            return sig.ret_type;
        }
        self.error("unknown extern class or struct '" + type_name + "' (no static method '" + method + "')");
        return TyUnknown;
    }

    fn check_extern_static_field_get(&mut self, type_name: String, field: String) -> Ty {
        let cls_found = self.externs.get(type_name);
        var cls_known = false;
        if let Some { value: b } = cls_found { cls_known = true; }
        if !cls_known {
            self.error("unknown extern class '" + type_name + "'");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        let f_found = self.extern_fields.get(type_name + "#" + field);
        if let Some { value: f } = f_found {
            if !f.is_static {
                self.error("field '" + field + "' on extern class '" + type_name + "' is not static");
            }
            return ty_from_name(f.type_name);
        }
        self.error("unknown static field '" + field + "' on extern class '" + type_name + "'");
        return TyUnknown;
    }

    fn check_extern_static_field_set(&mut self, type_name: String, field: String, value: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let vt = self.check_expr(value);
        let vn = ty_name(vt);
        let cls_found = self.externs.get(type_name);
        var cls_known = false;
        if let Some { value: b } = cls_found { cls_known = true; }
        if !cls_known {
            self.error("unknown extern class '" + type_name + "'");
            return ty_from_name(vn);
        }
        let f_found = self.extern_fields.get(type_name + "#" + field);
        if let Some { value: f } = f_found {
            if !f.is_static {
                self.error("field '" + field + "' on extern class '" + type_name + "' is not static");
            } else if vn != "?" && f.type_name != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(f.type_name, vn) {
                self.error("cannot assign " + vn + " to field '" + field + "' of type " + f.type_name);
            }
        } else {
            self.error("unknown static field '" + field + "' on extern class '" + type_name + "'");
        }
        return ty_from_name(vn);
    }

    fn check_array_lit(&mut self, elements: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        if elements.length() == 0 {
            self.error("empty array literal not supported (element type can't be inferred)");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        let first_ty = self.check_expr(elements.get(0));
        let first_name = ty_name(first_ty);
        var i = 1;
        while i < elements.length() {
            let t = self.check_expr(elements.get(i));
            let n = ty_name(t);
            if n != "?" && first_name != "?" && n != first_name {
                self.error("array elements must all have the same type, expected " + first_name + ", got " + n);
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return TyStruct { name: "[" + first_name + "]" };
    }

    // `[value; count]` -- `count` must be `Int`, and the element type is just `value`'s own type
    // (no same-type-as-siblings check needed, unlike `check_array_lit` -- there's only ever one
    // value expression here, re-evaluated at runtime, not a list of distinct ones to compare).
    fn check_array_repeat(&mut self, value: &Expr, count: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let vt = self.check_expr(value);
        let vn = ty_name(vt);
        let ct = self.check_expr(count);
        let cn = ty_name(ct);
        if cn != "Int" && cn != "?" {
            self.error("array repeat count must be Int, got " + cn);
        }
        return TyStruct { name: "[" + vn + "]" };
    }

    // `let result: Vec<Int> = [x * x for x in numbers if x > 0];` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own
    // `Comprehension` header for why `expected_type` (the surrounding `let`'s own declared type,
    // ALWAYS required -- `check_stmt`'s own `Let` handling is the only caller, and refuses to call
    // this at all without one) exists at all: there's no way to check/compile `result_expr` without
    // already knowing what it's expected to produce. Mirrors `check_for`'s own shape for the
    // iteration half (`push_scope`/`pop_scope` around `var_name`'s own per-iteration binding).
    fn check_comprehension(&mut self, result_expr: Expr, var_name: String, iter_expr: Expr, cond: Option<Expr>, expected_type: String) -> Ty {
        self.push_scope();
        let it = self.check_expr(&iter_expr);
        let in_ = ty_name(it);
        var elem_ty_name = "?";
        if in_ != "?" {
            if is_array_type_name(in_) {
                elem_ty_name = array_elem_type_name(in_);
            } else if is_mono_name(in_) && mono_base(in_) == "Vec" {
                elem_ty_name = mono_arg(in_);
            } else {
                self.error("comprehension source 'for " + var_name + " in ...' needs an array or Vec<T>, got " + in_);
            }
        }
        self.vars.register(var_name, ty_from_name(elem_ty_name));
        match cond {
            Some { value: c } => {
                let ct = self.check_expr(&c);
                let cn = ty_name(ct);
                if cn != "Bool" && cn != "?" {
                    self.error("comprehension filter ('if ...') must be Bool, got " + cn);
                }
            }
            None => {}
        }
        let rt = self.check_expr(&result_expr);
        let rn = ty_name(rt);
        self.pop_scope();
        var valid_expected = false;
        if is_mono_name(expected_type) {
            if mono_base(expected_type) == "Vec" { valid_expected = true; }
        }
        if !valid_expected {
            self.error("comprehension result type must be declared as Vec<T>, got " + expected_type);
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        let expected_elem = mono_arg(expected_type);
        if rn != "?" && expected_elem != "?" && rn != expected_elem {
            self.error("comprehension result expects " + expected_elem + ", got " + rn);
        }
        self.ensure_instantiated(expected_type);
        // `gen_comprehension`'s own synthesized bytecode calls `.push(...)` on the result `Vec`
        // directly -- HAND-EMITTED, not derived from a real, checked `MethodCall` AST node the
        // way an ordinary `x.push(y);` in real source would be -- so the ordinary path that
        // would otherwise trigger `ensure_method_instantiated` (`check_method_call`) never runs
        // for it. Real gap found testing a comprehension whose result element type had never
        // been `.push()`-ed anywhere else in the SAME program via ordinary checked code (every
        // earlier comprehension example happened to also use `Vec<Int>`/`Vec<String>` elsewhere,
        // which silently already had `push` registered) -- `Vec$Tuple2$Int$Int` genuinely never
        // got its own `push` method emitted at all, a real `NoSuchMethodError` at RUNTIME even
        // though checking/codegen both reported success. Explicit here, mirroring `ensure_
        // instantiated` right above it for the struct itself.
        self.ensure_method_instantiated(expected_type, "push");
        return TyStruct { name: expected_type };
    }

    fn check_index(&mut self, arr: &Expr, index: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let at = self.check_expr(arr);
        let an = ty_name(at);
        let it = self.check_expr(index);
        let in_ = ty_name(it);
        if in_ != "Int" && in_ != "?" {
            self.error("array index must be Int, got " + in_);
        }
        if an == "?" { return TyUnknown; }
        // A raw off-heap arena buffer can't be indexed alone -- there's no "the struct at index i"
        // JVM value to produce (it isn't a real object reference at all, just a byte range inside
        // one big `MemorySegment`); only `buf[i].field` (a combined index-then-field-read) makes
        // sense, handled entirely separately by `check_field_access`'s own arena branch below. This
        // mirrors the real compiler's own identical restriction and error message.
        if is_arena_type_name_chk(an) {
            self.error("cannot use a raw off-heap value; access a field instead, e.g. buf[i].field");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        if !is_array_type_name(an) {
            self.error("type '" + an + "' is not an array, can't index it");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        return ty_from_name(array_elem_type_name(an));
    }

    // `arr[start..end]` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.Slice` header. Unlike `check_index`, which
    // returns the ELEMENT type, this returns the SAME array type `arr` itself has: slicing yields
    // a new array, not a single element.
    fn check_slice(&mut self, arr: &Expr, start: &Expr, end: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let at = self.check_expr(arr);
        let an = ty_name(at);
        let st = self.check_expr(start);
        let sn = ty_name(st);
        if sn != "Int" && sn != "?" {
            self.error("slice start must be Int, got " + sn);
        }
        let et = self.check_expr(end);
        let en = ty_name(et);
        if en != "Int" && en != "?" {
            self.error("slice end must be Int, got " + en);
        }
        if an == "?" { return TyUnknown; }
        if is_arena_type_name_chk(an) {
            self.error("cannot slice a raw off-heap value");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        if !is_array_type_name(an) {
            self.error("type '" + an + "' is not an array, can't slice it");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        return ty_from_name(an);
    }

    // `for var_name in a..b { }` / `for var_name in arr_expr { }` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Stmt::For`
    // header for why both shapes share one AST variant. `var_name` is registered with the right
    // per-iteration type (`Int` for a range, the array's own element type otherwise) exactly like a
    // `let` binding elsewhere, but inside a scope this fn pushes itself (see `push_scope`'s own
    // header) -- so unlike before this checker had real lexical scoping, `var_name` no longer stays
    // visible after the loop ends. `loop_depth` bumped around the body exactly like `While`'s own
    // handling, right above -- `break`/`continue` are legal inside a `for` body for the same reason
    // they're legal inside a `while` one.
    fn check_for(&mut self, var_name: String, is_range: Bool, range_start: Expr, range_end: Expr, arr_expr: Expr, body: Vec<Stmt>) {
        self.push_scope();
        if is_range {
            let st = self.check_expr(range_start);
            let sn = ty_name(st);
            if sn != "Int" && sn != "?" {
                self.error("for-range start must be Int, got " + sn);
            }
            let et = self.check_expr(range_end);
            let en = ty_name(et);
            if en != "Int" && en != "?" {
                self.error("for-range end must be Int, got " + en);
            }
            self.vars.register(var_name, TyInt);
        } else {
            let at = self.check_expr(arr_expr);
            let an = ty_name(at);
            if an == "?" {
                self.vars.register(var_name, TyUnknown);
            } else if !is_array_type_name(an) {
                self.error("'for x in ...' needs a range (a..b) or an array, got " + an);
                self.vars.register(var_name, TyUnknown);
            } else {
                self.vars.register(var_name, ty_from_name(array_elem_type_name(an)));
            }
        }
        self.loop_depth = self.loop_depth + 1;
        self.check_block(body);
        self.loop_depth = self.loop_depth - 1;
        self.pop_scope();
    }

    // `arena Name[count]` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `ArenaNew` header.
    fn check_arena_new(&mut self, struct_name: String, count: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let ct = self.check_expr(count);
        let cn = ty_name(ct);
        if cn != "Int" && cn != "?" {
            self.error("arena buffer count must be Int, got " + cn);
        }
        let known = self.arena_structs.get(struct_name);
        var is_known = false;
        if let Some { value: v } = known { is_known = true; }
        if !is_known {
            self.error("unknown arena struct '" + struct_name + "'");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        return ty_from_name("arena " + struct_name);
    }

    // `"a{x}b"` -- every interpolated expression must be one of the types `Codegen.hotc`'s own
    // `gen_string_interp` actually has a real `StringBuilder.append` overload for, matching the
    // real compiler's own `STRINGIFIABLE` set exactly (now that `Long`/`Float`/`Double` are real
    // types in this subset too, not just `Int`/`Bool`/`String`).
    fn check_string_interp(&mut self, exprs: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        var i = 0;
        while i < exprs.length() {
            let t = self.check_expr(exprs.get(i));
            let n = ty_name(t);
            // A still-nullable `String?` interpolated directly (`"{s}"`) gets its own, clearer
            // message rather than falling into the generic "cannot interpolate" one below -- same
            // "possibly-null value used without a null check" enforcement `check_binary`'s own `+`
            // gate just above gives, applied here too.
            if is_nullable_type_name(n) && strip_nullable(n) == "String" {
                self.error("interpolated value is a possibly-null String (" + n + ") used without a null check");
            } else if n != "Int" && n != "Long" && n != "Float" && n != "Double" && n != "Char" && n != "Byte" && n != "Bool" && n != "String" && n != "?" {
                self.error("cannot interpolate " + n + " into a string -- only Int/Long/Float/Double/Char/Byte/Bool/String are supported");
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return TyStr;
    }

    fn check_index_assign(&mut self, arr: &Expr, index: &Expr, value: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let at = self.check_expr(arr);
        let an = ty_name(at);
        let it = self.check_expr(index);
        let in_ = ty_name(it);
        if in_ != "Int" && in_ != "?" {
            self.error("array index must be Int, got " + in_);
        }
        let vt = self.check_expr(value);
        let vn = ty_name(vt);
        if an == "?" { return ty_from_name(vn); }
        if !is_array_type_name(an) {
            self.error("type '" + an + "' is not an array, can't index it");
            return ty_from_name(vn);
        }
        let elem = array_elem_type_name(an);
        if vn != "?" && elem != vn {
            self.error("cannot assign " + vn + " to array element of type " + elem);
        }
        return ty_from_name(vn);
    }

    fn check_field_access(&mut self, obj: &Expr, field: String) -> Ty {
        let ot = self.check_expr(obj);
        let on = ty_name(ot);
        return self.check_field_access_on_type(on, field);
    }

    fn check_field_access_on_type(&mut self, on0: String, field: String) -> Ty {
        var on = on0;
        if on == "?" { return TyUnknown; }
        // A still-nullable receiver (`sm.toString()`-shaped, `sm: SecurityManager?`, no `== null`/
        // `!= null` guard narrowed it first) -- see README's own "Nullable types" section and
        // `check_stmt`'s own `If` header for the two narrowing idioms this checker recognizes.
        // Reported once, then treated as its underlying (non-null) type for the rest of this call
        // so a genuinely unrelated field-name typo doesn't ALSO get reported as a second, more
        // confusing error on top of this one.
        if is_nullable_type_name(on) {
            self.error("possibly-null value of type '" + on + "' used without a null check");
            on = strip_nullable(on);
        }
        return self.check_field_access_by_type(on, field);
    }

    // The real field-lookup body of `check_field_access_on_type`, factored out so
    // `check_safe_field_access` (`x?.field`, see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.SafeFieldAccess` header) can
    // reuse it against the receiver's own STRIPPED (non-null) type name, without going through
    // `check_field_access_on_type`'s own "still-nullable receiver is an error" gate above -- same
    // reasoning as `check_method_call_by_type`, right above `check_method_call` itself.
    fn check_field_access_by_type(&mut self, on: String, field: String) -> Ty {
        if is_array_type_name(on) {
            if field == "length" { return TyInt; }
            self.error("array has no field '" + field + "' (only 'length')");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        self.ensure_instantiated(on);
        let found = self.structs.get(on);
        if let Some { value: info } = found {
            let ft = find_field_type(info, field);
            if ft == "?" {
                self.error("struct '" + on + "' has no field '" + field + "'");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            return ty_from_name(ft);
        } else {
            let ext_found = self.externs.get(on);
            var ext_known = false;
            if let Some { value: b } = ext_found { ext_known = true; }
            if ext_known {
                let f_found = self.extern_fields.get(on + "#" + field);
                if let Some { value: f } = f_found {
                    return ty_from_name(f.type_name);
                }
                // `recv.field` falling back to a zero-arg `getField`/`isField` instance method
                // (Java bean-getter naming) -- see README's own "Property-style access" section.
                // A real declared field always wins (checked above, first); this is pure fallback
                // sugar, never a silent choice between the two.
                let cap = capitalize_first_chk(field);
                let get_found = self.extern_methods.get(on + "#get" + cap + "#0");
                if let Some { value: gm } = get_found {
                    return ty_from_name(gm.ret_type);
                }
                let is_found = self.extern_methods.get(on + "#is" + cap + "#0");
                if let Some { value: im } = is_found {
                    return ty_from_name(im.ret_type);
                }
                self.error("unknown field '" + field + "' on extern class '" + on + "' (also tried 'get" + cap + "()'/'is" + cap + "()')");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            self.error("type '" + on + "' has no field '" + field + "'");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
    }

    fn check_field_assign(&mut self, obj: &Expr, field: String, value: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let ot = self.check_expr(obj);
        let on = ty_name(ot);
        // Phase 3 borrow checking: `obj.field = value` needs `obj` itself to be mutably
        // accessible -- a `var` local, an `&mut`-declared param, or `self` in an `&mut self`
        // method (see `TyChecker.mut_capable`'s own header). Scoped to a bare `Ident` receiver
        // only (covers the overwhelming common case, including `self.field = ...`) -- a nested
        // chain (`a.b.c = x`) or any other computed receiver shape is trusted without this check,
        // a real, disclosed narrower scope than a full alias-tracking borrow checker would have.
        match obj {
            Ident { name } => {
                if !self.is_mut_capable(name) {
                    self.error("cannot assign to field '" + field + "' through '" + name + "' -- it's not a 'var' local, an '&mut'-borrowed param, or '&mut self'");
                }
            }
            _ => {}
        }
        return self.check_field_assign_on_type(on, field, value);
    }

    // `arr_name[index].field` (read). Handles BOTH an arena buffer and an ordinary array-of-structs
    // -- `arr_name`'s ACTUAL declared type decides which, same real distinction `check_index`
    // itself draws, just reached differently: see `Ast.hc`'s own `IndexFieldGet` header for why
    // this needs to be its own AST shape (a plain `arr_name: String`, not a nested `Index` sub-
    // expr) instead of peeking `Expr::FieldAccess { obj: Index {...} } }`'s shape reactively.
    fn check_index_field_get(&mut self, arr_name: String, index: &Expr, field: String) -> Ty {
        let at = self.check_ident(arr_name);
        let an = ty_name(at);
        let it = self.check_expr(index);
        let in_ = ty_name(it);
        if in_ != "Int" && in_ != "?" {
            self.error("array index must be Int, got " + in_);
        }
        if is_arena_type_name_chk(an) {
            let struct_name = arena_struct_name_chk(an);
            let found = self.structs.get(struct_name);
            if let Some { value: info } = found {
                let ft = find_field_type(info, field);
                if ft == "?" {
                    self.error("arena struct '" + struct_name + "' has no field '" + field + "'");
                    return TyUnknown;
                }
                return ty_from_name(ft);
            } else {
                self.error("unknown arena struct '" + struct_name + "'");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
        } else if an == "?" {
            return TyUnknown;
        } else if !is_array_type_name(an) {
            self.error("type '" + an + "' is not an array, can't index it");
            return TyUnknown;
        } else {
            return self.check_field_access_on_type(array_elem_type_name(an), field);
        }
    }

    // `arr_name[index].field = value` (write) -- see `check_index_field_get`'s own header.
    fn check_index_field_set(&mut self, arr_name: String, index: &Expr, field: String, value: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let at = self.check_ident(arr_name);
        let an = ty_name(at);
        let it = self.check_expr(index);
        let in_ = ty_name(it);
        if in_ != "Int" && in_ != "?" {
            self.error("array index must be Int, got " + in_);
        }
        if is_arena_type_name_chk(an) {
            let vt = self.check_expr(value);
            let vn = ty_name(vt);
            let struct_name = arena_struct_name_chk(an);
            let found = self.structs.get(struct_name);
            if let Some { value: info } = found {
                let ft = find_field_type(info, field);
                if ft == "?" {
                    self.error("arena struct '" + struct_name + "' has no field '" + field + "'");
                } else if vn != "?" && ft != vn {
                    self.error("cannot assign " + vn + " to field '" + field + "' of type " + ft);
                }
            } else {
                self.error("unknown arena struct '" + struct_name + "'");
            }
            return ty_from_name(vn);
        } else {
            var elem_ty = "?";
            if an != "?" && is_array_type_name(an) { elem_ty = array_elem_type_name(an); }
            if an != "?" && !is_array_type_name(an) {
                self.error("type '" + an + "' is not an array, can't index it");
            }
            return self.check_field_assign_on_type(elem_ty, field, value);
        }
    }

    fn check_field_assign_on_type(&mut self, on: String, field: String, value: &Expr) -> Ty {
        if on == "?" {
            let vt0 = self.check_expr(value);
            return ty_from_name(ty_name(vt0));
        }
        self.ensure_instantiated(on);
        let found = self.structs.get(on);
        if let Some { value: info } = found {
            let ft = find_field_type(info, field);
            // Same real gap `check_struct_lit`'s own per-field loop just got fixed for (see that
            // fn's own header, added wiring `TextureCache` over to `Option<HashMap<Texture>>`):
            // `recv.field = None;`/`recv.field = Some { value: ... };` needs the field's own
            // DECLARED type as `check_struct_lit`'s `expected_mono` hint -- the naive `self.
            // check_expr(value)` this used to call unconditionally has no way to resolve a bare
            // generic variant on its own (`self.cur_ret_ty` is the wrong context here, same as
            // every other nested-nested-generic-literal spot this bug family shows up in).
            var vt = TyUnknown;
            var handled_variant_assign = false;
            if ft != "?" {
                match value {
                    StructLit { name: nname, field_names: nfnames, field_values: nfvals, generic_base: ngbase } => {
                        vt = self.check_struct_lit(nname, nfnames, nfvals, ngbase, some_string3(ft));
                        handled_variant_assign = true;
                    }
                    Ident { name: iname } => {
                        let variant_found3 = self.variant_owner.get(iname);
                        let generic_variant_found3 = self.generic_variant_owner.get(iname);
                        var is_known_variant3 = false;
                        if let Some { value: ow3 } = variant_found3 { is_known_variant3 = true; }
                        if let Some { value: gow3 } = generic_variant_found3 { is_known_variant3 = true; }
                        if is_known_variant3 {
                            vt = self.check_struct_lit(iname, empty_str_vec2(), empty_expr_vec3(), none_string3(), some_string3(ft));
                            handled_variant_assign = true;
                        }
                    }
                    _ => {}
                }
            }
            if !handled_variant_assign {
                vt = self.check_expr(value);
            }
            let vn = ty_name(vt);
            if ft == "?" {
                self.error("struct '" + on + "' has no field '" + field + "'");
            } else if vn != "?" && ft != vn {
                self.error("cannot assign " + vn + " to field '" + field + "' of type " + ft);
            }
            return ty_from_name(vn);
        } else {
            let vt = self.check_expr(value);
            let vn = ty_name(vt);
            let ext_found = self.externs.get(on);
            var ext_known = false;
            if let Some { value: b } = ext_found { ext_known = true; }
            if ext_known {
                let f_found = self.extern_fields.get(on + "#" + field);
                if let Some { value: f } = f_found {
                    if vn != "?" && f.type_name != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(f.type_name, vn) {
                        self.error("cannot assign " + vn + " to field '" + field + "' of type " + f.type_name);
                    }
                } else {
                    // `recv.field = value` falling back to a single-arg `setField` instance method
                    // (Java bean-setter naming) -- the write-side counterpart to the getter fallback
                    // above, see README's own "Property-style access" section. Same "real field
                    // always wins" rule: only reached when no declared field by this name exists.
                    let cap = capitalize_first_chk(field);
                    let set_found = self.extern_methods.get(on + "#set" + cap + "#1");
                    if let Some { value: sm } = set_found {
                        if sm.param_types.length() == 1 {
                            let pt = sm.param_types.get(0);
                            if vn != "?" && pt != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(pt, vn) {
                                self.error("cannot assign " + vn + " to field '" + field + "' via 'set" + cap + "(" + pt + ")'");
                            }
                        }
                    } else {
                        self.error("unknown field '" + field + "' on extern class '" + on + "' (also tried 'set" + cap + "(...)')");
                    }
                }
            } else {
                self.error("type '" + on + "' has no field '" + field + "'");
            }
            return ty_from_name(vn);
        }
    }

    // `name` may be a real struct's own name, or an enum variant's name -- resolved to whichever
    // name the field table is actually registered under (the enum's own name for a variant, see
    // `register_enum`) so the returned `Ty` is always the *constructible* type's real name.
    fn check_struct_lit(&mut self, name: String, field_names: Vec<String>, field_values: Vec<Expr>, generic_base: Option<String>, expected_mono: Option<String>) -> Ty {
        var type_name = name;
        // An explicitly-qualified generic-enum-variant construction (`Option<Int>::Some { ... }`)
        // already names its exact concrete instantiation -- skip the bare-name `variant_owner`
        // lookup entirely (see `Ast.hc`'s own `EnumDecl` header for why that lookup alone can't
        // disambiguate once a generic enum is used at more than one type argument).
        var is_qualified = false;
        if let Some { value: base } = generic_base {
            type_name = base;
            is_qualified = true;
        }
        if !is_qualified {
            // A `variant_owner` entry is only trustworthy here when its OWNER is itself a
            // non-mono name -- a real, hand-declared non-generic enum's own name never contains
            // "$" (e.g. a program declaring its OWN `enum Opt2 { Some {...}, None {} }` alongside
            // the always-injected prelude's `Option<T>`, real `demo_program34.hcsrc` usage: both
            // enums use the SAME variant names "Some"/"None"). When the owner IS a mono name
            // ("Option$Expr"), it's a SIDE EFFECT of whichever concrete instantiation happened to
            // monomorphize FIRST (`ensure_instantiated` -> `register_enum(mono_name, ...)`, the
            // same non-generic registration path a real one-shot enum uses) -- once that happens,
            // every LATER bare `Some { ... }`/`None` site would silently resolve to that first
            // instantiation's owner forever, regardless of what type argument this particular
            // site actually needs (real gap found self-compiling `Parser.hotc`, which constructs
            // `Option<T>` at several different `T`s: `Option<Expr>`, `Option<Vec<Stmt>>`,
            // `Option<String>`) -- `generic_variant_owner` (registered once at DECLARATION time,
            // never mutated) is checked instead in that case; `type_name` becomes the generic
            // BASE ("Option"), resolved to the real mono name by the block right below. If
            // NEITHER resolves cleanly, falls back to the (possibly stale) `variant_owner` entry
            // anyway -- better than nothing, preserves this lookup's original behavior for
            // whatever edge case neither table above was meant to cover.
            var resolved = false;
            let owner_found = self.variant_owner.get(name);
            if let Some { value: owner } = owner_found {
                if !is_mono_name(owner) {
                    type_name = owner;
                    resolved = true;
                }
            }
            if !resolved {
                let generic_owner_found = self.generic_variant_owner.get(name);
                if let Some { value: gowner } = generic_owner_found {
                    type_name = gowner;
                    resolved = true;
                }
            }
            if !resolved {
                let owner_found2 = self.variant_owner.get(name);
                if let Some { value: owner2 } = owner_found2 {
                    type_name = owner2;
                }
            }
        }
        // A bare generic struct/enum literal ("Vec { ... }" / "Some { ... }", no "<Arg>") relying
        // on the surrounding `return` statement's own declared return type to supply the missing
        // type argument -- e.g. `return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };` inside a fn declared `->
        // Vec<FnDecl>`, or `return Some { value: e };` inside a fn declared `-> Option<Expr>`. A
        // real, disclosed-scope gap found self-compiling `Parser.hotc`, whose own helper fns
        // (`empty_fn_decl_vec`, `some_expr`, etc) all follow exactly this idiom. Scope cut: ONLY
        // return-position inference (the only shape any real self-hosted source actually needs,
        // see `check_expr`'s own `StructLit` dispatch, which passes `self.cur_ret_ty`
        // unconditionally as `expected_mono`) -- `let`/`var` still have no declared-type
        // annotation to infer from at all (this subset's own `let`/`var` are always inferred from
        // `init`, never stored).
        if !is_qualified {
            var is_generic_base = false;
            let base_is_generic_struct = self.generic_templates.get(type_name);
            if let Some { value: bs } = base_is_generic_struct { is_generic_base = true; }
            let base_is_generic_enum = self.generic_enum_templates.get(type_name);
            if let Some { value: be } = base_is_generic_enum { is_generic_base = true; }
            if is_generic_base {
                var resolved_via_mono = false;
                if let Some { value: mono } = expected_mono {
                    if is_mono_name(mono) && mono_base(mono) == type_name {
                        type_name = mono;
                        resolved_via_mono = true;
                    }
                }
                // Fallback: the "Vec { data: someVar.data, len: 0 }" idiom -- an "empty, same-
                // element-type Vec" built by reusing an ALREADY-typed local's own backing array,
                // instead of via a dedicated `empty_*_vec`-style helper fn in return position
                // (which the block above already handles). Real gap found self-compiling
                // `Checker.hotc`'s own `checker_new`, which constructs several fields exactly this
                // way (`var mono = vec_of("__init__"); var mono_needed = Vec { data: mono.data,
                // len: 0 };`) -- a `var`/`let` binding has no declared-type annotation to infer
                // from at all in this subset (see this fn's own header), so a "data" field whose
                // value is LITERALLY `someIdent.data` is the next best signal: look up
                // `someIdent`'s own already-known type directly (a registry read, not a
                // `check_expr` call -- calling `check_expr` on the SAME sub-expression twice, once
                // here and once in the normal per-field validation loop below, would risk a
                // "use of moved value" on a non-Copy type, see this file's own header on that trap).
                if !resolved_via_mono {
                    var di = 0;
                    while di < field_names.length() {
                        if field_names.get(di) == "data" {
                            let fv = field_values.get(di);
                            match fv {
                                FieldAccess { obj, field } => {
                                    if field == "data" {
                                        match obj {
                                            Ident { name: oname } => {
                                                let ovar = self.lookup_var(oname);
                                                if let Some { value: oty } = ovar {
                                                    let on = ty_name(oty);
                                                    if is_mono_name(on) && mono_base(on) == type_name {
                                                        type_name = on;
                                                    }
                                                }
                                            }
                                            _ => {}
                                        }
                                    }
                                }
                                _ => {}
                            }
                        }
                        di = di + 1;
                    }
                }
                // Fallback: infer T directly from a field literal's own AST shape when neither of
                // the above resolved it -- a bare `let x = Box { value: 42 };`, no surrounding
                // return-type or "data"-idiom context to lean on (a real, reported gap: this exact
                // shape, `examples/generics.hc`'s `Box { value: 42 }`, failed with "unknown struct
                // or enum variant 'Box'" before this fallback existed). Mirrors
                // `check_generic_call`'s own "exact bare type-param match" inference for generic FN
                // calls, just applied to a struct literal's fields instead of a call's args.
                // Deliberately shape-only (`shape_only_ty`, no `check_expr` call) to avoid checking
                // the same field expression twice -- see the Vec-idiom fallback's own header, right
                // above, for why that matters. A real, disclosed scope cut: only literals and an
                // already-typed local var are recognized (see `shape_only_ty`'s own header);
                // anything else (a nested call, a field access, ...) still falls through to the
                // "unknown struct or enum variant" error below, unchanged from before this fallback.
                if !is_mono_name(type_name) {
                    let tmpl_found2 = self.generic_templates.get(type_name);
                    if let Some { value: tmpl2 } = tmpl_found2 {
                        var fi = 0;
                        var inferred_arg = "";
                        var found_arg = false;
                        while fi < tmpl2.field_types.length() && !found_arg {
                            if tmpl2.field_types.get(fi) == tmpl2.type_param {
                                let want_field = tmpl2.field_names.get(fi);
                                var li = 0;
                                while li < field_names.length() && !found_arg {
                                    if field_names.get(li) == want_field {
                                        let shape_ty = self.shape_only_ty(field_values.get(li));
                                        if shape_ty != "?" {
                                            inferred_arg = shape_ty;
                                            found_arg = true;
                                        }
                                    }
                                    li = li + 1;
                                }
                            }
                            fi = fi + 1;
                        }
                        if found_arg {
                            type_name = type_name + "$" + inferred_arg;
                        }
                    }
                    // Same fallback, generic-ENUM-variant shaped: `let s = Some { value: "hello" };`
                    // (`examples/genericenum.hc`'s own real, reported failure -- "unknown struct or
                    // enum variant 'Some'"). `type_name` here already names the enum BASE
                    // ("Option"), resolved from the bare variant name "Some" by the
                    // `generic_variant_owner` lookup above -- this fallback only fires when that
                    // resolved base is itself a generic enum template still lacking a concrete
                    // instantiation. Finds the ONE declared variant matching `name` ("Some") within
                    // the template, then that variant's own field whose type is exactly the bare
                    // type param, same shape-only inference the struct case above already uses.
                    if !is_mono_name(type_name) {
                        let etmpl_found = self.generic_enum_templates.get(type_name);
                        if let Some { value: etmpl } = etmpl_found {
                            var vi2 = 0;
                            var einferred = "";
                            var efound = false;
                            while vi2 < etmpl.variants.length() && !efound {
                                let ev = etmpl.variants.get(vi2);
                                if ev.name == name {
                                    var fj2 = 0;
                                    while fj2 < ev.fields.length() && !efound {
                                        let evf = ev.fields.get(fj2);
                                        if evf.type_name == etmpl.type_param {
                                            var li2 = 0;
                                            while li2 < field_names.length() && !efound {
                                                if field_names.get(li2) == evf.name {
                                                    let eshape = self.shape_only_ty(field_values.get(li2));
                                                    if eshape != "?" {
                                                        einferred = eshape;
                                                        efound = true;
                                                    }
                                                }
                                                li2 = li2 + 1;
                                            }
                                        }
                                        fj2 = fj2 + 1;
                                    }
                                }
                                vi2 = vi2 + 1;
                            }
                            if efound {
                                type_name = type_name + "$" + einferred;
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        self.ensure_instantiated(type_name);
        let found = self.structs.get(type_name);
        if let Some { value: info } = found {
            // Prefer the CONSTRUCTED VARIANT's own dedicated field table over the enum-wide union
            // `info` (see `variant_field_info`'s own header) whenever `name` names a real variant
            // -- falls back to `info` for a plain (non-enum) struct, where there's only ever one
            // table anyway.
            var field_info = info;
            let variant_info = self.variant_field_info.get(type_name + "#" + name);
            if let Some { value: vinfo } = variant_info {
                field_info = vinfo;
            }
            var i = 0;
            while i < field_values.length() {
                let fname = field_names.get(i);
                // Computed BEFORE the field value is checked (moved up from right below its own
                // former position) so a genuine empty array literal (`data: []`, see `Ast.hc`'s
                // own `array_lit` header) can be special-cased against it directly -- `check_expr`
                // alone has nothing to infer an empty array's element type FROM (no elements),
                // but the struct's own declared field type already says exactly what's needed.
                // Fetched RAW (un-normalized) once via `find_field_type_raw` -- calling BOTH that
                // and the normalizing `find_field_type` on the same owned `field_info` would move
                // it twice (a real "use of moved value" caught building this exact fix); `expected`
                // is derived from `expected_raw` by the same one-line rule `find_field_type` itself
                // applies, rather than a second lookup call.
                let expected_raw = find_field_type_raw(field_info, fname);
                var expected = expected_raw;
                if is_ranged_int_type_chk(expected_raw) { expected = "Int"; }
                var fty = TyUnknown;
                var handled_empty_array = false;
                if expected != "?" && is_array_type_name(expected) {
                    let fv_shape = field_values.get(i);
                    match fv_shape {
                        ArrayLit { elements } => {
                            if elements.length() == 0 {
                                fty = TyStruct { name: expected };
                                handled_empty_array = true;
                            }
                        }
                        _ => {}
                    }
                }
                // Second special case, same reasoning as the empty-array one right above: a field
                // VALUE that's itself a bare generic struct/enum literal (`cache: None`, `inner:
                // Some { value: 5 }`) needs the DECLARED field type as its own `expected_mono`
                // hint -- `check_expr`'s own `StructLit` arm only ever threads `self.cur_ret_ty`
                // (see that arm's own header: "harmless when not needed," true for a `return`
                // position, but a field nested inside ANOTHER struct literal has nothing to do
                // with the enclosing FUNCTION's return type). Real gap found wiring `TextureCache`
                // over to a real `Option<HashMap<Texture>>` field: `TextureCache { cache: None }`
                // failed with "unknown struct or enum variant 'None'" even though `cache`'s own
                // declared type (`"Option$HashMap$Texture"`) says exactly what `None` should
                // resolve to -- calling `check_struct_lit` directly here, with `expected` as the
                // real hint, mirrors the fix as closely as possible to how `Return`/`Let` already
                // supply it.
                var handled_nested_lit = false;
                if !handled_empty_array && expected != "?" {
                    let fv_shape2 = field_values.get(i);
                    match fv_shape2 {
                        StructLit { name: nname, field_names: nfnames, field_values: nfvals, generic_base: ngbase } => {
                            fty = self.check_struct_lit(nname, nfnames, nfvals, ngbase, some_string3(expected));
                            handled_nested_lit = true;
                        }
                        // A BARE fieldless-enum-variant reference (`cache: None`, no `{}` at all --
                        // `check_ident`'s own header explains why this parses as a plain `Ident`,
                        // not a `StructLit`) needs the exact same `expected`-hint treatment; without
                        // this arm too, `TextureCache { cache: None }` still failed even after the
                        // `StructLit` arm above landed, since `None` itself never reaches it.
                        Ident { name: iname } => {
                            let variant_found2 = self.variant_owner.get(iname);
                            let generic_variant_found2 = self.generic_variant_owner.get(iname);
                            var is_known_variant2 = false;
                            if let Some { value: ow2 } = variant_found2 { is_known_variant2 = true; }
                            if let Some { value: gow2 } = generic_variant_found2 { is_known_variant2 = true; }
                            if is_known_variant2 {
                                fty = self.check_struct_lit(iname, empty_str_vec2(), empty_expr_vec3(), none_string3(), some_string3(expected));
                                handled_nested_lit = true;
                            }
                        }
                        _ => {}
                    }
                }
                if !handled_empty_array && !handled_nested_lit {
                    let fv = field_values.get(i);
                    fty = self.check_expr(&fv);
                }
                let fn_ = ty_name(fty);
                // Scope cut, disclosed: struct-literal field VALUES are deliberately NOT
                // move-checked (unlike `let`/`return`/call arguments) -- sharing one already-built
                // value (a `Vec`, say) across several sibling struct literals is common and
                // perfectly safe under this language's actual reference semantics (nothing here
                // is EVER really "consumed" at runtime), and real construction code leans on that
                // constantly. See README's own "Compile-time safety roadmap" section.
                // `health: Int<0..=100>` as a STRUCT FIELD -- see `IDEAS.md`'s own "Numeric range
                // bounds" entry and `Parser.hotc`'s own `type_name_ref` header. A ranged-Int
                // field's OWN declared type string (`expected`, e.g. `"Int<0..=100>"`) never
                // equals a plain value's checked type (`fn_`, always `"Int"`), so this needs its
                // own branch BEFORE the ordinary mismatch check right below would otherwise
                // reject every correct assignment to one. Same "literal gets proven statically,
                // anything else gets a real runtime check" split the `Let`-statement version of
                // this feature already established (`Codegen.hotc`'s own mirrored struct-literal
                // codegen inserts the runtime half) -- scoped to PLAIN structs only this pass, not
                // enum variant fields (`gen_variant_construct`'s own separate `PUTFIELD` codegen
                // path isn't touched).
                if expected == "?" {
                    self.error("'" + name + "' has no field '" + fname + "'");
                } else if is_ranged_int_type_chk(expected_raw) && fn_ == "Int" {
                    match field_values.get(i) {
                        IntLit { value } => {
                            let flo = ranged_int_lo_chk(expected_raw);
                            let fhi = ranged_int_hi_chk(expected_raw);
                            let fincl = ranged_int_inclusive_chk(expected_raw);
                            var fout = value < flo;
                            if fincl {
                                if value > fhi { fout = true; }
                            } else {
                                if value >= fhi { fout = true; }
                            }
                            if fout {
                                self.error("field '" + fname + "' of '" + name + "' = " + JString::valueOf(value) + " is out of the declared range " + expected_raw);
                            }
                        }
                        _ => {}
                    }
                } else if fn_ != "?" && expected != fn_ {
                    self.error("field '" + fname + "' of '" + name + "' expects " + expected + ", got " + fn_);
                }
                i = i + 1;
            }
            return TyStruct { name: type_name };
        } else {
            self.error("unknown struct or enum variant '" + name + "'");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
    }

    // Cheap, non-mutating type guess from an expression's own AST shape -- deliberately no
    // `check_expr` call, so it's safe to use in a context (`check_struct_lit`'s own generic-
    // inference fallback, right above) where the SAME expression gets `check_expr`'d for real
    // immediately afterward in the normal per-field loop -- calling `check_expr` twice on one
    // expression risks a "use of moved value" false positive once move checking lands (see this
    // file's own header on that trap), so this stays intentionally shape-only rather than reusing
    // `check_expr` here. Narrow on purpose: only the literal/already-typed-local shapes generic
    // struct-literal field values actually need to carry a usable type guess; anything else (a
    // nested call, a field access, ...) returns "?" and the caller falls through unchanged.
    fn shape_only_ty(&mut self, e: &Expr) -> String {
        match e {
            IntLit { value } => { return "Int"; }
            StringLit { value } => { return "String"; }
            BoolLit { value } => { return "Bool"; }
            Ident { name } => {
                let v = self.lookup_var(name);
                if let Some { value: ty } = v { return ty_name(ty); }
                return "?";
            }
            // A NESTED struct literal (`Box { value: Player { name: "Kai", hp: 50 } }`) -- its own
            // `name` field IS the type name for the common, non-generic case, no recursion needed.
            // Real gap found running `examples/methods.hc`: a SECOND bare `Box { ... }` literal
            // instantiating `T` as a struct type (rather than the first literal's `Int`) failed with
            // "unknown struct or enum variant 'Box'", since this fn returned "?" for a `StructLit`
            // shape entirely, same as any other unrecognized expression. A disclosed narrower scope
            // than the sibling arms above: a nested literal that's ITSELF still-generic (`Box {
            // value: Box { value: 5 } }`) returns its own bare, unresolved base name here rather than
            // a real mono name -- correct for the common concrete-nested-struct case this fixes, not
            // a full recursive re-implementation of this whole inference pass.
            StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => { return name; }
            // `Box { value: hero.level }` -- a plain field read off an already-typed local. Real
            // gap found running `examples/showcase.hc`: same "?" fallthrough every other
            // unrecognized shape gets, so this recurses through `shape_only_ty` on `obj` (covers
            // `a.b.c`-shaped chains too, via `obj` itself possibly being ANOTHER `FieldAccess`) then
            // looks the field up in that struct's own registered field table -- a real, non-`?`
            // registry read, not a `check_expr` call, same "safe to call from inside
            // `check_struct_lit`'s own inference, no double-check risk" discipline this whole fn
            // already follows.
            FieldAccess { obj, field } => {
                let ot = self.shape_only_ty(obj);
                if ot == "?" { return "?"; }
                let info_found = self.structs.get(ot);
                if let Some { value: info } = info_found {
                    return find_field_type(info, field);
                }
                return "?";
            }
            _ => { return "?"; }
        }
    }

    // Called once per `StructDecl` (by the driver, before any fn body is checked -- mirrors
    // `fn_sigs`' own forward-registration so field types resolve regardless of declaration
    // order). Declared field types are trusted at face value the same way a fn param's declared
    // type is -- see `ty_from_name`. A *generic* struct (`type_param` is `Some`) goes into
    // `generic_templates` instead -- it isn't directly usable until `ensure_instantiated`
    // monomorphizes some concrete `Base$Arg` name for it.
    fn register_struct(&mut self, name: String, type_param: Option<String>, fields: Vec<Param>, is_arena: Bool, super_class: Option<String>, type_param2: Option<String>) {
        var names = empty_str_vec2();
        var types = empty_str_vec2();
        var i = 0;
        while i < fields.length() {
            let f = fields.get(i);
            // Phase 3 borrow checking's own soundness rule (see README's own "Compile-time safety
            // roadmap" section): a struct can never HOLD a borrowed reference -- only owned
            // values -- since there's no lifetime/region tracking to know whether that reference
            // still points at something alive. `struct_fields` parses field declarations through
            // the same `one_param()` a real fn param uses, so `&`/`&mut` parsed there silently
            // WOULD set `is_ref`/`is_mut_ref` with nothing rejecting it before this fix.
            if f.is_ref {
                self.error("struct '" + name + "' field '" + f.name + "' cannot be a reference type ('&" + f.type_name + "') -- a struct can only hold owned values");
            }
            names.push(f.name);
            types.push(f.type_name);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if let Some { value: tp } = type_param {
            self.generic_templates.register(name, GenericTemplate { type_param: tp, field_names: names, field_types: types, type_param2: type_param2 });
        } else {
            self.structs.register(name, StructInfo { field_names: names, field_types: types });
        }
        if is_arena {
            self.arena_structs.register(name, true);
        }
        // `struct S extends C { }` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `StructDecl.extends` header. Whether `C`
        // itself is really a KNOWN extern class is intentionally NOT checked here: registration
        // happens in one flat pass over every struct before externs get their own pass (real
        // ordering `Codegen.hotc`'s own `compile_program` uses), so a same-file `extends` target
        // declared LATER in source would look unregistered yet even though it's perfectly valid --
        // `check_static_call`'s own `::new` fallback (the only reader of this registry) checks
        // `self.externs` itself, once every registration pass is long done.
        if let Some { value: sup } = super_class {
            if fields.length() > 0 {
                self.error("struct '" + name + "' extends '" + sup + "' but declares its own field(s) -- an extends-struct must have zero fields this phase");
            }
            self.struct_extends.register(name, sup);
        }
    }

    fn warn(&mut self, msg: String) {
        self.warnings.push(msg);
    }

    // Called once per `ComponentDecl` (mirrors `register_struct`, minus generics/`arena`/
    // `extends` -- a `component` is plain data, none of those apply). Registers the SAME name
    // into `self.structs` too (a component's fields work exactly like a struct's for every other
    // purpose) plus `self.components`, the one extra fact a `system`'s own `run` params get
    // checked against.
    fn register_component(&mut self, name: String, fields: Vec<Param>) {
        var names = empty_str_vec2();
        var types = empty_str_vec2();
        var i = 0;
        while i < fields.length() {
            let f = fields.get(i);
            // Same soundness rule `register_struct` enforces on its own fields, and for the same
            // reason -- see that fn's own comment.
            if f.is_ref {
                self.error("component '" + name + "' field '" + f.name + "' cannot be a reference type ('&" + f.type_name + "') -- a component can only hold owned values");
            }
            names.push(f.name);
            types.push(f.type_name);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        self.structs.register(name, StructInfo { field_names: names, field_types: types });
        self.components.register(name, true);
    }

    // Called once per `resource Name { ... }` -- identical shape to `register_component` right
    // above (same field-soundness rule, same "register into `self.structs` too" reasoning), but
    // into `self.resources` instead of `self.components` -- the ONE fact that lets `check_systems`
    // tell a resource-typed `run` param apart from an ordinary component-typed one.
    fn register_resource(&mut self, name: String, fields: Vec<Param>) {
        var names2 = empty_str_vec2();
        var types2 = empty_str_vec2();
        var i2 = 0;
        while i2 < fields.length() {
            let f2 = fields.get(i2);
            if f2.is_ref {
                self.error("resource '" + name + "' field '" + f2.name + "' cannot be a reference type ('&" + f2.type_name + "') -- a resource can only hold owned values");
            }
            names2.push(f2.name);
            types2.push(f2.type_name);
            i2 = i2 + 1;
        }
        self.structs.register(name, StructInfo { field_names: names2, field_types: types2 });
        self.resources.register(name, true);
    }

    // The real ownership-derived scheduling analysis -- see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own design doc for
    // the full picture. Called once, after every `component` is already registered (so a `run`
    // param's type can be validated against `self.components`). Three things happen, in order:
    // (1) validate every system (its `run` params must each name a known `component` by `&`/
    // `&mut` -- never by value, since a system borrows a component row, it doesn't own it; derive
    // the READ set from `is_ref && !is_mut_ref` params and the WRITE set from `is_mut_ref` ones --
    // this IS the query, no separate declaration needed -- EXCEPT for the two query-filter param
    // shapes, `Without<Component>`/`Changed<Component>`, which name a component WITHOUT `&`/
    // `&mut` at all, since neither borrows a real value; see the dedicated branches for both,
    // right in the loop below) and validate `@after`/`@before` targets
    // name real systems; (2) a real, always-on warning (see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Resolved
    // decisions" #6 -- the CLI opt-out for rapid iteration isn't wired yet, a disclosed following-
    // slice gap) for every pair of systems that conflict (`systems_conflict_ecs`) -- named by
    // system and by ONE shared component, so losing parallelism is never silent; (3) the real
    // execution order (`self.system_order`), topologically sorted by `@after`/`@before`
    // constraints with declaration order as the tie-break for anything they don't decide (see
    // "Resolved decisions" #4) -- a genuine cycle is a real error, not silently ignored.
    // `fns` is passed in directly (not read from `self.fn_sigs`) purely because of WHEN this runs:
    // `compile_program` calls `check_systems` right after components/resources register, but
    // BEFORE any ordinary top-level `fn` gets its own signature registered into `self.fn_sigs` --
    // so a `self.fn_sigs.get(rif)` lookup here would always fail even for a real, correctly-typed
    // `@run_if` target. Real gap found writing this feature's own test: `@run_if(regen_enabled)`
    // failed with "no fn named 'regen_enabled' exists" even though it was declared right there in
    // the same file. Scanning `fns` directly sidesteps the ordering question entirely.
    fn check_systems(&mut self, systems: &Vec<SystemDecl>, fns: &Vec<FnDecl>) {
        var names = empty_str_vec2();
        var reads = empty_str_vec_vec_ecs();
        var writes = empty_str_vec_vec_ecs();
        // `Vec<Vec<String>>`, not `Vec<String>` -- `@after(A, B, ...)` multi-target (2026-09-17,
        // `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Still open" #1), each system's own after/before list in full.
        // UNFILTERED (raw `sysd.after`/`sysd.before`, straight off the AST) -- the real, filtered
        // `after`/`before` (unknown-name entries dropped) get built fresh, by `.push()` alone, in
        // the second pass below, once `names` is complete enough to validate against. Deliberately
        // never mutated via `.set()` on a nested `Vec<Vec<String>>` entry -- a real, previously-
        // hit gap (see `group_variant_names_by_enum_cg`'s own header in `Codegen.hotc`): that
        // specific shape needs its own `.set` monomorphized, and nothing else in this compiler had
        // ever called it before, which produced a genuine `NoSuchMethodError` at RUNTIME the first
        // time something tried, even though compilation itself reported success.
        var raw_after = empty_str_vec_vec_ecs();
        var raw_before = empty_str_vec_vec_ecs();
        // `@run_if(condition_fn)` -- parallel to `names` (see `Ast.hc`'s own `SystemDecl.run_if`
        // header). `""` for a system with no `@run_if`.
        var run_ifs = empty_str_vec2();
        // `@main_thread` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `SystemDecl.has_main_thread` header. Indexed the
        // SAME way as `names`/`reads`/`writes` (built in this same loop), read by both
        // `systems_conflict_main_thread_ecs` call sites below.
        var main_thread = empty_bool_vec_chk();
        var i = 0;
        while i < systems.length() {
            let sysd = systems.get(i);
            names.push(sysd.name);
            main_thread.push(sysd.has_main_thread);
            var sys_reads = empty_str_vec2();
            var sys_writes = empty_str_vec2();
            var pi = 0;
            while pi < sysd.run_params.length() {
                let p = sysd.run_params.get(pi);
                // `Without<Component>`/`Changed<Component>` -- query filters, not component
                // borrows (see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Query filters" design note). Both ride the
                // EXISTING generic type-name syntax `type_name_ref` (`Parser.hotc`) already parses
                // for free (`Name<Arg>` -> the mono-name string `"Name$Arg"`, same convention
                // `Vec<Int>` uses) -- no grammar change needed, just recognizing these two
                // specific bases here before the ordinary component-borrow checks below, which
                // would otherwise misfire on them (neither takes `&`/`&mut` at all, since neither
                // binds a real value the synthesized `run` fn ever receives -- see `Codegen.hotc`'s
                // own `real_run_params_ecs`).
                if is_mono_name(p.type_name) && mono_base(p.type_name) == "Without" {
                    let excluded = mono_arg(p.type_name);
                    let exc_found = self.components.get(excluded);
                    var exc_known = false;
                    if let Some { value: _eck } = exc_found { exc_known = true; }
                    if !exc_known {
                        self.error("system '" + sysd.name + "' run param '" + p.name + "' names 'Without<" + excluded + ">', but '" + excluded + "' isn't a known 'component'");
                    }
                    // Excludes, touches no data -- no read/write, no scheduling-conflict
                    // implication at all (see `Codegen.hotc`'s own `excluded_components_of_
                    // system_ecs`, the only other reader of this filter kind).
                } else if is_mono_name(p.type_name) && mono_base(p.type_name) == "Changed" {
                    let changed_of = mono_arg(p.type_name);
                    let chg_found = self.components.get(changed_of);
                    var chg_known = false;
                    if let Some { value: _cck } = chg_found { chg_known = true; }
                    if !chg_known {
                        self.error("system '" + sysd.name + "' run param '" + p.name + "' names 'Changed<" + changed_of + ">', but '" + changed_of + "' isn't a known 'component'");
                    }
                    // Reads the component's own change-flag -- real data touched, so this DOES
                    // conflict with a concurrent WRITER of the same component (a writer might be
                    // updating the flag this same instant); folded into the ordinary read set so
                    // `systems_conflict_ecs` already forces the right serialization the day this
                    // actually runs.
                    sys_reads.push(changed_of);
                    // Real, implemented: a per-archetype-row `Vec<Bool>` change flag per component
                    // (`Codegen.hotc`'s own `archetype_field_changed_name_ecs`) is reset to `false`
                    // for every row at the START of each `world.run_all()` call (right after that
                    // call's own queued add/remove flush -- see `build_reset_changed_stmts_ecs`'s
                    // own header for why a flush-time "just added"/"just moved-into" flag does NOT
                    // survive this reset), then set `true` for any row a `&mut`-taking system's
                    // own dispatch touches during THIS SAME call. `Changed<T>` reads that flag per
                    // row, skipping the whole `run` call for a row where it's `false` (see
                    // `Codegen.hotc`'s own `synthesize_system_call_stmts_ecs`). Real, disclosed
                    // tick-boundary consequence: within one `world.run_all()` call, a `Changed<T>`
                    // reader only sees writes from `&mut`-taking systems scheduled BEFORE it that
                    // same call -- never a `world.add`/`world.remove` flushed that same call, and
                    // never a system scheduled AFTER it (that read simply sees "no changes yet
                    // this tick," not last tick's staleness). `check_systems`
                    // already forces a `Changed<T>` reader to serialize against a concurrent `&mut`
                    // writer of the same component (folded into `sys_reads` above), so this is an
                    // ordering nuance, not a thread-safety hazard.
                } else if resource_known_ecs(&self.resources, p.type_name) {
                    // A `resource`-typed param -- a single, world-wide value (see `Ast.hc`'s own
                    // `Program.resources` header), never archetype-matched, but still borrowed by
                    // `&`/`&mut` like a component, and still folded into the SAME conflict
                    // analysis right below: two systems both wanting `&mut` on the SAME resource
                    // (or one `&mut` while another `&`) genuinely can't run in parallel, exactly
                    // like two systems fighting over the same component would.
                    if !p.is_ref {
                        self.error("system '" + sysd.name + "' run param '" + p.name + "' must borrow its resource ('&" + p.type_name + "' or '&mut " + p.type_name + "') -- a system can't take ownership of one");
                    } else if p.is_mut_ref {
                        sys_writes.push(p.type_name);
                    } else {
                        sys_reads.push(p.type_name);
                    }
                } else {
                    let comp_found = self.components.get(p.type_name);
                    var comp_known = false;
                    if let Some { value: _ck } = comp_found { comp_known = true; }
                    if !comp_known {
                        self.error("system '" + sysd.name + "' run param '" + p.name + "' names '" + p.type_name + "', which isn't a known 'component' or 'resource'");
                    }
                    if !p.is_ref {
                        self.error("system '" + sysd.name + "' run param '" + p.name + "' must borrow its component ('&" + p.type_name + "' or '&mut " + p.type_name + "') -- a system can't take ownership of one");
                    } else if p.is_mut_ref {
                        sys_writes.push(p.type_name);
                    } else {
                        sys_reads.push(p.type_name);
                    }
                }
                pi = pi + 1;
            }
            reads.push(sys_reads);
            writes.push(sys_writes);
            raw_after.push(sysd.after);
            raw_before.push(sysd.before);
            run_ifs.push(sysd.run_if);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        var after = empty_str_vec_vec_ecs();
        var before = empty_str_vec_vec_ecs();
        var j = 0;
        while j < names.length() {
            // Every name in EACH list gets checked -- multi-target means there's no single "" to
            // downgrade to on an unknown target anymore, so an unresolved name is instead dropped
            // straight out of that system's own list (mirrors the old single-target "downgrade to
            // no constraint" reasoning, just per-entry now): a target that never becomes "placed"
            // would otherwise surface as a spurious cycle error stacked on top of the real one
            // just reported.
            var valid_after = empty_str_vec2();
            var ai2 = 0;
            let this_after = raw_after.get(j);
            while ai2 < this_after.length() {
                let an2 = this_after.get(ai2);
                if str_vec_contains_ecs(names, an2) {
                    valid_after.push(an2);
                } else {
                    self.error("system '" + names.get(j) + "' has '@after(" + an2 + ")', but no system named '" + an2 + "' exists");
                }
                ai2 = ai2 + 1;
            }
            after.push(valid_after);
            var valid_before = empty_str_vec2();
            var bi3 = 0;
            let this_before = raw_before.get(j);
            while bi3 < this_before.length() {
                let bn2 = this_before.get(bi3);
                if str_vec_contains_ecs(names, bn2) {
                    valid_before.push(bn2);
                } else {
                    self.error("system '" + names.get(j) + "' has '@before(" + bn2 + ")', but no system named '" + bn2 + "' exists");
                }
                bi3 = bi3 + 1;
            }
            before.push(valid_before);
            let rif = run_ifs.get(j);
            if rif != "" {
                var rif_found = false;
                var fi3 = 0;
                while fi3 < fns.length() {
                    let fd3 = fns.get(fi3);
                    if fd3.name == rif {
                        rif_found = true;
                        if fd3.params.length() != 0 {
                            self.error("system '" + names.get(j) + "' has '@run_if(" + rif + ")', but '" + rif + "' takes " + JString::valueOf(fd3.params.length()) + " parameter(s) -- it must take none");
                        }
                        let rif_ret = opt_string_or2(fd3.ret_type, "Unit");
                        if rif_ret != "Bool" {
                            self.error("system '" + names.get(j) + "' has '@run_if(" + rif + ")', but '" + rif + "' returns " + rif_ret + ", not Bool");
                        }
                    }
                    fi3 = fi3 + 1;
                }
                if !rif_found {
                    self.error("system '" + names.get(j) + "' has '@run_if(" + rif + ")', but no fn named '" + rif + "' exists");
                }
            }
            j = j + 1;
        }
        var k = 0;
        while k < names.length() {
            var m = k + 1;
            while m < names.length() {
                if systems_conflict_or_main_thread_ecs(reads.get(k), writes.get(k), main_thread.get(k), reads.get(m), writes.get(m), main_thread.get(m)) {
                    var shared = first_shared_component_ecs(writes.get(k), reads.get(m));
                    if shared == "" { shared = first_shared_component_ecs(writes.get(k), writes.get(m)); }
                    if shared == "" { shared = first_shared_component_ecs(writes.get(m), reads.get(k)); }
                    if shared == "" && main_thread.get(k) && main_thread.get(m) {
                        self.warn("systems '" + names.get(k) + "' and '" + names.get(m) + "' are both '@main_thread' -- forced to run sequentially, can't be scheduled in parallel");
                    } else {
                        self.warn("systems '" + names.get(k) + "' and '" + names.get(m) + "' both touch component '" + shared + "' (at least one writes it) -- forced to run sequentially, can't be scheduled in parallel");
                    }
                }
                m = m + 1;
            }
            k = k + 1;
        }
        // Phase B (real parallel dispatch, see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own design doc) needs more than a
        // flat order -- it needs to know WHICH ready systems can run in the SAME parallel batch.
        // Same Kahn's-algorithm shape as the flat version this replaced, but each round picks
        // EVERY ready, pairwise-non-conflicting system (in declaration order, greedily -- a
        // candidate joins the round's own group only if it doesn't conflict with anyone ALREADY
        // in it) instead of just the first one, so unrelated, conflict-free systems land in the
        // same group and become one real parallel batch (`Codegen.hotc`'s own `build_run_all_
        // body_ecs` dispatches a >1-member group onto Phoenix Flight's pool, `.join()`ing all of
        // them before the next group runs) -- a single-member group is still just a direct call,
        // identical to Phase A's own fully-sequential behavior. `self.system_order` (the flattened
        // group-by-group listing) is kept too, since nothing downstream needs to change to keep
        // reading it as a flat order.
        let n = names.length();
        var placed = bool_vec_of_len_ecs(n);
        var order = empty_str_vec2();
        var groups = empty_str_vec_vec_ecs();
        var remaining = n;
        var had_cycle = false;
        while remaining > 0 && !had_cycle {
            var group = empty_str_vec2();
            var group_idx = empty_int_vec_ecs();
            var group_reads = empty_str_vec_vec_ecs();
            var group_writes = empty_str_vec_vec_ecs();
            var group_main_thread = empty_bool_vec_chk();
            var gi = 0;
            while gi < n {
                if !placed.get(gi) && system_ready_ecs(gi, names, after, before, placed) {
                    var conflicts = false;
                    var mi = 0;
                    while mi < group.length() {
                        if systems_conflict_or_main_thread_ecs(reads.get(gi), writes.get(gi), main_thread.get(gi), group_reads.get(mi), group_writes.get(mi), group_main_thread.get(mi)) {
                            conflicts = true;
                        }
                        mi = mi + 1;
                    }
                    if !conflicts {
                        group.push(names.get(gi));
                        group_idx.push(gi);
                        group_reads.push(reads.get(gi));
                        group_writes.push(writes.get(gi));
                        group_main_thread.push(main_thread.get(gi));
                    }
                }
                gi = gi + 1;
            }
            if group.length() == 0 {
                self.error("system scheduling has a cycle in '@after'/'@before' constraints -- can't determine a real execution order");
                var bi2 = 0;
                while bi2 < n {
                    if !placed.get(bi2) {
                        order.push(names.get(bi2));
                        placed.set(bi2, true);
                    }
                    bi2 = bi2 + 1;
                }
                had_cycle = true;
            } else {
                var gj = 0;
                while gj < group.length() {
                    order.push(group.get(gj));
                    placed.set(group_idx.get(gj), true);
                    gj = gj + 1;
                }
                remaining = remaining - group.length();
                groups.push(group);
            }
        }
        self.system_order = order;
        self.system_groups = groups;
    }

    // Called once per `ExternClassDecl` (mirrors `register_struct`/`register_enum`). Declared
    // signatures are trusted at face value -- no real classpath reflection this phase (see
    // `Ast.hc`'s own `ExternClassDecl` header), the same "declared type is truth" honesty every
    // other declared type already gets in this checker.
    fn register_extern(&mut self, name: String, binary_name: String, methods: Vec<ExternMethod>, fields: Vec<ExternField>) {
        self.externs.register(name, binary_name);
        var fi = 0;
        while fi < fields.length() {
            let f = fields.get(fi);
            self.extern_fields.register(name + "#" + f.name, ExternFieldInfo { type_name: f.type_name, is_static: f.is_static });
            fi = fi + 1;
        }
        var i = 0;
        while i < methods.length() {
            let m = methods.get(i);
            // Keyed "Alias#methodName#arity", not just "Alias#methodName" -- the bare-name key
            // let only ONE overload of any given real Java method name (or constructor -- every
            // ctor is declared `fn new(...)`, so "Alias#new" always collided too) bind per alias,
            // which is why real ports needed a separate alias per arity (`JFileQGM`/`JFileDirQGM`,
            // both really `java.io.File`, split only because its 1-arg and 2-arg constructors
            // couldn't coexist under one alias). Arity alone doesn't disambiguate two overloads of
            // the SAME arity but different param types (Gson's `addProperty(String,String)` vs
            // `addProperty(String,Number)`) -- used to remain a real, disclosed narrower scope;
            // closed now by ALSO registering a full param-type-signature key (below) for every
            // overload -- `check_static_call`/`check_method_call` try that precise key FIRST,
            // falling back to this arity-only one (last-overload-registered-wins, same as always)
            // only when no exact-type match exists. Purely additive: a call site that used to
            // resolve via the arity key still does, since that key is still registered exactly as
            // before -- this only adds a MORE precise match multiple same-arity overloads can each
            // have their own entry for.
            let ptypes = param_type_names_of4(m.params);
            let key = name + "#" + m.name + "#" + JString::valueOf(m.params.length());
            self.extern_methods.register(key, ExternMethodInfo { param_types: ptypes, ret_type: m.ret_type, is_static: m.is_static, is_ctor: m.is_ctor });
            let sig_key = name + "#" + m.name + "#" + JString::valueOf(m.params.length()) + "#" + join_type_names_chk(ptypes);
            self.extern_methods.register(sig_key, ExternMethodInfo { param_types: ptypes, ret_type: m.ret_type, is_static: m.is_static, is_ctor: m.is_ctor });
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    // Called once per `InterfaceDecl`, before any fn/method body is checked (same forward-
    // registration convention `register_struct`/`register_extern` already use) -- `check_method_
    // call`'s own "dyn" branch is the only reader. Registers the FULL, FLATTENED method set (own
    // methods plus every method inherited, transitively, from `extends`) via `Ast.hc`'s own
    // `flatten_interface_methods` -- every downstream reader (`check_method_call`'s "dyn" branch,
    // `check_interface_completeness`, `Codegen.hotc`'s own "dyn Name#method" descriptor table) sees
    // an interface's real, inherited method set this way without having to walk `extends` itself.
    // `all_interfaces` is the WHOLE program's interface list (not just this one) -- flattening a
    // supertrait chain needs to look up other `InterfaceDecl`s by name, which a single `InterfaceDecl`
    // alone can't do.
    fn register_interface(&mut self, name: String, is_sealed: Bool, parents: Vec<String>, all_interfaces: &Vec<InterfaceDecl>) {
        if self.extends_reaches(name, name, &all_interfaces, 0) {
            self.error("interface inheritance cycle involving '" + name + "'");
        }
        var si = 0;
        while si < parents.length() {
            let sup = parents.get(si);
            if !self.interface_exists(sup, &all_interfaces) {
                self.error("interface '" + name + "' extends unknown interface '" + sup + "'");
            }
            si = si + 1;
        }
        let flattened = flatten_interface_methods(&all_interfaces, name);
        self.interfaces.register(name, flattened);
        let supertraits = transitive_supertraits(&all_interfaces, name, 0);
        self.interface_supertraits.register(name, supertraits);
        if is_sealed {
            self.sealed_interfaces.register(name, true);
        }
    }

    // Does walking `from`'s own `extends` chain, transitively, ever reach `target`? Called as
    // `self.extends_reaches(name, name, ...)` by `register_interface` above to detect a real
    // inheritance cycle (`interface A : B` + `interface B : A`, or a longer chain back to itself) --
    // depth-capped at 20 rather than a real visited-set for the same reason `Ast.hc`'s own
    // `flatten_interface_methods_depth` is (see that fn's own header): a real cycle terminates here
    // either way (just returns `false` past the cap instead of `true`), so the cap only risks
    // under-reporting an absurdly deep chain, never a false cycle report on legitimate code.
    fn interface_exists(&mut self, name: String, all_interfaces: &Vec<InterfaceDecl>) -> Bool {
        var i = 0;
        while i < all_interfaces.length() {
            if all_interfaces.get(i).name == name {
                return true;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return false;
    }

    fn extends_reaches(&mut self, from: String, target: String, all_interfaces: &Vec<InterfaceDecl>, depth: Int) -> Bool {
        if depth > 20 {
            return false;
        }
        var i = 0;
        while i < all_interfaces.length() {
            let ifd = all_interfaces.get(i);
            if ifd.name == from {
                var si = 0;
                while si < ifd.parents.length() {
                    let sup = ifd.parents.get(si);
                    if sup == target {
                        return true;
                    }
                    if self.extends_reaches(sup, target, &all_interfaces, depth + 1) {
                        return true;
                    }
                    si = si + 1;
                }
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return false;
    }

    // Called once per `StaticDecl` (mirrors `register_struct`/`register_extern`'s own forward-
    // registration, done before any fn body is checked so a static's declaration order relative to
    // its uses never matters) -- the declared `type_name` is trusted at face value here, same
    // "declared type is truth" honesty every other declared type already gets in this checker.
    // `s.value` is NOT evaluated against it here -- this fires too early, before every fn/method
    // signature and generic template is registered, so a generic call inside an initializer
    // couldn't resolve its instantiation yet. `Codegen.hotc`'s own `compile_program` runs a real
    // `check_expr` (and the matching type check) over every static's `.value` in a later pass,
    // once the rest of the program is fully registered -- see its own header, right before the
    // loop that does it, for the real bug this closed (`StringIndexOutOfBoundsException` deep in
    // ASM, not a clean compile error).
    fn register_static_decl(&mut self, s: &StaticDecl) {
        self.statics.register(s.name, ty_from_name(s.type_name));
    }

    // If `name` is a monomorphized instantiation (`"Box$Int"`) not yet concretely registered,
    // substitutes its generic template's type parameter with the real argument and registers the
    // result as an ordinary struct under `name` -- after this call, `name` behaves exactly like
    // any real, non-generic struct everywhere else in this file. Idempotent (checks `self.structs`
    // first) and records `name` into `mono_needed` on first instantiation only. Cascades: if a
    // substituted field type is itself a *different* generic instantiation (`"Vec$Int"`, from a
    // declared field like `Registry<T> { entries: Vec<T> }`), that nested instantiation gets
    // `ensure_instantiated`'d right here too -- otherwise `"Vec$Int"` would sit in `Registry$Int`'s
    // own field table naming a class that never actually got emitted, a real
    // `NoClassDefFoundError` waiting to happen at runtime the moment the field was ever accessed
    // by code this checker hadn't *also* happened to check some other way (see
    // `substitute_field_type`'s own header for the plain substitution half of this).
    fn ensure_instantiated(&mut self, name: String) {
        if !is_mono_name(name) { return; }
        let already = self.structs.get(name);
        var exists = false;
        if let Some { value: x } = already { exists = true; }
        if exists { return; }
        let base = mono_base(name);
        let arg = mono_arg(name);
        let tmpl_found = self.generic_templates.get(base);
        if let Some { value: tmpl } = tmpl_found {
            // `struct HashMap<K, V> { ... }` -- a SECOND type param, mirroring the enum branch
            // right below exactly (see that branch's own header for the full reasoning):
            // `mono_arg(name)` alone is right for a one-param template, wrong for a two-param one
            // ("HashMap$ItemStack$Int" needs "ItemStack" and "Int" separately) -- `mono_arg1_of2`/
            // `mono_arg2_of2` do that split, ONLY reached when `tmpl.type_param2` is actually
            // `Some`.
            var has_tp2s = false;
            var tp2s = "";
            if let Some { value: tp2sv } = tmpl.type_param2 { has_tp2s = true; tp2s = tp2sv; }
            var arg2s = "";
            var real_args = arg;
            if has_tp2s {
                real_args = mono_arg1_of2(name);
                arg2s = mono_arg2_of2(name);
            }
            var fields = empty_param_vec3();
            var i = 0;
            while i < tmpl.field_names.length() {
                var ftype = substitute_field_type(tmpl.field_types.get(i), tmpl.type_param, real_args);
                if has_tp2s {
                    ftype = substitute_field_type(ftype, tp2s, arg2s);
                }
                let ctarget = cascade_target(ftype);
                if is_mono_name(ctarget) {
                    self.ensure_instantiated(ctarget);
                }
                fields.push(Param { name: tmpl.field_names.get(i), type_name: ftype, is_ref: false, is_mut_ref: false });
                i = i + 1;
            }
            self.register_struct(name, none_string3(), fields, false, none_string3(), none_string3());
            self.mono_needed.push(name);
            return;
        }
        let etmpl_found = self.generic_enum_templates.get(base);
        if let Some { value: etmpl } = etmpl_found {
            // `Result<T, E>` -- a SECOND type param, see `Ast.hc`'s own `EnumDecl.type_param2`
            // header. `mono_arg(name)` alone (everything after the FIRST `$`) is exactly right for
            // a one-param template (its own arg might itself be a nested mono name, e.g.
            // "Box$Vec$Int"), but wrong for a two-param one ("Result$Int$String" needs "Int" and
            // "String" separately) -- `mono_arg1_of2`/`mono_arg2_of2` do that split instead,
            // ONLY reached when `etmpl.type_param2` is actually `Some` (a one-param template never
            // takes this branch, so its own possibly-nested single arg is never mis-split).
            var has_tp2 = false;
            var tp2 = "";
            if let Some { value: tp2v } = etmpl.type_param2 { has_tp2 = true; tp2 = tp2v; }
            var arg2 = "";
            var real_arg = arg;
            if has_tp2 {
                real_arg = mono_arg1_of2(name);
                arg2 = mono_arg2_of2(name);
            }
            var new_variants = empty_variant_vec3();
            var vi2 = 0;
            while vi2 < etmpl.variants.length() {
                let v = etmpl.variants.get(vi2);
                var new_fields = empty_param_vec3();
                var fi3 = 0;
                while fi3 < v.fields.length() {
                    let f = v.fields.get(fi3);
                    var ftype2 = substitute_field_type(f.type_name, etmpl.type_param, real_arg);
                    if has_tp2 {
                        ftype2 = substitute_field_type(ftype2, tp2, arg2);
                    }
                    let ctarget2 = cascade_target(ftype2);
                    if is_mono_name(ctarget2) {
                        self.ensure_instantiated(ctarget2);
                    }
                    new_fields.push(Param { name: f.name, type_name: ftype2, is_ref: false, is_mut_ref: false });
                    fi3 = fi3 + 1;
                }
                new_variants.push(EnumVariant { name: v.name, fields: new_fields });
                vi2 = vi2 + 1;
            }
            self.register_enum(name, none_string3(), new_variants, none_string3());
            self.mono_needed.push(name);
        }
    }

    // If `name` is a monomorphized FUNCTION instantiation (`"identity$Int"`) not yet concretely
    // registered, substitutes its generic template's type parameter into every param's declared
    // type (and the return type), registers the concrete signature under `name`, and re-checks a
    // substituted-params-AND-BODY copy of the template's own body against `name` (reusing
    // `check_fn` directly, same as a static impl method does) -- after this call, `name` behaves
    // exactly like any real, non-generic fn everywhere else in this file (`check_call` looks it up
    // in `fn_sigs`). Idempotent and records `name` into `fn_mono_needed` on first instantiation
    // only, mirroring `ensure_instantiated`. The body itself is walked too now (`subst_stmt_list`,
    // closing a gap disclosed earlier): a generic fn's body CAN reference its own still-abstract
    // `T` inside a nested generic construction site (`vec_of<T>`'s own `return Vec<T> { data:
    // [first; 4], len: 1 };`, say) -- that gets fixed up to the real concrete instantiation before
    // this substituted copy is checked, so `check_struct_lit`'s own `ensure_instantiated` call
    // cascades into the nested struct/enum instantiation exactly like it would for hand-written
    // concrete source, no special-casing needed here beyond the fix-up itself.
    // `<T: A + B>` -- validates that `arg` (the concrete type substituted in at THIS
    // instantiation, e.g. `apply_damage$Player`'s own `"Player"`) genuinely implements every
    // bound trait, checking direct `impl`s and transitive supertraits (`self.interface_supertraits`,
    // the same table `is_arg_type_mismatch`'s own `&dyn` widening already reads) -- a bound is
    // satisfied through interface inheritance too, same as any other interface-typed position in
    // this language. No-op when `bounds` is empty (an unbounded, or non-generic, fn). See `Ast.hc`'s
    // own `FnDecl.type_bounds` header for the one guarantee this DOESN'T cover (an abstract,
    // once-only body check against the bound, independent of which concrete type ever gets used) --
    // a real, disclosed narrower scope than the retired Kotlin compiler's own bounded generics.
    // `/// @see Target` -- run once, at the very end of checking (once every symbol table is
    // fully populated -- `Codegen.hotc`'s own `compile_program` is the only caller, right before
    // its `has_real_errors` gate), resolving every `@see` target against the real symbol table.
    // "No dead doc links, ever" is a real compile error, not just an aspiration -- see README's
    // own "Structured doc comments" section.
    fn check_doc_sees(&mut self, doc_names: &Vec<String>, docs: &Vec<DocComment>) {
        var i = 0;
        while i < docs.length() {
            let d = docs.get(i);
            var si = 0;
            while si < d.see_targets.length() {
                let target = d.see_targets.get(si);
                if !self.resolve_doc_see_target(target) {
                    self.error("@see target '" + target + "' (in doc comment for '" + doc_names.get(i) + "') doesn't resolve to a known struct, fn, enum, interface, or extern class");
                }
                si = si + 1;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    // Resolves both a bare name (`SomeStruct`, `some_fn`, `SomeEnum`, `SomeInterface`, an extern
    // class alias) and a dotted form (`SomeStruct.field`, `SomeEnum.Variant`) against every
    // relevant registry -- structs/fns/enums each have a separate "still-generic template" table
    // from their "already concrete" one (see `register_struct`/`register_enum`'s own headers), so
    // both get checked for either shape to resolve, whether the target was ever actually
    // instantiated or not.
    fn resolve_doc_see_target(&mut self, target: String) -> Bool {
        let dot = target.indexOf(".");
        if dot >= 0 {
            let base = target.substring(0, dot);
            let member = target.substring(dot + 1, target.length());
            let sfound = self.structs.get(base);
            if let Some { value: si } = sfound {
                var fi = 0;
                while fi < si.field_names.length() {
                    if si.field_names.get(fi) == member { return true; }
                    fi = fi + 1;
                }
            }
            let gsfound = self.generic_templates.get(base);
            if let Some { value: gsi } = gsfound {
                var gfi = 0;
                while gfi < gsi.field_names.length() {
                    if gsi.field_names.get(gfi) == member { return true; }
                    gfi = gfi + 1;
                }
            }
            let efound = self.enum_variants.get(base);
            if let Some { value: variants } = efound {
                var vi = 0;
                while vi < variants.length() {
                    if variants.get(vi) == member { return true; }
                    vi = vi + 1;
                }
            }
            let gefound = self.generic_enum_templates.get(base);
            if let Some { value: getmpl } = gefound {
                var gvi = 0;
                while gvi < getmpl.variants.length() {
                    if getmpl.variants.get(gvi).name == member { return true; }
                    gvi = gvi + 1;
                }
            }
            return false;
        }
        let s1 = self.structs.get(target);
        if let Some { value: s1v } = s1 { return true; }
        let s2 = self.generic_templates.get(target);
        if let Some { value: s2v } = s2 { return true; }
        let f1 = self.fn_sigs.get(target);
        if let Some { value: f1v } = f1 { return true; }
        let f2 = self.generic_fn_templates.get(target);
        if let Some { value: f2v } = f2 { return true; }
        let e1 = self.enum_variants.get(target);
        if let Some { value: e1v } = e1 { return true; }
        let e2 = self.generic_enum_templates.get(target);
        if let Some { value: e2v } = e2 { return true; }
        let i1 = self.interfaces.get(target);
        if let Some { value: i1v } = i1 { return true; }
        let x1 = self.externs.get(target);
        if let Some { value: x1v } = x1 { return true; }
        return false;
    }

    // `expr?` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.TryOp` header for the full design. `self.cur_ret_ty`
    // (already tracked for `Return`/`StructLit` return-position inference, see those own headers)
    // is exactly the "enclosing fn's own declared return type" this needs -- no new tracking.
    fn check_try_op(&mut self, expr: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let result_ty = self.check_expr(expr);
        let rn = ty_name(result_ty);
        if rn == "?" { return TyUnknown; }
        if !is_mono_name(rn) || mono_base(rn) != "Result" || !is_two_part_mono_chk(rn) {
            self.error("'?' operator requires a Result<T, E> value, got '" + rn + "'");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        let t = mono_arg1_of2(rn);
        let e = mono_arg2_of2(rn);
        let fn_rn = self.cur_ret_ty;
        if !is_mono_name(fn_rn) || mono_base(fn_rn) != "Result" || !is_two_part_mono_chk(fn_rn) {
            self.error("'?' operator can only be used inside a fn whose own return type is Result<T, E> -- this fn returns '" + fn_rn + "'");
            return ty_from_name(t);
        }
        let fn_e = mono_arg2_of2(fn_rn);
        if e != fn_e {
            self.error("'?' operator's error type '" + e + "' doesn't match the enclosing fn's own Result error type '" + fn_e + "'");
        }
        return ty_from_name(t);
    }

    fn check_type_param_bounds(&mut self, type_param: String, arg: String, bounds: Vec<String>) {
        if bounds.length() == 0 { return; }
        var implemented = empty_str_vec2();
        let impl_found = self.struct_interfaces.get(arg);
        if let Some { value: il } = impl_found { implemented = il; }
        var bi = 0;
        while bi < bounds.length() {
            let bound = bounds.get(bi);
            var satisfied = false;
            var ii = 0;
            while ii < implemented.length() {
                let iface = implemented.get(ii);
                if iface == bound { satisfied = true; }
                let supers_found = self.interface_supertraits.get(iface);
                if let Some { value: supers } = supers_found {
                    var si = 0;
                    while si < supers.length() {
                        if supers.get(si) == bound { satisfied = true; }
                        si = si + 1;
                    }
                }
                ii = ii + 1;
            }
            if !satisfied {
                self.error("'" + arg + "' for type param '" + type_param + "' doesn't implement " + bound);
            }
            bi = bi + 1;
        }
    }

    fn ensure_fn_instantiated(&mut self, name: String) {
        if !is_mono_name(name) { return; }
        let already = self.fn_sigs.get(name);
        var exists = false;
        if let Some { value: s } = already { exists = true; }
        if exists { return; }
        let base = mono_base(name);
        let arg = mono_arg(name);
        let tmpl_found = self.generic_fn_templates.get(base);
        if let Some { value: tmpl } = tmpl_found {
            var found_tp = "";
            if let Some { value: tp } = tmpl.type_param { found_tp = tp; }
            // `fn hash_map2_new<K: Hashable, V>(...)` -- a SECOND, independently-bounded type
            // param, mirroring `ensure_instantiated`'s own struct branch (see `Ast.hc`'s own
            // `FnDecl.type_param2` header for why this exists at all). `mono_arg(name)` alone is
            // right for a one-param template, wrong for a two-param one -- `mono_arg1_of2`/
            // `mono_arg2_of2` split "hash_map2_new$ItemStack$Int" into "ItemStack"/"Int"
            // separately, ONLY reached when `tmpl.type_param2` is actually `Some`.
            var has_tp2fn = false;
            var tp2fn = "";
            if let Some { value: tp2fnv } = tmpl.type_param2 { has_tp2fn = true; tp2fn = tp2fnv; }
            var arg2fn = "";
            var real_argfn = arg;
            if has_tp2fn {
                real_argfn = mono_arg1_of2(name);
                arg2fn = mono_arg2_of2(name);
            }
            self.check_type_param_bounds(found_tp, real_argfn, tmpl.type_bounds);
            if has_tp2fn {
                self.check_type_param_bounds(tp2fn, arg2fn, tmpl.type_bounds2);
            }
            // Builds `new_param_tys` alongside `new_params` in the same pass -- rather than
            // deriving it afterward via `param_types_of(new_params)`, which would MOVE
            // `new_params` (it takes `Vec<Param>` by value) before `mono_decl` below needs it
            // again.
            var new_params = empty_param_vec3();
            var new_param_tys = empty_ty_vec();
            var pi = 0;
            while pi < tmpl.params.length() {
                let p = tmpl.params.get(pi);
                var pty = substitute_field_type(p.type_name, found_tp, real_argfn);
                if has_tp2fn {
                    pty = substitute_field_type(pty, tp2fn, arg2fn);
                }
                new_params.push(Param { name: p.name, type_name: pty, is_ref: p.is_ref, is_mut_ref: p.is_mut_ref });
                new_param_tys.push(ty_from_name(pty));
                pi = pi + 1;
            }
            let ret_ty_str = opt_string_or2(tmpl.ret_type, "Int");
            var new_ret = substitute_field_type(ret_ty_str, found_tp, real_argfn);
            if has_tp2fn {
                new_ret = substitute_field_type(new_ret, tp2fn, arg2fn);
            }
            self.fn_sigs.register(name, FnSig { param_types: new_param_tys, ret_type: ty_from_name(new_ret) });
            self.fn_mono_needed.push(name);
            var substituted_body = subst_stmt_list(tmpl.body, found_tp, real_argfn);
            if has_tp2fn {
                substituted_body = subst_stmt_list(substituted_body, tp2fn, arg2fn);
            }
            var mono_decl = FnDecl { name: name, params: new_params, ret_type: some_string3(new_ret), body: substituted_body, type_param: none_string3(), type_bounds: empty_str_vec2(), self_is_mut: false, type_param2: none_string3(), type_bounds2: empty_str_vec2() };
            // Real bug found building this: `ensure_fn_instantiated` can fire from the MIDDLE of
            // checking a totally different function's body (a call site inside it just triggered
            // the first-ever use of this instantiation) -- but `check_fn` unconditionally resets
            // `self.vars` to a fresh scope for whatever it's given, so calling it here without
            // saving/restoring first would wipe out every local the *caller's* own function had
            // already bound (a `let n = identity(37);` earlier in `combo`'s body, say), surfacing
            // as spurious "unknown variable" errors for names that were very much still in scope.
            // Save/restore around the nested check, same as any other re-entrant use of shared
            // mutable state.
            let saved_vars = self.vars;
            let saved_scopes = self.var_scopes;
            let saved_moved = self.moved;
            let saved_moved_scopes = self.moved_scopes;
            let saved_mut_capable = self.mut_capable;
            let saved_mut_capable_scopes = self.mut_capable_scopes;
            let saved_ret_ty = self.cur_ret_ty;
            self.check_fn(mono_decl);
            self.vars = saved_vars;
            self.var_scopes = saved_scopes;
            self.moved = saved_moved;
            self.moved_scopes = saved_moved_scopes;
            self.mut_capable = saved_mut_capable;
            self.mut_capable_scopes = saved_mut_capable_scopes;
            self.cur_ret_ty = saved_ret_ty;
        }
    }

    // Called once per `EnumDecl` (mirrors `register_struct`). Registers the *union* of every
    // variant's fields under the enum's own name (a real, disclosed simplification -- field
    // lookups and `StructLit` validation don't distinguish which variant a field actually belongs
    // to, only whether it exists on *some* variant of this enum; see this file's own header), and
    // records each variant name -> this enum's name in `variant_owner` (bare lookup, unqualified
    // construction sites only) and `variant_owner_q` (qualified "Owner#Variant" existence check,
    // `check_match` reads this one). A *generic* enum (`type_param` is `Some`) goes into
    // `generic_enum_templates` instead, same as `register_struct`'s own generic branch -- it isn't
    // directly usable until `ensure_instantiated` monomorphizes a concrete `Base$Arg` name for it.
    fn register_enum(&mut self, name: String, type_param: Option<String>, variants: Vec<EnumVariant>, type_param2: Option<String>) {
        // A single `if let ... { move variants; return; }` followed by code that also uses
        // `variants` trips the move-checker's conservative branch-merge behavior even though the
        // early `return` makes the two paths genuinely exclusive (it doesn't reason about
        // reachability past a `return`) -- restructured as one exhaustive `if`/`else`, each branch
        // using `variants` exactly once, mirroring `register_struct`'s own shape.
        var is_generic = false;
        var tp_val = "";
        if let Some { value: tp } = type_param {
            is_generic = true;
            tp_val = tp;
        }
        if is_generic {
            var gvi = 0;
            while gvi < variants.length() {
                let gv = variants.get(gvi);
                self.generic_variant_owner.register(gv.name, name);
                gvi = gvi + 1;
            }
            self.generic_enum_templates.register(name, GenericEnumTemplate { type_param: tp_val, variants: variants, type_param2: type_param2 });
        } else {
            var names = empty_str_vec2();
            var types = empty_str_vec2();
            var variant_names = empty_str_vec2();
            var vi = 0;
            while vi < variants.length() {
                let v = variants.get(vi);
                self.variant_owner.register(v.name, name);
                self.variant_owner_q.register(name + "#" + v.name, true);
                variant_names.push(v.name);
                var v_names = empty_str_vec2();
                var v_types = empty_str_vec2();
                var fi = 0;
                while fi < v.fields.length() {
                    let f = v.fields.get(fi);
                    names.push(f.name);
                    types.push(f.type_name);
                    v_names.push(f.name);
                    v_types.push(f.type_name);
                    fi = fi + 1;
                }
                // Keyed "OwnerName#VariantName" (mirrors `variant_owner_q`), NOT bare `v.name` --
                // a bare key would collide across multiple concrete instantiations of the same
                // GENERIC enum (`Option$Expr#Some`'s `value: Expr` vs `Option$String#Some`'s
                // `value: String`, both registered under this same enum's own template, each via
                // its own `ensure_instantiated` -> `register_enum(mono_name, ...)` call), the
                // exact same collision already disclosed for `variant_owner` itself (see
                // `build_program_info`'s own header in `Codegen.hotc`) -- found self-compiling
                // `Parser.hotc`, whose real source constructs `Option<T>` at several different T's.
                self.variant_field_info.register(name + "#" + v.name, StructInfo { field_names: v_names, field_types: v_types });
                vi = vi + 1;
            }
            self.structs.register(name, StructInfo { field_names: names, field_types: types });
            self.enum_variants.register(name, variant_names);

            // `Codegen.hotc`'s `gen_enum` emits a real `valueOf(String) -> Name` static method and
            // a real `.name() -> String` instance method onto this enum's own class, but ONLY when
            // it has "has_singleton" (at least one zero-field variant) -- mirror that same
            // condition here so HC's OWN source can call `Name::valueOf(...)`/`x.name()` through
            // the normal `static_methods`/`methods` registries too, not just real Java callers
            // (who could already call them since they're genuine JVM methods, just invisible to
            // this checker until now).
            var has_singleton2 = false;
            var hsi2 = 0;
            while hsi2 < variants.length() {
                if variants.get(hsi2).fields.length() == 0 { has_singleton2 = true; }
                hsi2 = hsi2 + 1;
            }
            if has_singleton2 {
                var vof_params = empty_ty_vec();
                vof_params.push(ty_from_name("String"));
                self.static_methods.register(name + "#valueOf", FnSig { param_types: vof_params, ret_type: ty_from_name(name) });
                self.methods.register(name + "#name", FnSig { param_types: empty_ty_vec(), ret_type: ty_from_name("String") });
            }
        }
    }

    // A LOCAL (`self.vars`, reset fresh per fn/method) is checked first, so a local can still
    // shadow a top-level `static` of the same name -- only falls back to `self.statics` (global,
    // never reset) when there's no local by this name. See `Ast.hc`'s own `StaticDecl` header.
    fn check_ident(&mut self, name: String) -> Ty {
        let found = self.lookup_var(name);
        if let Some { value: t } = found {
            // The ONE central place a bare identifier read goes through -- covers a direct value
            // use, a field access's own `obj`, a method-call receiver, an argument, everything --
            // so this single check is enough to catch "used `x` after it was moved" for every
            // shape that use can take, without needing a separate check at each call site. See
            // `TyChecker.moved`'s own header for the full move-checking design.
            if self.is_moved(name) {
                self.error("use of moved value '" + name + "'");
            }
            return t;
        }
        let static_found = self.statics.get(name);
        if let Some { value: st } = static_found {
            return st;
        }
        // Neither a local nor a static -- a BARE fieldless-enum-variant reference used as an
        // ordinary value expression (real `Lexer.hotc`/`Parser.hotc` usage: `Token { kind: FN }`,
        // `kind == RETURN`, treating a fieldless enum's variants like plain named constants; also
        // covers real `Parser.hotc`'s own bare `return None;`, a GENERIC fieldless variant --
        // `self.cur_ret_ty` supplies the missing type argument, same as `check_expr`'s own
        // `StructLit` dispatch). Delegates straight to `check_struct_lit` with empty field lists --
        // exactly what `Name {}` would check, reusing that logic rather than duplicating it (and
        // inheriting its existing, disclosed imprecision: neither path verifies every declared
        // field was actually supplied, so this doesn't introduce a new gap, just extends an old
        // one to a new syntax form).
        let variant_found = self.variant_owner.get(name);
        let generic_variant_found = self.generic_variant_owner.get(name);
        var is_known_variant = false;
        if let Some { value: owner } = variant_found { is_known_variant = true; }
        if let Some { value: gowner } = generic_variant_found { is_known_variant = true; }
        if is_known_variant {
            return self.check_struct_lit(name, empty_str_vec2(), empty_expr_vec3(), none_string3(), some_string3(self.cur_ret_ty));
        }
        self.error("unknown variable '" + name + "'");
        return TyUnknown;
    }

    fn check_binary(&mut self, op: String, left: &Expr, right: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let lt = self.check_expr(left);
        let rt = self.check_expr(right);
        var ln = ty_name(lt);
        var rn = ty_name(rt);
        // Operator overloading -- a plain struct's own `impl` method named per a fixed
        // convention (mirroring Rust's own operator-trait method names -- `add`/`sub`/`mul`/
        // `div`/`rem`/`eq`, NOT a real trait/interface declaration this checker has to verify
        // separately) lets `+`/`-`/`*`/`/`/`%`/`==`/`!=` dispatch to a real user-defined method
        // instead of requiring `Int`/`Long`/`Float`/`Double`. See README's own "Operator
        // overloading" section. Checked FIRST, before any built-in numeric/string path below --
        // safe to do unconditionally, since a real primitive's own type name (`"Int"`, ...) can
        // never collide with a registered struct method key (`self.methods` is only ever
        // populated from a real `impl StructName { ... }` block).
        let opm = operator_method_name(op);
        if opm != "" {
            let mkey = ln + "#" + opm;
            let m_found = self.methods.get(mkey);
            if let Some { value: sig } = m_found {
                if sig.param_types.length() != 1 {
                    self.error("operator method '" + opm + "' on '" + ln + "' must take exactly 1 parameter");
                    return TyUnknown;
                }
                let expected = sig.param_types.get(0);
                let en = ty_name(expected);
                if en != "?" && rn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(en, rn) {
                    self.error("'" + op + "' on '" + ln + "' expects " + en + ", got " + rn);
                }
                if op == "==" || op == "!=" {
                    return TyBool;
                }
                return sig.ret_type;
            }
        }
        // `s + "..."` / `"..." + s` where `s: String?` hasn't been narrowed -- same "possibly-null
        // value used without a null check" enforcement `check_field_access_by_type`/
        // `check_method_call` already give a struct/extern-class receiver, extended to native
        // `String?` (see README's own "Nullable types" section on why `String?` needs its own gate
        // at each of `String`'s own special-cased operations, rather than reusing one shared
        // check). Reported once, then treated as its underlying `String` so the ordinary
        // `String`+`String` case right below still resolves normally instead of ALSO reporting a
        // confusing "'+' needs matching Int, Long, Float, or Double operands" on top.
        if op == "+" {
            if is_nullable_type_name(ln) && strip_nullable(ln) == "String" {
                self.error("'+' operand is a possibly-null String (" + ln + ") used without a null check");
                ln = strip_nullable(ln);
            }
            if is_nullable_type_name(rn) && strip_nullable(rn) == "String" {
                self.error("'+' operand is a possibly-null String (" + rn + ") used without a null check");
                rn = strip_nullable(rn);
            }
        }
        if op == "+" && ln == "String" && rn == "String" {
            return TyStr;
        } else if op == "+" || op == "-" || op == "*" || op == "/" || op == "%" {
            if ln == "?" || rn == "?" {
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            // A mixed numeric pair widens to the "bigger" side (`Int`/`Long` up to `Float`/
            // `Double`, `Float` up to `Double`) rather than requiring an exact match -- real gap
            // found writing ordinary game-code arithmetic (`radius * 2`, `count as Int` mixed with
            // a `Double` delta-time, ...), where forcing an explicit `as Double` on every bare Int
            // literal/local mixed into float math was real, constant friction. `Int`/`Long`
            // together is deliberately still NOT widened (a real, disclosed scope cut, unchanged
            // from before this pass: `Codegen.hotc`'s own `gen_binary` would need real sign-
            // extension bytecode for that pair too, not just a symmetric "which side is bigger"
            // rule, and no real caller needed it this round). See `numeric_widen_result`'s own
            // header for the exact rule, shared with `Codegen.hotc`'s own identical `_cg` twin.
            let widened = numeric_widen_result(ln, rn);
            if widened != "" {
                return ty_from_name(widened);
            }
            self.error("'" + op + "' needs matching Int, Long, Float, or Double operands, got " + ln + " and " + rn);
            return TyUnknown;
        } else if op == "&&" || op == "||" {
            if ln == "Bool" && rn == "Bool" {
                return TyBool;
            } else if ln == "?" || rn == "?" {
                return TyUnknown;
            } else {
                self.error("'" + op + "' needs Bool operands, got " + ln + " and " + rn);
                return TyUnknown;
            }
        } else if op == "&" || op == "|" || op == "^" {
            // Real bitwise (`Int`/`Long`) AND/OR/XOR -- distinct from `&&`/`||` right above (those
            // short-circuit and are Bool-only; these never short-circuit and work on numeric
            // operands, matching real Java `&`/`|`/`^` semantics, including its own less-common but
            // real `Bool & Bool`/`Bool | Bool`/`Bool ^ Bool` non-short-circuiting logical form --
            // e.g. hex color/flag manipulation, `Integer.MIN_VALUE`-safe masking `Math.abs`/
            // division can't express. See `Codegen.hotc`'s own `gen_binary` for the matching
            // `IAND`/`IOR`/`IXOR`/`LAND`/`LOR`/`LXOR` codegen half of this.
            if ln == "Int" && rn == "Int" {
                return TyInt;
            } else if ln == "Long" && rn == "Long" {
                return TyLong;
            } else if ln == "Bool" && rn == "Bool" {
                return TyBool;
            } else if ln == "?" || rn == "?" {
                return TyUnknown;
            } else {
                self.error("'" + op + "' needs matching Int, Long, or Bool operands, got " + ln + " and " + rn);
                return TyUnknown;
            }
        } else if op == "<<" || op == ">>" || op == ">>>" {
            // Real bitwise shift -- Java semantics: the shift AMOUNT (right operand) is always
            // `Int`, regardless of the left operand's own type, and the RESULT's type matches only
            // the left operand (`Int` or `Long` -- no `Bool` shift form exists, unlike `&`/`|`/`^`
            // right above). See `Codegen.hotc`'s own `gen_binary` for the matching `ISHL`/`ISHR`/
            // `IUSHR`/`LSHL`/`LSHR`/`LUSHR` codegen half of this.
            if rn != "Int" && rn != "?" {
                self.error("'" + op + "' needs an Int shift amount, got " + rn);
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            if ln == "Int" {
                return TyInt;
            } else if ln == "Long" {
                return TyLong;
            } else if ln == "?" {
                return TyUnknown;
            } else {
                self.error("'" + op + "' needs an Int or Long left operand, got " + ln);
                return TyUnknown;
            }
        } else if op == "<" || op == "<=" || op == ">" || op == ">=" {
            if ln == "?" || rn == "?" {
                return TyBool;
            }
            // Same widening `numeric_widen_result` gives the arithmetic ops above -- comparing a
            // bare `Int` loop counter against a `Double` threshold is exactly as common as mixing
            // them in arithmetic.
            let widened2 = numeric_widen_result(ln, rn);
            if widened2 != "" {
                return TyBool;
            }
            self.error("'" + op + "' needs matching Int, Long, Float, or Double operands, got " + ln + " and " + rn);
            return TyBool;
        } else {
            // "==" / "!=" -- always produce Bool; real structural-vs-reference-equality
            // subtleties (see IDEAS.md) are left to codegen, not this checker's concern.
            return TyBool;
        }
    }

    fn check_unary(&mut self, op: String, inner: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let it = self.check_expr(inner);
        let iname = ty_name(it);
        if op == "!" {
            if iname == "Bool" || iname == "?" {
                return TyBool;
            } else {
                self.error("'!' needs a Bool operand, got " + iname);
                return TyBool;
            }
        } else {
            // Real gap found alongside the `Float`/`Double` port: this used to accept ONLY `Int`
            // (always returning `TyInt` even for a `Long` operand) -- `Codegen.hotc`'s own
            // `gen_unary` matched that with an unconditional `INEG`, silently wrong for anything
            // else. Both fixed together: negation now works for every numeric type this subset has.
            if iname == "?" {
                return TyUnknown;
            } else if iname == "Int" || iname == "Long" || iname == "Float" || iname == "Double" || iname == "Char" || iname == "Byte" {
                // `it` was already consumed by `ty_name(it)` above (`Ty` isn't `Copy` -- see
                // `Types.hotc`'s own `ty_name` header) -- rebuilding from the `String` (which IS
                // `Copy`) instead of trying to reuse `it` directly. `ty_from_name` would build a
                // bogus `TyStruct { name: "?" }` for the unknown case, hence the branch above.
                return ty_from_name(iname);
            } else {
                // Operator overloading, unary half -- see `check_binary`'s own header for the
                // full design; `neg` is the one unary member of that same fixed naming
                // convention, a zero-param method returning `Self` (typically).
                let neg_found = self.methods.get(iname + "#neg");
                if let Some { value: sig } = neg_found {
                    if sig.param_types.length() != 0 {
                        self.error("operator method 'neg' on '" + iname + "' must take no parameters");
                        return TyUnknown;
                    }
                    return sig.ret_type;
                }
                self.error("unary '-' needs an Int, Long, Float, or Double operand, got " + iname);
                return TyInt;
            }
        }
    }

    // Checks a lambda literal's body against `target_type` -- the DECLARED param type of whichever
    // call argument position it was written in (always a `"dyn InterfaceName"`-shaped string, the
    // only place a bare lambda literal is legal at all, see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.Lambda` header).
    // Requires the target to be a single-abstract-method interface (no ambiguity about which method
    // the lambda's body implements) and the lambda's own param count to match that method's real
    // param count exactly -- both real, disclosed scope cuts (no bare method-reference syntax, no
    // inferring which overload of a multi-method interface is meant). Binds each lambda param's
    // type from the method's own declared param types (never inferred any other way -- lambda
    // params carry no type annotation of their own), then checks the body IN THE ENCLOSING SCOPE
    // (not a fresh one -- a lambda is a real closure over its enclosing locals, unlike a top-level
    // fn body) with those params added on top, save/restoring `self.vars` around it exactly the way
    // `ensure_fn_instantiated` already does for its own nested re-entrant check.
    fn check_lambda(&mut self, params: Vec<String>, body: Expr, target_type: String) -> Ty {
        if !is_dyn_type_name_chk(target_type) {
            self.error("lambda literal's target type '" + target_type + "' isn't an interface (expected a single-method 'extern interface')");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        let iface_name = dyn_interface_name_chk(target_type);
        let iface_found = self.interfaces.get(iface_name);
        if let Some { value: iface_methods } = iface_found {
            if iface_methods.length() != 1 {
                self.error("lambda literal's target interface '" + iface_name + "' must have exactly one method, has " + JString::valueOf(iface_methods.length()));
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            let m = iface_methods.get(0);
            if params.length() != m.params.length() {
                self.error("lambda literal has " + JString::valueOf(params.length()) + " param(s), '" + iface_name + "." + m.name + "' needs " + JString::valueOf(m.params.length()));
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            // A real, popped scope (not a save/mutate/restore of `self.vars` directly) -- params
            // registered here must vanish once the lambda body's been checked, not linger in the
            // enclosing scope afterward.
            self.push_scope();
            var pi = 0;
            while pi < params.length() {
                self.vars.register(params.get(pi), ty_from_name(m.params.get(pi).type_name));
                pi = pi + 1;
            }
            let body_ty = self.check_expr(body);
            self.pop_scope();
            let body_tn = ty_name(body_ty);
            if m.ret_type != "Unit" && body_tn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(m.ret_type, body_tn) {
                self.error("lambda body for '" + iface_name + "." + m.name + "' expects " + m.ret_type + ", got " + body_tn);
            }
            return ty_from_name(target_type);
        }
        self.error("unknown interface '" + iface_name + "' (lambda literal's target)");
        return TyUnknown;
    }

    // `Type::method` used directly as a lambda-typed argument (no `|params| Type::method(params)`
    // wrapper) -- see README's own "Lambda literals" section for why this was previously scoped
    // out ("wrap the call instead"). Reuses `check_lambda` entirely: builds a synthetic body
    // (`StaticCall { type_name, method, args: [synthesized idents] }`, one per the target
    // interface's own single abstract method param) and hands it to `check_lambda` exactly as if
    // the user had written `|mref_p0, ...| type_name::method(mref_p0, ...)` themselves -- real
    // resolution failures (unknown method, wrong arity, wrong return type) surface through the
    // exact same `check_static_call` error paths a hand-written call would hit, no duplicated
    // validation logic. Covers both `extern class` static methods and plain struct static methods
    // uniformly, since `check_static_call` (reached via `check_expr` inside `check_lambda`) already
    // tries extern first, struct static as the fallback -- same as any other `Type::method(...)`
    // call site.
    fn check_method_ref(&mut self, type_name: String, method: String, target_type: String) -> Ty {
        if !is_dyn_type_name_chk(target_type) {
            self.error("method reference's target type '" + target_type + "' isn't an interface (expected a single-method 'extern interface')");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        let iface_name = dyn_interface_name_chk(target_type);
        let iface_found = self.interfaces.get(iface_name);
        if let Some { value: iface_methods } = iface_found {
            if iface_methods.length() != 1 {
                self.error("method reference's target interface '" + iface_name + "' must have exactly one method, has " + JString::valueOf(iface_methods.length()));
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            let m = iface_methods.get(0);
            var synth_params = empty_str_vec2();
            var synth_args = empty_expr_vec3();
            var pi = 0;
            while pi < m.params.length() {
                let pname = "mref_p" + JString::valueOf(pi);
                synth_params.push(pname);
                synth_args.push(Ident { name: pname });
                pi = pi + 1;
            }
            let body = StaticCall { type_name: type_name, method: method, args: synth_args };
            return self.check_lambda(synth_params, body, target_type);
        }
        self.error("unknown interface '" + iface_name + "' (method reference's target)");
        return TyUnknown;
    }

    // `check_arg_list`'s own lambda-aware sibling: checks each arg via `check_expr` as usual,
    // EXCEPT a `Lambda` literal (which `check_expr` alone would always reject -- it has no type
    // without knowing its target first, see `Expr.Lambda`'s own header) gets a `TyUnknown`
    // placeholder here instead, filled in for real once the call's own target signature is known
    // (see `check_call`/`check_method_call`/`check_static_call`, all three of which need this same
    // "resolve param types first, THEN check a lambda arg against the right one" ordering -- unlike
    // every other arg shape, which can check itself with no outside context at all).
    fn check_args_lambda_aware(&mut self, args: &Vec<Expr>) -> Vec<Ty> {
        var out = empty_ty_vec();
        var i = 0;
        while i < args.length() {
            let a = args.get(i);
            match a {
                Lambda { params, body } => { out.push(TyUnknown); }
                // `Type::method` -- ambiguous at this point between a genuine static FIELD read
                // (already resolvable now, no target-type context needed) and a method reference
                // (deferred, same reason `Lambda` is, see `check_method_ref`'s own header) --
                // disambiguated purely by whether `type_name` has a REAL declared field named
                // `field`; only a field-shaped miss defers.
                ExternStaticFieldGet { type_name, field } => {
                    let mref_field_found = self.extern_fields.get(type_name + "#" + field);
                    var mref_is_field = false;
                    if let Some { value: mfv } = mref_field_found { mref_is_field = true; }
                    if mref_is_field {
                        out.push(self.check_expr(a));
                    } else {
                        out.push(TyUnknown);
                    }
                }
                _ => { out.push(self.check_expr(a)); }
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }

    // Backfills `arg_tys` in place for every `Lambda`-shaped arg, now that `param_types` (the
    // callee's real, resolved signature) is known -- called right after arity/signature resolution
    // succeeds in `check_call`/`check_method_call`/`check_static_call`, before their own normal
    // per-arg mismatch-check loop runs (which then sees a fully real `arg_tys`, lambda positions
    // included, same as if `check_expr` had handled them directly).
    fn backfill_lambda_args(&mut self, args: &Vec<Expr>, param_types: &Vec<String>, arg_tys: &mut Vec<Ty>) {
        var i = 0;
        while i < args.length() && i < param_types.length() {
            let a = args.get(i);
            match a {
                Lambda { params, body } => {
                    let lt = self.check_lambda(params, body, param_types.get(i));
                    arg_tys.set(i, lt);
                }
                // Only reached for a REAL deferred method-ref candidate -- `check_args_lambda_aware`
                // already resolved a genuine field-get eagerly, so this re-checks the exact same
                // "is it a real field" condition purely to know whether to act, not to re-decide
                // anything.
                ExternStaticFieldGet { type_name, field } => {
                    let mref_field_found2 = self.extern_fields.get(type_name + "#" + field);
                    var mref_is_field2 = false;
                    if let Some { value: mfv2 } = mref_field_found2 { mref_is_field2 = true; }
                    if !mref_is_field2 {
                        let mrt = self.check_method_ref(type_name, field, param_types.get(i));
                        arg_tys.set(i, mrt);
                    }
                }
                _ => {}
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    // `backfill_lambda_args`'s own `Vec<Ty>` sibling -- `check_call`'s own `FnSig.param_types` is
    // `Vec<Ty>` (a plain top-level fn's declared params, resolved through `ty_from_name` at
    // registration time), unlike an extern method's `Vec<String>` (see `register_extern`'s own
    // header) -- both files' declared-type storage predates lambdas needing to read it back out,
    // so this converts rather than changing either's own shape.
    fn backfill_lambda_args_ty(&mut self, args: &Vec<Expr>, param_types: &Vec<Ty>, arg_tys: &mut Vec<Ty>) {
        var i = 0;
        while i < args.length() && i < param_types.length() {
            let a = args.get(i);
            match a {
                Lambda { params, body } => {
                    let lt = self.check_lambda(params, body, ty_name(param_types.get(i)));
                    arg_tys.set(i, lt);
                }
                ExternStaticFieldGet { type_name, field } => {
                    let mref_field_found3 = self.extern_fields.get(type_name + "#" + field);
                    var mref_is_field3 = false;
                    if let Some { value: mfv3 } = mref_field_found3 { mref_is_field3 = true; }
                    if !mref_is_field3 {
                        let mrt2 = self.check_method_ref(type_name, field, ty_name(param_types.get(i)));
                        arg_tys.set(i, mrt2);
                    }
                }
                _ => {}
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    // `createEnemy(health: 100, damage: 20)` -- reorders a fully-named argument list back to
    // ordinary positional order by looking up `callee`'s own declared param names
    // (`self.fn_param_names`, registered once per top-level fn in `Codegen.hotc`'s own
    // `compile_program`), so every downstream check (`check_call`'s own arity/type-mismatch loop)
    // runs completely unchanged against the reordered list -- see README's own "Named arguments"
    // section. A no-op (returns `args` untouched) when no `NamedArg` is present at all, so an
    // ordinary positional call pays nothing extra. Scoped to plain top-level fn calls only this
    // pass: mixing named and positional in one call, or naming an argument to something that
    // isn't a known plain top-level fn, is a real error, not silently reinterpreted.
    fn resolve_named_args_chk(&mut self, callee: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Vec<Expr> {
        var has_named = false;
        var has_positional = false;
        var i = 0;
        while i < args.length() {
            match args.get(i) {
                NamedArg { name, value } => { has_named = true; }
                _ => { has_positional = true; }
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if !has_named { return args; }
        if has_positional {
            self.error("mixing named and positional arguments isn't supported in a call to '" + callee + "'");
            return args;
        }
        let names_found = self.fn_param_names.get(callee);
        var declared = empty_str_vec2();
        var known = false;
        if let Some { value: dn } = names_found { declared = dn; known = true; }
        if !known {
            self.error("named arguments used in a call to '" + callee + "', but only plain top-level fn calls support named arguments this pass");
            return args;
        }
        if declared.length() != args.length() {
            self.error("named-argument call to '" + callee + "' expects " + JString::valueOf(declared.length()) + " arg(s), got " + JString::valueOf(args.length()));
            return args;
        }
        var reordered = empty_expr_vec3();
        var di = 0;
        while di < declared.length() {
            let pname = declared.get(di);
            var found_val = false;
            var val = IntLit { value: 0 };
            var ai = 0;
            while ai < args.length() {
                match args.get(ai) {
                    NamedArg { name, value } => {
                        if name == pname { found_val = true; val = value; }
                    }
                    _ => {}
                }
                ai = ai + 1;
            }
            if !found_val {
                self.error("named-argument call to '" + callee + "' is missing argument '" + pname + "'");
            }
            reordered.push(val);
            di = di + 1;
        }
        return reordered;
    }

    fn check_call(&mut self, callee: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        let args = self.resolve_named_args_chk(callee, args);
        var arg_tys = self.check_args_lambda_aware(args);
        let found = self.fn_sigs.get(callee);
        if let Some { value: sig } = found {
            let expected_count = sig.param_types.length();
            let got_count = arg_tys.length();
            if expected_count != got_count {
                self.error("'" + callee + "' expects " + JString::valueOf(expected_count) + " args, got " + JString::valueOf(got_count));
            } else {
                self.backfill_lambda_args_ty(&args, &sig.param_types, &mut arg_tys);
                // Move-check: a by-value param (no leading `&`/`&mut`, see `Ast.hc`'s own `Param.
                // is_ref` header) moves whichever bare, non-Copy local its argument names -- an
                // is_ref param never does. Missing/short `is_ref` data (a generic instantiation
                // this checker doesn't bother registering, see `TyChecker.fn_param_is_ref`'s own
                // header) means every arg here is trusted as a borrow, never flagged as a move.
                let is_ref_found = self.fn_param_is_ref.get(callee);
                var is_ref_list = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                if let Some { value: irl } = is_ref_found { is_ref_list = irl; }
                let is_mutref_found = self.fn_param_is_mut_ref.get(callee);
                var is_mutref_list = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                if let Some { value: imrl } = is_mutref_found { is_mutref_list = imrl; }
                var borrow_names = empty_str_vec2();
                var borrow_is_mut = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                var borrow_present = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                var i = 0;
                while i < got_count {
                    let expected = sig.param_types.get(i);
                    let got = arg_tys.get(i);
                    let en = ty_name(expected);
                    let gn = ty_name(got);
                    if en != "?" && gn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(en, gn) {
                        self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + callee + "' expects " + en + ", got " + gn);
                    } else if is_ranged_int_type_chk(en) && gn == "Int" {
                        // `take_damage(150)` -- a literal argument against a ranged-Int param is
                        // provable at COMPILE time (same split `check_struct_lit`'s own matching
                        // branch already established); anything else (a variable, a computed
                        // expression) is trusted here and gets a real RUNTIME check instead,
                        // inserted by `Codegen.hotc`'s own mirrored call-argument codegen right
                        // after each argument value is pushed for the call. Scoped to PLAIN
                        // top-level fn calls only this pass -- method/static calls aren't wired
                        // up with the runtime half yet, even though `is_arg_type_mismatch`'s own
                        // widened compatibility rule (see its own header) already makes them
                        // compile cleanly.
                        match args.get(i) {
                            IntLit { value } => {
                                let alo = ranged_int_lo_chk(en);
                                let ahi = ranged_int_hi_chk(en);
                                let aincl = ranged_int_inclusive_chk(en);
                                var aout = value < alo;
                                if aincl {
                                    if value > ahi { aout = true; }
                                } else {
                                    if value >= ahi { aout = true; }
                                }
                                if aout {
                                    self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + callee + "' = " + JString::valueOf(value) + " is out of the declared range " + en);
                                }
                            }
                            _ => {}
                        }
                    }
                    var arg_is_ref = true;
                    if i < is_ref_list.length() { arg_is_ref = is_ref_list.get(i); }
                    if !arg_is_ref {
                        let ai2 = args.get(i);
                        self.move_check_consumed(&ai2, gn);
                    }
                    var arg_is_mut_ref = false;
                    if i < is_mutref_list.length() { arg_is_mut_ref = is_mutref_list.get(i); }
                    let ai3 = args.get(i);
                    match ai3 {
                        Ident { name } => {
                            borrow_names.push(name);
                            borrow_is_mut.push(arg_is_mut_ref);
                            borrow_present.push(true);
                        }
                        _ => {
                            borrow_names.push("");
                            borrow_is_mut.push(false);
                            borrow_present.push(false);
                        }
                    }
                    i = i + 1;
                }
                self.check_borrow_rules(borrow_names, borrow_is_mut, borrow_present, "call to '" + callee + "'");
            }
            return sig.ret_type;
        } else {
            let gtmpl_found = self.generic_fn_templates.get(callee);
            if let Some { value: gtmpl } = gtmpl_found {
                return self.check_generic_call(callee, gtmpl, arg_tys);
            }
            // Unknown callee (e.g. a real builtin like `print` this phase's symbol table
            // doesn't know about) -- trust it, same spirit as `extern class`'s own "no
            // verification, trust the call site" honesty elsewhere in this compiler.
            return TyUnknown;
        }
    }

    // Infers a generic top-level fn's type argument from whichever argument's corresponding
    // declared param type is EXACTLY the bare type parameter (`fn identity<T>(x: T)` -- `x`'s
    // declared type is literally `"T"`, so the arg passed for `x` gives the concrete type
    // directly) -- a real, disclosed scope cut: doesn't infer through `"[T]"` or a nested generic
    // argument, only this one bare-match rule (see `Ast.hc`'s own `FnDecl` header). Builds the
    // mono name (`"identity$Int"`), `ensure_fn_instantiated`s it, and returns its real (now-
    // concrete) return type.
    // Runs the SAME per-param inference scan `check_generic_call` used to do inline for its one
    // and only type param, now factored out so it can be run a SECOND, independent time for `fn
    // hash_map2_new<K: Hashable, V>(...)`-style two-param generics (see `Ast.hc`'s own `FnDecl.
    // type_param2` header) -- `target_tp` is whichever of the two the caller wants inferred THIS
    // call, entirely independent of the other. Returns `"?"` (the same sentinel `ty_name`'s own
    // catch-all already uses) on failure to infer.
    fn infer_one_type_param(&self, params: &Vec<Param>, arg_tys: &Vec<Ty>, target_tp: String) -> String {
        var inferred = "";
        var found_infer = false;
        var i = 0;
        while i < params.length() {
            let p = params.get(i);
            if p.type_name == target_tp && i < arg_tys.length() {
                inferred = ty_name(arg_tys.get(i));
                found_infer = true;
            } else if is_mono_name(p.type_name) && mono_arg(p.type_name) == target_tp && i < arg_tys.length() {
                // `b: Box<T>` -- the param's OWN declared type isn't the bare type param (that's
                // the case right above), it's a generic struct/enum PARAMETERIZED BY it
                // (`type_name_ref` parses `Box<T>` to the mono-shaped string "Box$T", same as any
                // other `Base<Arg>` -- "T" here just happens to be this fn's own type param, not a
                // concrete type). A real, reported gap: `fn unwrap<T>(b: Box<T>) -> T` called as
                // `unwrap(boxInt)` (`examples/generics.hc`) failed with "could not infer generic
                // type argument" even once `check_struct_lit`'s own inference (right above this
                // fn) fixed constructing `boxInt` itself, since this loop never tried to look
                // INSIDE the param's own generic argument for a match. The concrete arg passed at
                // the call site is already a real mono instantiation (`"Box$Int"`, from
                // `check_struct_lit`'s fallback or an explicit `Box<Int>{...}`) whose OWN mono arg
                // is exactly the concrete type this fn's `T` needs to be -- `mono_arg("Box$Int")` =
                // `"Int"`, no scanning needed, same technique `ensure_method_instantiated` already
                // uses for a mono receiver. Doesn't verify the ARG's mono base actually matches the
                // param's declared base (`"Box$Int"` against a `b: Wrapper<T>` param, say) --
                // `check_call`'s own per-arg mismatch check right after this fn returns still runs
                // against the now-substituted mono param type and would catch that case anyway.
                let argty = arg_tys.get(i);
                let argn = ty_name(argty);
                if is_mono_name(argn) {
                    inferred = mono_arg(argn);
                    found_infer = true;
                }
            } else if is_array_type_name(p.type_name) && array_elem_type_name(p.type_name) == target_tp && i < arg_tys.length() {
                // `arr: &[T]` -- the param's own declared type is an ARRAY of the bare type param
                // (`type_name_ref` parses `[T]` to the plain string `"[T]"`, same "just a type-name
                // string" convention `"dyn Name"`/`Base$Arg` already use -- see that fn's own
                // header). A real, disclosed scope gap this entry closes: `fn first<T>(arr: &[T])
                // -> T` called as `first(&nums)` (`examples/arrays.hc`) used to fail with "could
                // not infer generic type argument" -- this loop never tried to look INSIDE the
                // param's own array element type for a match, only a bare match (above) or a
                // generic-struct-wrapped one (right above this). The concrete arg's own array
                // element type (`"[Int]"`'s own `"Int"`) is exactly what `T` needs to be here, no
                // scanning needed, same "peel one layer off the arg's real type" technique the
                // `Base<T>` case right above already uses.
                let argty2 = arg_tys.get(i);
                let argn2 = ty_name(argty2);
                if is_array_type_name(argn2) {
                    inferred = array_elem_type_name(argn2);
                    found_infer = true;
                }
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if !found_infer {
            return "?";
        }
        return inferred;
    }

    // Infers a generic top-level fn's type argument(s) from whichever argument's corresponding
    // declared param type is EXACTLY the bare type parameter (see `infer_one_type_param`'s own
    // header for the full scope/rules) -- builds the mono name (`"identity$Int"`, or
    // `"hash_map2_new$ItemStack$Int"` for a two-param generic), `ensure_fn_instantiated`s it, and
    // returns its real (now-concrete) return type.
    fn check_generic_call(&mut self, callee: String, gtmpl: FnDecl, arg_tys: Vec<Ty>) -> Ty {
        var found_tp = "";
        if let Some { value: tp } = gtmpl.type_param { found_tp = tp; }
        var has_tp2call = false;
        var found_tp2call = "";
        if let Some { value: tp2 } = gtmpl.type_param2 { has_tp2call = true; found_tp2call = tp2; }
        let inferred1 = self.infer_one_type_param(&gtmpl.params, &arg_tys, found_tp);
        if inferred1 == "?" {
            self.error("could not infer generic type argument for call to '" + callee + "'");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        var mono_name = callee + "$" + inferred1;
        if has_tp2call {
            let inferred2 = self.infer_one_type_param(&gtmpl.params, &arg_tys, found_tp2call);
            if inferred2 == "?" {
                self.error("could not infer second generic type argument for call to '" + callee + "'");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            mono_name = mono_name + "$" + inferred2;
        }
        self.ensure_fn_instantiated(mono_name);
        let sig_found = self.fn_sigs.get(mono_name);
        if let Some { value: sig } = sig_found {
            return sig.ret_type;
        }
        return TyUnknown;
    }

    // If `rn` is a mono struct instantiation (`"Box$Int"`) and `method` names an instance method
    // declared in the generic struct's own `impl` block (`generic_method_templates`, keyed by the
    // BASE struct name -- `"Box#get"`) not yet concretely registered under `rn`'s own key, infers
    // `T` directly from `rn` itself (`mono_arg("Box$Int")` = `"Int"` -- no scanning needed, unlike
    // a generic top-level fn's own call-site inference, since a method's receiver already names
    // its own concrete instantiation), substitutes params/return/body, registers the concrete
    // `FnSig` under `rn + "#" + method`, and re-checks the substituted copy (`check_method`,
    // save/restore `self.vars` around it -- same re-entrancy concern `ensure_fn_instantiated`'s own
    // header explains). A no-op for anything that isn't this exact shape (a plain struct's own
    // method, `String`'s built-ins, a genuine typo) -- `check_method_call`'s own existing lookup
    // right after this call handles all of those unchanged.
    fn ensure_method_instantiated(&mut self, owner: String, method: String) {
        if !is_mono_name(owner) { return; }
        let key = owner + "#" + method;
        let already = self.methods.get(key);
        var exists = false;
        if let Some { value: s } = already { exists = true; }
        if exists { return; }
        let base = mono_base(owner);
        let arg = mono_arg(owner);
        let gm_found = self.generic_method_templates.get(base + "#" + method);
        if let Some { value: gm } = gm_found {
            var found_tp = "";
            var has_tp2m = false;
            var tp2m = "";
            let tmpl_found = self.generic_templates.get(base);
            if let Some { value: tmpl } = tmpl_found {
                found_tp = tmpl.type_param;
                if let Some { value: tp2mv } = tmpl.type_param2 { has_tp2m = true; tp2m = tp2mv; }
            }
            // `impl Pair<K, V> { fn describe(&self) { self.key.hash_key(); } }` -- a SECOND type
            // param on the RECEIVER's own struct, mirroring `ensure_fn_instantiated`'s own two-
            // param branch (see `Ast.hc`'s own `StructDecl.type_param2` header). `mono_arg(owner)`
            // alone is right for a one-param receiver, wrong for a two-param one
            // ("Pair$ItemStack$String" needs "ItemStack" and "String" separately) -- `mono_arg1_
            // of2`/`mono_arg2_of2` do that split, ONLY reached when the receiver's own template
            // actually has a `type_param2`.
            var real_argm = arg;
            var arg2m = "";
            if has_tp2m {
                real_argm = mono_arg1_of2(owner);
                arg2m = mono_arg2_of2(owner);
            }
            var new_params = empty_param_vec3();
            var new_param_tys = empty_ty_vec();
            var pi = 0;
            while pi < gm.params.length() {
                let p = gm.params.get(pi);
                var pty = substitute_field_type(p.type_name, found_tp, real_argm);
                if has_tp2m {
                    pty = substitute_field_type(pty, tp2m, arg2m);
                }
                new_params.push(Param { name: p.name, type_name: pty, is_ref: p.is_ref, is_mut_ref: p.is_mut_ref });
                new_param_tys.push(ty_from_name(pty));
                pi = pi + 1;
            }
            let ret_ty_str = opt_string_or2(gm.ret_type, "Int");
            var new_ret = substitute_field_type(ret_ty_str, found_tp, real_argm);
            if has_tp2m {
                new_ret = substitute_field_type(new_ret, tp2m, arg2m);
            }
            self.methods.register(key, FnSig { param_types: new_param_tys, ret_type: ty_from_name(new_ret) });
            self.method_mono_needed.push(key);
            var substituted_body = subst_stmt_list(gm.body, found_tp, real_argm);
            if has_tp2m {
                substituted_body = subst_stmt_list(substituted_body, tp2m, arg2m);
            }
            var mono_decl = FnDecl { name: method, params: new_params, ret_type: some_string3(new_ret), body: substituted_body, type_param: none_string3(), type_bounds: empty_str_vec2(), self_is_mut: gm.self_is_mut, type_param2: none_string3(), type_bounds2: empty_str_vec2() };
            let saved_vars = self.vars;
            let saved_scopes = self.var_scopes;
            let saved_moved = self.moved;
            let saved_moved_scopes = self.moved_scopes;
            let saved_mut_capable = self.mut_capable;
            let saved_mut_capable_scopes = self.mut_capable_scopes;
            let saved_ret_ty = self.cur_ret_ty;
            self.check_method(owner, mono_decl);
            self.vars = saved_vars;
            self.var_scopes = saved_scopes;
            self.moved = saved_moved;
            self.moved_scopes = saved_moved_scopes;
            self.mut_capable = saved_mut_capable;
            self.mut_capable_scopes = saved_mut_capable_scopes;
            self.cur_ret_ty = saved_ret_ty;
        }
    }

    // A concrete struct implementing an interface (`impl InterfaceName for StructName { ... }`) is
    // legal wherever a `&dyn InterfaceName` is expected -- without this, every one of the plain
    // `expected != gn` exact-string-match argument checks scattered across this file (`check_call`/
    // `check_static_call`/`check_method_call`'s own arg-checking loops) would reject perfectly
    // legal code passing a concrete struct where an interface reference is declared, since
    // `"dyn Shape" != "Circle"` as bare strings even when `Circle` genuinely implements `Shape`.
    // Centralized here as one predicate rather than duplicating the coercion check at all 5 call
    // sites -- see `Codegen.hotc`'s own header on why no bytecode conversion is needed either way
    // (a struct instance already IS a real implementor of the interface at the class-file level
    // once `gen_struct` adds it to the `implements` list, so passing it where the interface type is
    // expected is just an ordinary widening reference conversion, the same as any real Java
    // subtype-to-supertype argument pass).
    fn is_arg_type_mismatch(&mut self, expected: String, got: String) -> Bool {
        if expected == got { return false; }
        // `fn take_damage(amount: Int<0..=100>)` -- a ranged-Int param's own declared type string
        // never exact-matches a plain argument's checked `"Int"`, so it needs the same "treat as
        // compatible" rule `check_struct_lit`'s own matching branch already established for
        // struct fields (see `IDEAS.md`'s own "Numeric range bounds" entry). Real bounds
        // enforcement (a literal argument checked at compile time, anything else at runtime)
        // happens in `check_call`/`Codegen.hotc`'s own mirrored call-argument codegen, NOT here --
        // this fn only ever answers "is this a real type error", so it can't see the actual
        // argument EXPRESSION to decide literal-vs-not. Widening this rule through here also
        // (harmlessly) makes a ranged-Int param compatible for method/static calls too, even
        // though this pass only wires up the real enforcement for plain top-level fn calls.
        if is_ranged_int_type_chk(expected) && got == "Int" { return false; }
        // A non-null value where a nullable one is expected is real, legal widening (`Type` ->
        // `Type?`) -- the other direction (`Type?` -> `Type`) is still a mismatch, unchanged: a
        // still-nullable value needs narrowing first, not an implicit conversion here.
        if is_nullable_type_name(expected) && strip_nullable(expected) == got {
            return false;
        }
        if is_dyn_type_name_chk(expected) {
            let iface = dyn_interface_name_chk(expected);
            let impl_found = self.struct_interfaces.get(got);
            if let Some { value: implemented_list } = impl_found {
                var ii = 0;
                while ii < implemented_list.length() {
                    let implemented = implemented_list.get(ii);
                    if implemented == iface { return false; }
                    // `implemented` (one of the struct's own direct interfaces -- more than one is
                    // real now, see `struct_interfaces`'s own header) might not be `iface` itself
                    // but still genuinely satisfy it through interface INHERITANCE -- passing a
                    // `Circle` (`impl Child for Circle`, `interface Child : Parent`) where `&dyn
                    // Parent` is expected is real, legal widening. See `Ast.hc`'s own
                    // `transitive_supertraits` header.
                    let supers_found = self.interface_supertraits.get(implemented);
                    if let Some { value: supers } = supers_found {
                        var i = 0;
                        while i < supers.length() {
                            if supers.get(i) == iface { return false; }
                            i = i + 1;
                        }
                    }
                    ii = ii + 1;
                }
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    // `extend Target { fn m(&self, ...) -> Ret { body } }` desugars (see `Codegen.hotc`'s own
    // `materialize_extend_fns` header) into an ORDINARY top-level fn taking the receiver as an
    // explicit first param -- `self.fn_sigs` already has it registered exactly like any other
    // top-level fn by the time method bodies are checked, so `recv.m(args)` resolving to it is
    // just an arity/self-type-compatible lookup against that SAME table, reusing
    // `is_arg_type_mismatch`'s existing interface-widening logic (a struct-direct extension's self
    // param is the bare struct name; an open-interface extension's self param is `"dyn Name"`,
    // which already accepts any implementing struct through the exact same mechanism a `&dyn
    // Name`-typed extern-method param does). Deliberately silent (no error) on a near-miss --
    // arity mismatch, or a self-type that doesn't actually match -- rather than risk misdiagnosing
    // an unrelated same-named top-level fn as "the" extension candidate; same "trust it, don't
    // block compilation over an imprecise diagnosis" honesty this checker already has for a
    // genuinely unknown method (see this fn's own struct-receiver fallback, right below).
    fn check_extension_fallback(&mut self, method: String, recv_ty_name: String, arg_tys: Vec<Ty>) -> Ty {
        let found = self.fn_sigs.get(method);
        if let Some { value: sig } = found {
            let expected_count = sig.param_types.length();
            let got_count = arg_tys.length() + 1;
            if expected_count != got_count {
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            let expected0 = ty_name(sig.param_types.get(0));
            if expected0 != "?" && recv_ty_name != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(expected0, recv_ty_name) {
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            var i = 0;
            while i < arg_tys.length() {
                let expected = ty_name(sig.param_types.get(i + 1));
                let got = arg_tys.get(i);
                let gn = ty_name(got);
                if expected != "?" && gn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(expected, gn) {
                    self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + method + "' expects " + expected + ", got " + gn);
                }
                i = i + 1;
            }
            return sig.ret_type;
        }
        return TyUnknown;
    }

    fn check_method_call(&mut self, recv: &Expr, method: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        let rt = self.check_expr(recv);
        var rn = ty_name(rt);
        // `world.add(entity, Component { ... })` / `world.remove(entity, Component)` -- see
        // `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Archetype-transition mechanics". Handled HERE, before the generic
        // `check_args_lambda_aware` pass right below, specifically because `remove`'s own second
        // argument is a BARE component type name (`Transform`, not `Transform { ... }` -- there's
        // no value to construct for a removal) -- an ordinary `Ident`, which the generic
        // arg-checking pass would otherwise try to resolve as a real variable/static/enum-variant
        // and reject as an unknown identifier. `add`'s own second argument (a real struct
        // literal) would check out fine either way, but handling both together here keeps every
        // `World`-specific special case in one place.
        if rn == "World" && (method == "add" || method == "remove") {
            return self.check_world_add_or_remove(method, args);
        }
        // `world.get(id)` -- see ECS_IDEAS.md's own "Still open" #6 answer: reading a component's
        // value back out by entity
        // id, the one real gap `resources` never actually solved. No real generic-method syntax
        // (`world.get::<Transform>(id)`) -- the queried component type `T` is read back from the
        // enclosing `let`'s own declared `Option<T>` result type instead (`let t: Option<Transform>
        // = world.get(id);`), threaded here via `self.cur_ret_ty` -- see `check_stmt`'s own `Let`
        // arm header for why that's the ambient hint mechanism, not a new parameter.
        if rn == "World" && method == "get" {
            return self.check_world_get(args);
        }
        var arg_tys = self.check_args_lambda_aware(args);
        if rn == "?" {
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        // See `check_field_access_on_type`'s own header, same idea: a still-nullable receiver
        // calling a method is exactly the `sm.toString()`-before-narrowing case README's own
        // "Nullable types" section calls out. Reported once, then treated as its underlying type
        // so the real method lookup below still resolves normally (a separate "unknown method"
        // error on top would just be noise).
        if is_nullable_type_name(rn) {
            self.error("possibly-null value of type '" + rn + "' used without a null check");
            rn = strip_nullable(rn);
        }
        return self.check_method_call_by_type(rn, method, args, arg_tys);
    }

    // The real validation body of `world.add(...)`/`world.remove(...)` -- see `check_method_
    // call`'s own header on why this lives here rather than in `check_method_call_by_type`
    // alongside `spawn`/`run_all`. Just like `spawn`'s own gate, the STRUCT LITERAL argument's
    // own field values aren't separately `check_expr`'d here (same disclosed imprecision that
    // gate already has) -- this only validates the SHAPE (a real component struct literal for
    // `add`, a real component's bare name for `remove`) and records which component types are
    // ever added/removed anywhere in the program, into `add_component_types`/
    // `remove_component_types` -- `Codegen.hotc`'s own archetype-transition synthesis is the only
    // reader, computing the full closure of archetype signatures the program could ever need from
    // these two lists plus `archetype_signatures` itself (spawn's own starting set).
    fn check_world_add_or_remove(&mut self, method: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        if args.length() != 2 {
            var arg2_desc = "struct literal";
            if method == "remove" { arg2_desc = "type name"; }
            self.error("'world." + method + "(...)' takes exactly 2 arguments: an entity id and a component " + arg2_desc);
            return ty_from_name("Unit");
        }
        let entity_arg = args.get(0);
        let entity_ty = self.check_expr(&entity_arg);
        let entity_tn = ty_name(entity_ty);
        if entity_tn != "Int" && entity_tn != "?" {
            self.error("'world." + method + "(...)' argument 1 must be an Int entity id, got " + entity_tn);
        }
        let comp_arg = args.get(1);
        if method == "add" {
            match comp_arg {
                StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => {
                    let comp_found = self.components.get(name);
                    var comp_known = false;
                    if let Some { value: _ck } = comp_found { comp_known = true; }
                    if !comp_known {
                        self.error("'world.add(...)' argument 2 names '" + name + "', which isn't a known 'component'");
                    }
                    if !str_vec_contains_ecs(&self.add_component_types, name) {
                        self.add_component_types.push(name);
                    }
                }
                _ => {
                    self.error("'world.add(...)' argument 2 must be a component struct literal (e.g. a 'Transform' literal)");
                }
            }
        } else {
            match comp_arg {
                Ident { name } => {
                    let comp_found2 = self.components.get(name);
                    var comp_known2 = false;
                    if let Some { value: _ck2 } = comp_found2 { comp_known2 = true; }
                    if !comp_known2 {
                        self.error("'world.remove(...)' argument 2 names '" + name + "', which isn't a known 'component'");
                    }
                    if !str_vec_contains_ecs(&self.remove_component_types, name) {
                        self.remove_component_types.push(name);
                    }
                }
                _ => {
                    self.error("'world.remove(...)' argument 2 must be a bare component type name (e.g. 'Transform'), not a value");
                }
            }
        }
        return ty_from_name("Unit");
    }

    // `world.get(id)` -- see ECS_IDEAS.md's own "Still open" #6 answer / `check_method_call`'s own
    // header for the full design. Needs the queried component type `T` from the enclosing `let`'s
    // own declared `Option<T>` result type (`self.cur_ret_ty`, threaded by `check_stmt`'s own
    // `Let` arm) -- there is no real generic-method syntax carrying it any other way.
    fn check_world_get(&mut self, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        if args.length() != 1 {
            self.error("'world.get(...)' takes exactly 1 argument: an entity id");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        let entity_arg = args.get(0);
        let entity_ty = self.check_expr(&entity_arg);
        let entity_tn = ty_name(entity_ty);
        if entity_tn != "Int" && entity_tn != "?" {
            self.error("'world.get(...)' argument 1 must be an Int entity id, got " + entity_tn);
        }
        let inner_found = option_inner_type_chk(self.cur_ret_ty);
        match inner_found {
            Some { value: t } => {
                let comp_found3 = self.components.get(t);
                var comp_known3 = false;
                if let Some { value: _ck3 } = comp_found3 { comp_known3 = true; }
                if !comp_known3 {
                    self.error("'world.get(...)' is being assigned to a declared 'Option<" + t + ">', but '" + t + "' isn't a known 'component'");
                    return TyUnknown;
                }
                if !str_vec_contains_ecs(&self.queried_component_types, t) {
                    self.queried_component_types.push(t);
                }
                // Registers the real `Option$<T>` monomorphized enum instantiation (variant
                // ownership tables included) -- without this, the CALLER's own `match t1 { Some
                // {...} => ..., None => ... }` on the result fails with "'Some' is not a variant
                // of 'Option$Transform'", since nothing else in this whole call path ever
                // constructs a REAL `Some`/`None` literal that would trigger this registration
                // the normal way (`check_struct_lit`'s own path, unreached here -- this result
                // comes back from a query, not a literal).
                self.ensure_instantiated("Option$" + t);
                return ty_from_name("Option$" + t);
            }
            None => {
                self.error("'world.get(...)' needs a declared 'Option<T>' result type on its 'let', e.g. 'let t: Option<Transform> = world.get(id);'");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
        }
    }

    // The real method-lookup/arg-validation body of `check_method_call`, factored out so
    // `check_safe_call` (`x?.method(...)`, see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.SafeCall` header) can reuse it
    // against the receiver's own STRIPPED (non-null) type name, without going through
    // `check_method_call`'s own "still-nullable receiver is an error" gate above -- `?.` is
    // precisely the alternative to narrowing first, not a second way to trigger that same error.
    fn check_method_call_by_type(&mut self, rn: String, method: String, args: Vec<Expr>, arg_tys0: Vec<Ty>) -> Ty {
        var arg_tys = arg_tys0;
        // `world.set_resource(DeltaTime { seconds: 0.016 })` -- sets the ONE world-wide value of
        // a `resource` type (see `Ast.hc`'s own `Program.resources` header for the real gap this
        // closes: getting live external per-frame state, a camera/input/delta-time, into a
        // system). Exactly one argument, a resource struct literal -- unlike `spawn` right below,
        // there's no variable-length archetype signature to build, so this doesn't need its own
        // `Vec$<T>#push` instantiation nudge either; `Codegen.hotc`'s own `World#set_resource`
        // branch is a single, direct `PUTSTATIC`.
        if rn == "World" && method == "set_resource" {
            if args.length() != 1 {
                self.error("'world.set_resource(...)' takes exactly one argument (a resource struct literal), got " + JString::valueOf(args.length()));
                return ty_from_name("Unit");
            }
            let a0 = args.get(0);
            match a0 {
                StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => {
                    let res_found = self.resources.get(name);
                    var res_known = false;
                    if let Some { value: _rk2 } = res_found { res_known = true; }
                    if !res_known {
                        self.error("'world.set_resource(...)' argument names '" + name + "', which isn't a known 'resource'");
                    }
                }
                _ => {
                    self.error("'world.set_resource(...)' argument must be a resource struct literal (e.g. a 'DeltaTime' literal), not this expression shape");
                }
            }
            return ty_from_name("Unit");
        }
        // `world.spawn(Transform { ... }, Velocity { ... }, ...)` -- see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own
        // design doc. NOT a real declared method (there's no way to give `spawn` one fixed
        // signature -- it takes an arbitrary set of DIFFERENT component types per call site), so
        // this is checked here directly, gated on the receiver/method name exactly the way
        // `Codegen.hotc`'s own `PhoenixPool::spawn` capture check is gated. Each argument must be
        // a component struct literal; the SORTED-free, as-written list of component names is one
        // "archetype signature" -- `Codegen.hotc`'s own archetype-synthesis pass reads `self.
        // archetype_signatures` back to know exactly which archetype storage shapes this program
        // actually needs. Real, disclosed v1 imprecision: the SAME component set spawned in a
        // different argument order at another call site creates a SEPARATE archetype rather than
        // being recognized as the same shape (no canonicalization/sorting this pass) -- wastes a
        // little storage, never a correctness problem.
        if rn == "World" && method == "spawn" {
            var sig = empty_str_vec2();
            var i = 0;
            while i < args.length() {
                let a = args.get(i);
                match a {
                    StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => {
                        let comp_found = self.components.get(name);
                        var comp_known = false;
                        if let Some { value: _ck } = comp_found { comp_known = true; }
                        if !comp_known {
                            self.error("'world.spawn(...)' argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " names '" + name + "', which isn't a known 'component'");
                        }
                        sig.push(name);
                    }
                    _ => {
                        self.error("'world.spawn(...)' arguments must be component struct literals (e.g. a 'Transform' literal) -- argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " isn't one");
                    }
                }
                i = i + 1;
            }
            if !vec_str_vec_contains_ecs(self.archetype_signatures, sig) {
                self.archetype_signatures.push(sig);
            }
            // `Codegen.hotc`'s own `World#spawn` branch emits a raw, hand-written `INVOKEVIRTUAL
            // Vec$<T>.push` for the entity-id vec and every component vec -- real bytecode that
            // completely bypasses the ORDINARY `.push(...)` method-call path this checker would
            // otherwise use to monomorphize `Vec$<T>`'s own generic template on demand (`Checker.
            // hotc`'s own `ensure_method_instantiated`, normally triggered by `check_method_call_
            // by_type`'s generic-struct dispatch when a REAL `.push(...)` call gets type-checked).
            // Without this, `Vec$Int#push`/`Vec$<Component>#push` never make it into `self.methods`
            // (or, downstream, `Codegen.hotc`'s own `struct_methods`), so `gen_vec_push_ecs` finds
            // no real method descriptor and hands ASM an empty one -- a real, previously-untested
            // crash (`StringIndexOutOfBoundsException` inside ASM's own stack-frame simulation)
            // caught only once `world.spawn(...)` was finally exercised end-to-end. Explicitly
            // nudging every `Vec$<T>#push` this call site's own hand-written codegen will need is
            // the fix -- one for the shared entity-id vec, one per component in `sig`.
            self.ensure_method_instantiated("Vec$Int", "push");
            // `entities.length()` too -- `Codegen.hotc`'s own spawn codegen now ALSO reads it
            // (to compute the row this entity's data just landed at, for `__entity_row`) -- see
            // `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Archetype-transition mechanics".
            self.ensure_method_instantiated("Vec$Int", "length");
            var ei2 = 0;
            while ei2 < sig.length() {
                self.ensure_method_instantiated("Vec$" + sig.get(ei2), "push");
                ei2 = ei2 + 1;
            }
            // `world.spawn(...)` now returns the entity id it just created (an `Int`) -- needed
            // so `world.add`/`world.remove` have something to operate on later.
            return ty_from_name("Int");
        }
        // `world.run_all()` -- runs every `system` once per matching entity, in `self.
        // system_order`'s own already-derived, already-validated order. Takes no arguments (there's
        // nothing per-call to configure -- which systems run and in what order is a whole-program
        // property, already fixed by `check_systems` above). Not a real declared method for the
        // same reason `spawn` isn't -- `Codegen.hotc`'s own `World#run_all` branch is the only
        // real codegen for it, and it's a thin forward to one synthesized `__world_run_all` fn
        // (see that branch's own header).
        if rn == "World" && method == "run_all" {
            if args.length() != 0 {
                self.error("'world.run_all()' takes no arguments");
            }
            return ty_from_name("Unit");
        }
        // `world.shutdown()` -- releases the real `PhoenixPool` `world.run_all()` may have
        // created for Phase B dispatch (see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Phase B" writeup) -- REUSED
        // across every `world.run_all()` call, not recreated per call, so it's on the program to
        // call this once, near the end, same as `PhoenixPool::shutdown()` itself already asks of
        // ordinary Phoenix Flight callers (`Executors.newFixedThreadPool`'s own worker threads
        // are not daemon threads -- a pool that's never shut down keeps the process alive
        // forever). A real no-op when no `system` in the program ever needed more than one
        // thread (the common case) -- `Codegen.hotc`'s own `__world_shutdown` fn (this gate's
        // only real codegen, a thin forward like `run_all`'s own) has an empty body then.
        if rn == "World" && method == "shutdown" {
            if args.length() != 0 {
                self.error("'world.shutdown()' takes no arguments");
            }
            return ty_from_name("Unit");
        }
        // A `&dyn InterfaceName`-typed receiver dispatches against the INTERFACE's own declared
        // method table, not a concrete struct's -- that's the entire point of dynamic dispatch:
        // the checker only knows the static type is "some struct implementing InterfaceName", not
        // WHICH one, so `self.methods` (keyed by concrete struct name) has nothing to look up here
        // at all. No arg-type verification against the interface signature this phase (a real,
        // disclosed narrower check than the concrete-struct-method branch below gets) -- trusted,
        // same "declared type is truth" honesty `extern class`/`check_cast` already get elsewhere.
        if is_dyn_type_name_chk(rn) {
            let iface_name = dyn_interface_name_chk(rn);
            let iface_found = self.interfaces.get(iface_name);
            if let Some { value: iface_methods } = iface_found {
                var imi = 0;
                while imi < iface_methods.length() {
                    let im = iface_methods.get(imi);
                    if im.name == method {
                        return ty_from_name(im.ret_type);
                    }
                    imi = imi + 1;
                }
                return self.check_extension_fallback(method, rn, arg_tys);
            }
            self.error("unknown interface '" + iface_name + "'");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        self.ensure_instantiated(rn);
        self.ensure_method_instantiated(rn, method);
        let key = rn + "#" + method;
        // See `register_extern`'s own header: extern methods are keyed with an arity suffix, and
        // (to disambiguate same-arity overloads) an ADDITIONAL full-signature key -- try the
        // precise one first, falling back to arity-only.
        var arg_type_names2 = empty_str_vec2();
        var ati2 = 0;
        while ati2 < arg_tys.length() {
            arg_type_names2.push(ty_name(arg_tys.get(ati2)));
            ati2 = ati2 + 1;
        }
        let sig_key2 = rn + "#" + method + "#" + JString::valueOf(args.length()) + "#" + join_type_names_chk(arg_type_names2);
        let extern_key = rn + "#" + method + "#" + JString::valueOf(args.length());
        var extern_found = self.extern_methods.get(sig_key2);
        var ef_known = false;
        if let Some { value: efv } = extern_found { ef_known = true; }
        if !ef_known {
            extern_found = self.extern_methods.get(extern_key);
        }
        if let Some { value: m } = extern_found {
            let expected_count = m.param_types.length();
            let got_count = arg_tys.length();
            if expected_count != got_count {
                self.error("'" + method + "' expects " + JString::valueOf(expected_count) + " args, got " + JString::valueOf(got_count));
            } else {
                self.backfill_lambda_args(&args, &m.param_types, &mut arg_tys);
                var i = 0;
                while i < got_count {
                    let expected = m.param_types.get(i);
                    let got = arg_tys.get(i);
                    let gn = ty_name(got);
                    if expected != "?" && gn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(expected, gn) {
                        self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + method + "' expects " + expected + ", got " + gn);
                    }
                    i = i + 1;
                }
            }
            if m.ret_type == "Self" { return ty_from_name(rn); }
            return ty_from_name(m.ret_type);
        }
        let found = self.methods.get(key);
        if let Some { value: sig } = found {
            let expected_count = sig.param_types.length();
            let got_count = arg_tys.length();
            if expected_count != got_count {
                self.error("'" + method + "' expects " + JString::valueOf(expected_count) + " args, got " + JString::valueOf(got_count));
            } else {
                // Real gap found building Phoenix Flight's own `pool.spawn(|| ...)`: a lambda
                // literal passed to a PLAIN (non-extern) struct method's own `&dyn Interface`
                // param never got its target type inferred at all -- this branch never called
                // `backfill_lambda_args_ty` the way the extern-method branch above already calls
                // its own `Vec<String>` sibling, so the lambda reached `Codegen.hotc`'s own
                // `gen_expr` with NO target interface known, throwing "lambda literal used
                // outside a valid call-argument context" even though `t.go(|| 42)`-shaped code is
                // exactly what a `&dyn Interface` param is FOR.
                self.backfill_lambda_args_ty(&args, &sig.param_types, &mut arg_tys);
                // Move-check: same "is_ref param never moves its argument" rule `check_call` uses
                // -- see `TyChecker.method_param_is_ref`'s own header.
                let is_ref_found2 = self.method_param_is_ref.get(key);
                var is_ref_list2 = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                if let Some { value: irl2 } = is_ref_found2 { is_ref_list2 = irl2; }
                let is_mutref_found2 = self.method_param_is_mut_ref.get(key);
                var is_mutref_list2 = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                if let Some { value: imrl2 } = is_mutref_found2 { is_mutref_list2 = imrl2; }
                var borrow_names2 = empty_str_vec2();
                var borrow_is_mut2 = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                var borrow_present2 = empty_bool_vec_chk();
                var i = 0;
                while i < got_count {
                    let expected = sig.param_types.get(i);
                    let got = arg_tys.get(i);
                    let en = ty_name(expected);
                    let gn = ty_name(got);
                    if en != "?" && gn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(en, gn) {
                        self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + method + "' expects " + en + ", got " + gn);
                    }
                    var arg_is_ref2 = true;
                    if i < is_ref_list2.length() { arg_is_ref2 = is_ref_list2.get(i); }
                    if !arg_is_ref2 {
                        let ai3 = args.get(i);
                        self.move_check_consumed(&ai3, gn);
                    }
                    var arg_is_mut_ref2 = false;
                    if i < is_mutref_list2.length() { arg_is_mut_ref2 = is_mutref_list2.get(i); }
                    let ai4 = args.get(i);
                    match ai4 {
                        Ident { name } => {
                            borrow_names2.push(name);
                            borrow_is_mut2.push(arg_is_mut_ref2);
                            borrow_present2.push(true);
                        }
                        _ => {
                            borrow_names2.push("");
                            borrow_is_mut2.push(false);
                            borrow_present2.push(false);
                        }
                    }
                    i = i + 1;
                }
                self.check_borrow_rules(borrow_names2, borrow_is_mut2, borrow_present2, "call to '" + method + "'");
            }
            return sig.ret_type;
        } else {
            // No user-defined method by this name on this receiver type -- trust it (same
            // "unknown, don't verify" spirit as `check_call`'s own unknown-callee case). Covers
            // real receivers this checker has no method table for at all (`String`'s built-in
            // methods, resolved for real only in `Codegen.hotc`) as well as genuine typos, which
            // is a real, disclosed imprecision -- not distinguishing the two costs an error
            // message on a typo, but doesn't reject anything that should compile. `extend`'s own
            // desugared top-level fn (see this fn's own `check_extension_fallback` header) is
            // tried first -- silently falls through to the same "trust it" `TyUnknown` when even
            // THAT doesn't match, so a genuine typo still costs nothing more than it already did.
            return self.check_extension_fallback(method, rn, arg_tys);
        }
    }

    // `x?.method(args)` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.SafeCall` header. Unlike a plain `.` call, a
    // NULLABLE receiver is exactly what this operator is FOR (no "possibly-null used without a
    // check" error the way `check_method_call` gives one) -- a genuinely non-nullable receiver, on
    // the other hand, is the real error here (nothing for `?.` to ever short-circuit around, `.`
    // is the honest spelling). The whole result is wrapped back to nullable (unless the member's
    // own declared return type was already nullable) -- scoped to a struct/extern-class-shaped
    // result only, same "no boxed nullable primitive" restriction `Type?` itself has everywhere
    // else.
    fn check_safe_call(&mut self, recv: &Expr, method: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        let rt = self.check_expr(recv);
        let rn = ty_name(rt);
        let arg_tys = self.check_args_lambda_aware(args);
        if rn == "?" { return TyUnknown; }
        var base = rn;
        if is_nullable_type_name(rn) {
            base = strip_nullable(rn);
        } else {
            self.error("'?.' used on a non-nullable value of type '" + rn + "' (use '.' instead)");
        }
        let result = self.check_method_call_by_type(base, method, args, arg_tys);
        return self.wrap_safe_result(result, method);
    }

    // `x?.field` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.SafeFieldAccess` header. Mirrors `check_safe_call`'s
    // own header exactly, just against `check_field_access_by_type` instead of a method lookup.
    fn check_safe_field_access(&mut self, recv: &Expr, field: String) -> Ty {
        let rt = self.check_expr(recv);
        let rn = ty_name(rt);
        if rn == "?" { return TyUnknown; }
        var base = rn;
        if is_nullable_type_name(rn) {
            base = strip_nullable(rn);
        } else {
            self.error("'?.' used on a non-nullable value of type '" + rn + "' (use '.' instead)");
        }
        let result = self.check_field_access_by_type(base, field);
        return self.wrap_safe_result(result, field);
    }

    // Shared by `check_safe_call`/`check_safe_field_access`: wraps a member's own result type back
    // to nullable (leaving an already-nullable result alone, rather than double-suffixing), and
    // rejects a primitive result outright -- there's no boxed "nullable Int" this checker can hand
    // back, the same restriction `Type?` itself has everywhere. `member_name` is only for the error
    // message.
    fn wrap_safe_result(&mut self, result: Ty, member_name: String) -> Ty {
        let rname = ty_name(result);
        if rname == "?" { return TyUnknown; }
        if rname == "Int" || rname == "Bool" || rname == "Long" || rname == "Float" || rname == "Double" || rname == "Unit" || is_array_type_name(rname) {
            self.error("'?.' result of '" + member_name + "' must be a struct/extern-class type, got " + rname + " (nullable primitives aren't supported)");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        if is_nullable_type_name(rname) {
            return ty_from_name(rname);
        }
        return ty_from_name(rname + "?");
    }

    // `x ?: default` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.Elvis` header. `left` must genuinely be nullable
    // (nothing to short-circuit around otherwise); `right`'s type must match `left`'s underlying
    // (non-null) type, same widening `is_arg_type_mismatch` already applies to an ordinary call
    // argument.
    fn check_elvis(&mut self, left: &Expr, right: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let lt = self.check_expr(left);
        let ln = ty_name(lt);
        let rt = self.check_expr(right);
        let rn = ty_name(rt);
        if ln == "?" {
            return ty_from_name(rn);
        }
        if !is_nullable_type_name(ln) {
            self.error("'?:' left side must be a nullable (Type?) value, got " + ln);
            return ty_from_name(rn);
        }
        let base = strip_nullable(ln);
        if rn != "?" && base != rn && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(base, rn) {
            self.error("'?:' right side expects " + base + ", got " + rn);
        }
        return ty_from_name(base);
    }

    fn check_assign(&mut self, name: String, value: &Expr) -> Ty {
        let vt = self.check_expr(value);
        let vn = ty_name(vt);
        // Move-check: `x = y;` moves `y` (a bare, non-Copy local) into `x`, same as `let x = y;`.
        self.move_check_consumed(value, vn);
        let existing = self.lookup_var(name);
        if let Some { value: et } = existing {
            let en = ty_name(et);
            if en != "?" && vn != "?" && en != vn {
                self.error("cannot assign " + vn + " to '" + name + "' of type " + en);
            }
        } else {
            // Not a local -- fall back to a top-level `static`, same "local shadows static"
            // precedence `check_ident`'s own read-side lookup already establishes (a local can
            // never collide here anyway: this branch only runs when NO local by this name
            // exists). Real gap found needing genuine mutable module-level state (a stateful
            // "manager" class ported from Java, `private static` fields reassigned across
            // calls): every `pub static` was previously write-once (`<clinit>` only), with
            // `Assign` unconditionally erroring "unknown variable" for anything not already a
            // local, even a real static of the exact same name -- see `gen_stmt`'s own `Assign`
            // arm (`Codegen.hotc`) for the matching `PUTSTATIC` codegen half of this fix.
            let static_existing = self.statics.get(name);
            if let Some { value: set } = static_existing {
                let sen = ty_name(set);
                if sen != "?" && vn != "?" && sen != vn {
                    self.error("cannot assign " + vn + " to '" + name + "' of type " + sen);
                }
            } else {
                self.error("unknown variable '" + name + "'");
            }
        }
        // `x` is freshly reinitialized -- un-moves it, regardless of whether it was ever moved to
        // begin with (a no-op either way).
        self.unmark_moved(name);
        // `vt` was already consumed by `ty_name(vt)` above -- reconstruct fresh from the name
        // string instead of trying to reuse the (now-moved) original enum value.
        return ty_from_name(vn);
    }

    // === Statements ===

    fn check_stmt(&mut self, s: Stmt) {
        match s {
            // See `Ast.hc`'s own `Stmt::Let.declared_type` header: a declared `&dyn Interface`
            // annotation, when `init`'s own real (inferred) type is a struct that genuinely
            // implements it (checked via the exact same `is_arg_type_mismatch` widening logic an
            // ordinary argument pass already gets), overrides the registered variable type to the
            // WIDER declared one -- everything else (no annotation, or one that doesn't apply)
            // keeps the plain inferred type, unchanged from before this feature.
            Let { name, mutable, init, declared_type, line } => {
                // Consumed exactly ONCE, right here, into plain `Copy`-safe locals -- both the NEW
                // comprehension special-case below and the EXISTING `dyn`-override check need
                // `declared_type`, and destructuring an `Option<String>` twice trips the same
                // "use of moved value" trap this file's own header repeatedly documents elsewhere.
                var dt_str = "";
                var has_dt = false;
                if let Some { value: dt00 } = declared_type { dt_str = dt00; has_dt = true; }
                // A comprehension init is checked entirely differently (`check_comprehension`, NOT
                // the generic `check_expr` dispatch every other `init` shape goes through) --
                // see `Ast.hc`'s own `Comprehension` header for why it genuinely needs
                // `declared_type` to exist at all. `self.check_expr(&init)` (the fallback path) is
                // called FROM INSIDE the wildcard arm itself, not after this whole match -- the
                // move-checker's own conservative branch handling treats `init` as "possibly
                // moved" after ANY match arm moves it, regardless of which arm actually ran.
                var gn = "?";
                match init {
                    Comprehension { result_expr, var_name, iter_expr, cond } => {
                        if !has_dt {
                            self.error("comprehension needs a declared result type, e.g. 'let " + name + ": Vec<T> = [...]'");
                        }
                        let ct = self.check_comprehension(result_expr, var_name, iter_expr, cond, dt_str);
                        gn = ty_name(ct);
                        self.vars.register(name, ty_from_name(gn));
                        self.unmark_moved(name);
                        self.mark_mut_capable(name, mutable);
                    }
                    // A BARE fieldless-enum-variant reference used as a `let`/`var` initializer
                    // (`let e: Option<Int> = None;`, no `{}` at all -- `check_ident`'s own header
                    // explains why this parses as a plain `Ident`, not a `StructLit`) needs the
                    // declared type as ITS OWN `expected_mono` hint, same real gap `check_struct_
                    // lit`'s per-field loop and `check_field_assign_on_type` already got fixed for
                    // (see those two's own headers) -- `check_expr`'s generic `StructLit`/`Ident`
                    // dispatch only ever threads `self.cur_ret_ty` (the ENCLOSING FUNCTION's own
                    // return type, e.g. `Unit` for `main`), never a `let`'s own declared type. Real
                    // gap found writing a real test for the enum-structural-equality fix: `let e:
                    // Option<Int> = None;` failed with "unknown struct or enum variant 'None'"
                    // even though `e`'s own declared type said exactly what was needed. Falls back
                    // to the ordinary `_` wildcard arm's own body (duplicated here rather than
                    // shared, since `init` isn't a known bare variant with a declared type EVERY
                    // time an `Ident` shows up here -- most of the time it's just an ordinary
                    // variable reference, `let x = someVar;`) whenever this ISN'T that one narrow
                    // shape.
                    Ident { name: iname } => {
                        var used_variant_hint = false;
                        if has_dt {
                            let local_found3 = self.lookup_var(iname);
                            var is_ordinary3 = false;
                            if let Some { value: lv3 } = local_found3 { is_ordinary3 = true; }
                            let static_found3 = self.statics.get(iname);
                            if let Some { value: sv3 } = static_found3 { is_ordinary3 = true; }
                            if !is_ordinary3 {
                                let variant_found3 = self.variant_owner.get(iname);
                                let generic_variant_found3 = self.generic_variant_owner.get(iname);
                                var is_known_variant3 = false;
                                if let Some { value: ow3 } = variant_found3 { is_known_variant3 = true; }
                                if let Some { value: gow3 } = generic_variant_found3 { is_known_variant3 = true; }
                                if is_known_variant3 {
                                    let ct3 = self.check_struct_lit(iname, empty_str_vec2(), empty_expr_vec3(), none_string3(), some_string3(dt_str));
                                    gn = ty_name(ct3);
                                    self.vars.register(name, ty_from_name(gn));
                                    self.unmark_moved(name);
                                    self.mark_mut_capable(name, mutable);
                                    used_variant_hint = true;
                                }
                            }
                        }
                        if !used_variant_hint {
                            let t3b = self.check_expr(&init);
                            gn = ty_name(t3b);
                            self.move_check_consumed(&init, gn);
                            var final_ty3b = ty_from_name(gn);
                            if has_dt && is_dyn_type_name_chk(dt_str) {
                                if gn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(dt_str, gn) {
                                    self.error("cannot assign " + gn + " to '" + name + "' of declared type " + dt_str);
                                } else {
                                    final_ty3b = ty_from_name(dt_str);
                                }
                            }
                            self.vars.register(name, final_ty3b);
                            self.unmark_moved(name);
                            self.mark_mut_capable(name, mutable);
                        }
                    }
                    // A generic struct/enum-variant literal used as a `let`/`var` initializer
                    // (`let s: Option<String> = Some { value: expensive() };`) hits the SAME real
                    // gap as the bare-`Ident` case right above, just for the `{ ... }` form:
                    // `check_expr`'s `StructLit` dispatch only ever threads `self.cur_ret_ty`, and
                    // `check_struct_lit`'s own shape-only field-literal fallback (see its own
                    // header) only recognizes a NARROW set of field-value shapes (a literal, or an
                    // already-typed local) -- anything else, like a binary expression or a
                    // function call, still falls through to "unknown struct or enum variant".
                    // Real gap found writing a real test for the enum-structural-equality fix:
                    // `Some { value: "hel" + "lo" }` (a `Binary` field value) failed even with a
                    // perfectly good declared type sitting right there on the `let`. Threading
                    // `dt_str` through directly removes the need to guess from the field's own
                    // shape at all.
                    StructLit { name: sname, field_names: sfnames, field_values: sfvals, generic_base: sgbase } => {
                        var t_sl = TyUnknown;
                        if has_dt {
                            t_sl = self.check_struct_lit(sname, sfnames, sfvals, sgbase, some_string3(dt_str));
                        } else {
                            t_sl = self.check_struct_lit(sname, sfnames, sfvals, sgbase, none_string3());
                        }
                        gn = ty_name(t_sl);
                        var final_ty_sl = ty_from_name(gn);
                        if has_dt && is_dyn_type_name_chk(dt_str) {
                            if gn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(dt_str, gn) {
                                self.error("cannot assign " + gn + " to '" + name + "' of declared type " + dt_str);
                            } else {
                                final_ty_sl = ty_from_name(dt_str);
                            }
                        }
                        self.vars.register(name, final_ty_sl);
                        self.unmark_moved(name);
                        self.mark_mut_capable(name, mutable);
                    }
                    _ => {
                        // `world.get(id)` (see `check_method_call`'s own `World`/`"get"` header)
                        // needs `dt_str` (this `let`'s own declared `Option<T>` type) to know
                        // which component `T` is being queried -- there's no real generic-method
                        // syntax to carry it any other way. Threaded through the SAME ambient
                        // `self.cur_ret_ty` fallback `check_struct_lit`'s/bare-variant-`Ident`'s
                        // own dispatch already consults (see this fn's own header on why it's
                        // otherwise "the enclosing FUNCTION's own return type") -- save/restored
                        // around this ONE call, same idiom `check_generic_method_instantiation`'s
                        // own monomorphization pass already uses elsewhere in this file.
                        // Unconditional whenever `has_dt` (not gated on `init`'s own shape) is
                        // harmless when unused, for the identical reason `Codegen.hotc`'s own
                        // matching `self.ret_ty` override already is: nothing here consults it
                        // unless nothing else resolves the type first.
                        var saved_ret_ty_let = self.cur_ret_ty;
                        if has_dt { self.cur_ret_ty = dt_str; }
                        let t = self.check_expr(&init);
                        self.cur_ret_ty = saved_ret_ty_let;
                        // `t` is consumed exactly once, right here (`ty_name` takes it by value) --
                        // the rest of this arm rebuilds whichever `Ty` it ultimately registers
                        // FRESH from the resulting `gn` string (`ty_name`/`ty_from_name` already
                        // round-trip losslessly for every type this checker has), rather than ever
                        // reading `t` itself again, so there is no later "use of moved value"
                        // regardless of which conditional branch below actually runs.
                        gn = ty_name(t);
                        // Move-check: `let x = y;` moves `y` (a bare, non-Copy local) into `x` --
                        // see this file's own move-checking header (`TyChecker.moved`).
                        self.move_check_consumed(&init, gn);
                        // `let x: Int<0..=100> = 150;` -- a real, disclosed narrower scope than
                        // `IDEAS.md`'s own "Numeric range bounds" entry (see `Parser.hotc`'s own
                        // `type_name_ref` header): only a `let`/`var`'s own declared type, no
                        // struct fields/fn params/returns, and no resulting-range propagation
                        // through arithmetic -- a ranged `Int` is registered as plain `"Int"`
                        // below and behaves as an ordinary `Int` everywhere else from this point
                        // on. What IS real: a LITERAL init out of range is a compile-time error
                        // right here (the "only literal-vs-literal gets proven statically" cut
                        // that entry's own header explicitly suggests as "a defensible, much
                        // simpler first cut"); anything else (a variable, a computed expression)
                        // gets a REAL runtime bounds check instead, inserted unconditionally by
                        // `Codegen.hotc`'s own mirrored `gen_stmt` `Let` arm (which independently
                        // re-checks `dt_str`/`init`'s own shape rather than this pass threading a
                        // new AST field through -- both passes read the exact same already-
                        // existing `declared_type` string and `init` node, so there's nothing new
                        // for `Codegen.hotc` to trust from THIS pass beyond "it didn't error").
                        if has_dt && is_ranged_int_type_chk(dt_str) {
                            if gn != "?" && gn != "Int" {
                                self.error("cannot assign " + gn + " to '" + name + "' of declared type " + dt_str);
                            } else {
                                match init {
                                    IntLit { value } => {
                                        let rlo = ranged_int_lo_chk(dt_str);
                                        let rhi = ranged_int_hi_chk(dt_str);
                                        let rincl = ranged_int_inclusive_chk(dt_str);
                                        var rout = value < rlo;
                                        if rincl {
                                            if value > rhi { rout = true; }
                                        } else {
                                            if value >= rhi { rout = true; }
                                        }
                                        if rout {
                                            self.error("'" + name + "' = " + JString::valueOf(value) + " is out of the declared range " + dt_str);
                                        }
                                    }
                                    _ => {}
                                }
                            }
                        }
                        var final_ty = ty_from_name(gn);
                        if has_dt && is_dyn_type_name_chk(dt_str) {
                            if gn != "?" && self.is_arg_type_mismatch(dt_str, gn) {
                                self.error("cannot assign " + gn + " to '" + name + "' of declared type " + dt_str);
                            } else {
                                // Always overridden once a `dyn` annotation is present and passes
                                // (or is simply untyped, "?", trusted the same honest way every
                                // other unknown-typed value already is elsewhere in this file) --
                                // never left silently un-applied, so `Codegen.hotc`'s own `Let`
                                // codegen (which has no `struct_interfaces` registry of its own to
                                // re-verify this against) can unconditionally trust "declared_type
                                // present and dyn" without ever risking a checker/codegen type
                                // disagreement -- see that fn's own header.
                                final_ty = ty_from_name(dt_str);
                            }
                        }
                        self.vars.register(name, final_ty);
                        self.unmark_moved(name);
                        self.mark_mut_capable(name, mutable);
                    }
                }
                // `name` is a fresh binding (even when it shadows an existing local of the same
                // name in the SAME scope frame -- `register` overwrites in place, see `Registry`'s
                // own "last write wins" header) -- any stale moved-flag left over from whatever it
                // shadows must not leak onto it, same reasoning `check_assign`'s own header gives.
                // Phase 3 borrow checking: only a `var` local's own fields can be assigned into,
                // or the local itself re-borrowed `&mut` -- see `TyChecker.mut_capable`'s own
                // header. Both already applied inside each match arm above (`unmark_moved`/
                // `mark_mut_capable`), once per arm rather than once here, since each arm's own
                // `name` registration happens at a different point in the match.
            }
            ExprStmt { expr, line } => {
                // `@must_use` -- a bare `Call` to a must-use fn with nothing consuming its return
                // value (not a `let`/`return`/argument/anything else) is a real error, not a
                // silent no-op. Only this exact shape (`Expr.Call` directly at statement level) is
                // checked -- a must-use fn's result flowing through a larger expression before
                // being discarded (rare, and this checker doesn't track "does ANY subexpression's
                // value ultimately go unused") isn't caught, same disclosed narrower scope the
                // retired Kotlin compiler's own version had.
                match expr {
                    Call { callee, args } => {
                        let mu_found = self.must_use_fns.get(callee);
                        if let Some { value: mu } = mu_found {
                            self.error("return value of '" + callee + "' must be used");
                        }
                    }
                    _ => {}
                }
                self.check_expr(expr);
            }
            Return { value, line } => {
                if let Some { value: v } = value {
                    let rt = self.check_expr(&v);
                    // Move-check: `return y;` moves `y` out to the caller, same as `let x = y;`.
                    self.move_check_consumed(&v, ty_name(rt));
                }
            }
            If { cond, then_body, else_body, line, narrow_name, narrow_is_eq } => {
                let ct = self.check_expr(cond);
                let cn = ty_name(ct);
                if cn != "Bool" && cn != "?" {
                    self.error("if condition must be Bool, got " + cn);
                }
                // Guard-clause null-narrowing -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Stmt::If.narrow_name` header
                // for why the parser, not this fn, already did the "is `cond` exactly `Ident ==
                // null` / `Ident != null`" recognition, and README's own "Nullable types" section
                // for the two idioms this drives. `narrow_name` might name a var that ISN'T
                // actually nullable-typed (`if n == null` where `n: Int`, say -- the parser's own
                // lookahead has no type information to rule that out) -- `is_nullable_type_name`
                // below is what actually gates real narrowing, silently no-op-ing otherwise.
                var do_narrow = false;
                var narrow_orig_type = "";
                if narrow_name != "" {
                    let vfound = self.lookup_var(narrow_name);
                    if let Some { value: vty } = vfound {
                        let vtyname = ty_name(vty);
                        if is_nullable_type_name(vtyname) {
                            do_narrow = true;
                            narrow_orig_type = vtyname;
                        }
                    }
                }
                // Move-checking's own "only one branch actually runs" concern -- see `snapshot_
                // moved_state`'s own header. Taken BEFORE any of the three narrowing shapes below
                // touch `then_body`/`eb`, so it's the true pre-`if` state regardless of which
                // shape this particular `If` takes.
                let if_move_snap = self.snapshot_moved_state();
                // Divergence for BOTH sides, checked up front (before either `then_body`/`eb` is
                // handed to `check_block`, which consumes it) -- `merge_if_moved`'s own header
                // explains why this matters: a branch that unconditionally `return`s/`throw`s
                // never reaches whatever comes after the whole `if`, so its own moves must not
                // affect that code either.
                let then_diverges = stmt_list_diverges_chk(&then_body);
                var else_diverges = false;
                if let Some { value: ebd } = else_body {
                    else_diverges = stmt_list_diverges_chk(&ebd);
                }
                if do_narrow && !narrow_is_eq {
                    // `if x != null { ... }` -- narrow WITHIN the then-block only; outside it
                    // (including any `else`), `x` was never proven non-null.
                    self.vars.register(narrow_name, ty_from_name(strip_nullable(narrow_orig_type)));
                    self.check_block(then_body);
                    self.vars.register(narrow_name, ty_from_name(narrow_orig_type));
                    let then_move_snap = self.snapshot_moved_state();
                    self.restore_moved_state(if_move_snap);
                    if let Some { value: eb } = else_body {
                        self.check_block(eb);
                    }
                    self.merge_if_moved(then_move_snap, then_diverges, else_diverges);
                } else if do_narrow && narrow_is_eq {
                    // `if x == null { ... }` -- `x` stays nullable INSIDE this branch (it genuinely
                    // is null there).
                    self.check_block(then_body);
                    let then_move_snap2 = self.snapshot_moved_state();
                    self.restore_moved_state(if_move_snap);
                    if let Some { value: eb } = else_body {
                        // Reaching `else` means the condition was false (`x != null`) -- narrow
                        // within it, restore after, same as the `!=` case above.
                        self.vars.register(narrow_name, ty_from_name(strip_nullable(narrow_orig_type)));
                        self.check_block(eb);
                        self.vars.register(narrow_name, ty_from_name(narrow_orig_type));
                    }
                    self.merge_if_moved(then_move_snap2, then_diverges, else_diverges);
                    if then_diverges {
                        // Anything after this whole `if`, in the SAME enclosing block, is only
                        // reachable via a path where `x` is non-null -- narrow PERMANENTLY. This
                        // registers directly into `self.vars` as it stands RIGHT NOW (the enclosing
                        // block's own scope, since `check_block`'s own push/pop for `then_body`/`eb`
                        // has already returned by this point -- see `TyChecker.var_scopes`'s own
                        // header for the real, now-real lexical scoping this relies on), so it
                        // correctly persists for the rest of the current block without any extra
                        // plumbing, the exact guard-clause idiom README's own "Nullable types"
                        // section documents.
                        self.vars.register(narrow_name, ty_from_name(strip_nullable(narrow_orig_type)));
                    }
                } else {
                    self.check_block(then_body);
                    let then_move_snap3 = self.snapshot_moved_state();
                    self.restore_moved_state(if_move_snap);
                    if let Some { value: eb } = else_body {
                        self.check_block(eb);
                    }
                    self.merge_if_moved(then_move_snap3, then_diverges, else_diverges);
                }
            }
            While { cond, body, line } => {
                let ct = self.check_expr(cond);
                let cn = ty_name(ct);
                if cn != "Bool" && cn != "?" {
                    self.error("while condition must be Bool, got " + cn);
                }
                self.loop_depth = self.loop_depth + 1;
                self.check_block(body);
                self.loop_depth = self.loop_depth - 1;
            }
            For { var_name, is_range, range_start, range_end, inclusive, arr_expr, body, line } => {
                self.check_for(var_name, is_range, range_start, range_end, arr_expr, body);
            }
            Match { subject, arms, line } => {
                self.check_match(subject, arms);
            }
            // `throw` now accepts either a plain `String` message (wrapped into a hardcoded
            // `java.lang.RuntimeException` at codegen, unchanged) OR an already-constructed real
            // exception object (`throw LexError::new(msg);` -- real `Lexer.hotc` usage, a genuine
            // gap found self-compiling it, since `extern class` support didn't exist AT ALL when
            // this restriction was first written) -- thrown directly via a real `ATHROW`, no
            // wrapping. `Int`/`Bool` are the only types that can never be legitimately thrown
            // either way; everything else (String, a struct, an extern-typed value) is trusted,
            // matching this checker's existing "no real classpath reflection, trust declared
            // types" honesty elsewhere (no verification that a non-String value is ACTUALLY a real
            // `Throwable` under the hood).
            Throw { value, line } => {
                let vt = self.check_expr(value);
                let vn = ty_name(vt);
                if vn == "Int" || vn == "Bool" {
                    self.error("throw needs a String message or a real exception value, got " + vn);
                }
            }
            // No drop-list tracking (`varsToDropBeforeBreak`/`varsToDropBeforeContinue` in the
            // real compiler) -- this subset has no `Drop`/RAII mechanism at all, so there's
            // nothing to clean up on the way out. `self.loop_depth` (incremented/decremented
            // around a `While`'s own body check, below) is the only thing that needs tracking.
            Break { line } => {
                if self.loop_depth == 0 {
                    self.error("'break' outside of a loop");
                }
            }
            Continue { line } => {
                if self.loop_depth == 0 {
                    self.error("'continue' outside of a loop");
                }
            }
            // `catch_type` must name a real, known `extern class` -- there's no other way this
            // subset has to name a real JVM exception type. `catch_var` is registered into a scope
            // pushed for this catch clause alone -- it's a fresh exception value on every catch, not
            // something later code outside the clause should still see.
            Try { try_block, catches, line } => {
                self.check_block(try_block);
                var ci = 0;
                while ci < catches.length() {
                    let c = catches.get(ci);
                    let ext_found = self.externs.get(c.catch_type);
                    var found_ext = false;
                    if let Some { value: bin } = ext_found { found_ext = true; }
                    self.push_scope();
                    if found_ext {
                        self.vars.register(c.catch_var, TyStruct { name: c.catch_type });
                    } else {
                        self.error("'catch' type '" + c.catch_type + "' is not a known extern class");
                    }
                    self.check_block(c.catch_block);
                    self.pop_scope();
                    ci = ci + 1;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // Real exhaustiveness checking (below, near this fn's own end) -- every declared variant of
    // `sn`'s enum must be covered by name, or a `_` wildcard arm present. Each arm's bound field
    // names are validated against the *enum's* union field table (see `register_enum`'s own
    // header) when there's no per-variant table, not the specific variant's own fields -- same
    // simplification `StructLit` construction already makes.
    fn check_match(&mut self, subject: Expr, arms: Vec<MatchArm>) {
        let st = self.check_expr(subject);
        let sn = ty_name(st);
        // `match`ing a `&dyn InterfaceName` value is a genuinely different shape (arms name
        // STRUCTS implementing the interface, not enum variants) with its own exhaustiveness rule
        // (every SEALED interface's real implementor covered, not every enum variant) -- routed to
        // its own fn entirely rather than threading `if is_dyn`/`else` branches through every step
        // below, mirroring how the real compiler keeps `checkSealedMatch` separate from its own
        // plain `checkMatch` too.
        if is_dyn_type_name_chk(sn) {
            self.check_sealed_match(dyn_interface_name_chk(sn), arms);
        } else {
            // A generic enum instantiation might only ever be *matched* in this particular function
            // (constructed elsewhere, possibly checked later -- fn declaration order doesn't imply
            // check order dependencies should exist at all), so unlike a plain enum's already-real
            // `structs`/`enum_variants`/`variant_owner_q` entries, `sn` might not be registered yet.
            // Real bug found building this: `check_field_access`/`check_method_call` already call
            // `ensure_instantiated` on their receiver's type for exactly this reason -- `check_match`
            // was missing the same call, so a generic-enum-typed match subject with no construction
            // site checked earlier in the same run failed every arm with a spurious "'Some' is not a
            // variant of 'Opt$Int'".
            self.ensure_instantiated(sn);
            var has_wildcard = false;
            var covered = empty_str_vec2();
            var i = 0;
            while i < arms.length() {
                let arm = arms.get(i);
                if arm.is_literal {
                    // A literal pattern (`"+" => { ... }`) never names a real variant -- skip the
                    // variant-existence check/`covered` tracking entirely (see `Ast.hc`'s own
                    // `MatchArm.is_literal` header for why there's no exhaustiveness requirement
                    // for these either). Just checks the literal's own type actually matches the
                    // subject's.
                    let lt = self.check_expr(arm.literal);
                    let ln = ty_name(lt);
                    if sn != "?" && ln != "?" && ln != sn {
                        self.error("match arm literal is " + ln + ", subject is " + sn);
                    }
                } else if arm.variant_name == "_" {
                    has_wildcard = true;
                } else {
                    covered.push(arm.variant_name);
                    // `variant_owner_q` (a qualified "Owner#Variant" existence table, populated for
                    // every enum -- plain and generic mono instantiations alike) rather than the bare
                    // `variant_owner` lookup, since the latter would collide across multiple concrete
                    // instantiations of the same generic enum (see `Ast.hc`'s own `EnumDecl` header).
                    let owner_q_found = self.variant_owner_q.get(sn + "#" + arm.variant_name);
                    var owner_ok = false;
                    if let Some { value: ok } = owner_q_found { owner_ok = true; }
                    if !owner_ok && sn != "?" {
                        self.error("'" + arm.variant_name + "' is not a variant of '" + sn + "'");
                    }
                }
                // Real scope, pushed for this arm alone: a binding leaking into a LATER arm or past
                // the whole `match` (the old, flat-`self.vars` behavior) would let one arm's field
                // name shadow another's real local by accident -- popped right after this arm's own
                // body is checked, below.
                self.push_scope();
                var fj = 0;
                // Real loop bound is `bind_names.length()`, not `field_names.length()` --
                // `Goblin(hp)` (`arm.is_positional`) leaves `field_names` EMPTY (the parser has
                // no way to know the variant's own real field names), so looping on IT would
                // never run at all for a positional arm. Both are the same length for the
                // ordinary `{ field: name }` form anyway (built together, one push per binding).
                while fj < arm.bind_names.length() {
                    let bname = arm.bind_names.get(fj);
                    var fty = "?";
                    // `Goblin(hp)` resolves `hp` to whichever field sits at POSITION `fj` in the
                    // variant's own declared field table (`variant_field_info`, DECLARATION
                    // order) -- the ordinary `{ field: name }` form already names the real field
                    // directly, so only the positional shape needs this extra lookup.
                    var fname = "";
                    if arm.is_positional {
                        let vinfo_for_pos = self.variant_field_info.get(sn + "#" + arm.variant_name);
                        if let Some { value: vfp } = vinfo_for_pos {
                            if fj < vfp.field_names.length() {
                                fname = vfp.field_names.get(fj);
                            } else {
                                self.error("'" + arm.variant_name + "' has fewer than " + JString::valueOf(fj + 1) + " field(s), but the pattern binds " + JString::valueOf(arm.bind_names.length()));
                            }
                        }
                    } else {
                        fname = arm.field_names.get(fj);
                    }
                    // Prefer the ARM's OWN variant's dedicated field table (`variant_field_info`,
                    // keyed "OwnerName#VariantName") over the enum-wide union `self.structs.get(sn)`
                    // -- the same real gap `check_struct_lit` already had and fixed (see that fn's own
                    // `variant_field_info` header): `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr` enum reuses the field name
                    // "value" across many variants at DIFFERENT types (`IntLit.value: Int`,
                    // `StringLit.value: String`, ...), so the union table's first-match-wins linear
                    // scan always resolved every arm's "value" binding to whichever variant registered
                    // first (`IntLit`'s `Int`) -- a real bug found self-compiling `Checker.hotc`'s own
                    // `check_match`, silently mistyping every OTHER variant's bindings until this.
                    // Falls back to the union table when there's no per-variant entry (a plain,
                    // non-enum struct never being matched this way in practice, but harmless either
                    // way).
                    let variant_info_found = self.variant_field_info.get(sn + "#" + arm.variant_name);
                    if let Some { value: vinfo } = variant_info_found {
                        fty = find_field_type(vinfo, fname);
                    } else {
                        let info_found = self.structs.get(sn); // fresh every iteration -- see check_match's own header
                        if let Some { value: info } = info_found {
                            fty = find_field_type(info, fname);
                        }
                    }
                    self.vars.register(bname, ty_from_name(fty));
                    fj = fj + 1;
                }
                self.check_block(arm.body);
                self.pop_scope();
                i = i + 1;
            }
            if !has_wildcard && sn != "?" {
                let variants_found = self.enum_variants.get(sn);
                if let Some { value: variants } = variants_found {
                    var vi = 0;
                    while vi < variants.length() {
                        let vname = variants.get(vi);
                        var found_cov = false;
                        var ci = 0;
                        while ci < covered.length() {
                            if covered.get(ci) == vname { found_cov = true; }
                            ci = ci + 1;
                        }
                        if !found_cov {
                            self.error("match on '" + sn + "' is not exhaustive, missing variant '" + vname + "'");
                        }
                        vi = vi + 1;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // Every struct name currently registered as implementing `iface_name` (`self.struct_interfaces`
    // is `StructName -> every interface it directly implements`, so this is a real reverse scan,
    // not a point lookup) -- `Registry<T>`'s own `.entries` field read directly, same as any other
    // struct field, since `Registry<T>` has no `.keys()`/iteration method of its own to reuse here.
    fn sealed_implementers(&mut self, iface_name: String) -> Vec<String> {
        var out = empty_str_vec2();
        var i = 0;
        while i < self.struct_interfaces.entries.length() {
            let e = self.struct_interfaces.entries.get(i);
            var ej = 0;
            while ej < e.value.length() {
                if e.value.get(ej) == iface_name {
                    out.push(e.key);
                }
                ej = ej + 1;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }

    // `match subject { StructName { field, ... } => { body } ... }` on a `&dyn InterfaceName`
    // value -- each arm names a STRUCT implementing `InterfaceName`, not an enum variant, but
    // parses through the exact same `MatchArm` shape (`Name { field, ... } => { body }` is
    // syntactically identical either way, so `Parser.hotc` needed zero changes for this). Real
    // exhaustiveness: every struct `sealed_implementers` currently knows about must be covered by
    // name, or a `_` wildcard arm present -- mirrors `check_match`'s own enum-variant exhaustiveness
    // check exactly, just against the interface's real implementors instead of an enum's declared
    // variants. Only a `sealed interface` supports this at all (see `Ast.hc`'s own
    // `InterfaceDecl.is_sealed` header for why) -- a plain `interface` can't ever be exhaustive,
    // since nothing closes its set of implementors within this compile unit.
    fn check_sealed_match(&mut self, iface_name: String, arms: Vec<MatchArm>) {
        let sealed_found = self.sealed_interfaces.get(iface_name);
        var is_sealed = false;
        if let Some { value: s } = sealed_found { is_sealed = true; }
        if !is_sealed {
            self.error("match over '&dyn " + iface_name + "' requires '" + iface_name + "' to be a 'sealed interface'");
            var ei = 0;
            while ei < arms.length() {
                self.check_block(arms.get(ei).body);
                ei = ei + 1;
            }
        } else {
            var has_wildcard = false;
            var covered = empty_str_vec2();
            var i = 0;
            while i < arms.length() {
                let arm = arms.get(i);
                // Scoped to this arm alone, same as `check_match`'s own arm bindings -- see that
                // fn's own comment.
                self.push_scope();
                if arm.variant_name == "_" {
                    has_wildcard = true;
                } else {
                    let struct_name = arm.variant_name;
                    let impls_found = self.struct_interfaces.get(struct_name);
                    var implements_iface = false;
                    if let Some { value: real_ifaces } = impls_found {
                        var rii = 0;
                        while rii < real_ifaces.length() {
                            if real_ifaces.get(rii) == iface_name { implements_iface = true; }
                            rii = rii + 1;
                        }
                    }
                    if !implements_iface {
                        self.error("'" + struct_name + "' doesn't implement sealed interface '" + iface_name + "'");
                    } else {
                        covered.push(struct_name);
                    }
                    var fj = 0;
                    while fj < arm.field_names.length() {
                        let fname = arm.field_names.get(fj);
                        let bname = arm.bind_names.get(fj);
                        var fty = "?";
                        let info_found = self.structs.get(struct_name);
                        if let Some { value: info } = info_found {
                            fty = find_field_type(info, fname);
                        }
                        self.vars.register(bname, ty_from_name(fty));
                        fj = fj + 1;
                    }
                }
                self.check_block(arm.body);
                self.pop_scope();
                i = i + 1;
            }
            if !has_wildcard {
                let implementers = self.sealed_implementers(iface_name);
                var vi = 0;
                while vi < implementers.length() {
                    let sname = implementers.get(vi);
                    var found_cov = false;
                    var ci = 0;
                    while ci < covered.length() {
                        if covered.get(ci) == sname { found_cov = true; }
                        ci = ci + 1;
                    }
                    if !found_cov {
                        self.error("match on '&dyn " + iface_name + "' is not exhaustive, missing '" + sname + "' (add arms or a '_' wildcard)");
                    }
                    vi = vi + 1;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // Fresh, empty enclosing-scope stack -- called by `check_fn`/`check_method` alongside their own
    // existing `self.vars` reset, so a re-entrant nested check (`ensure_fn_instantiated`/
    // `ensure_method_instantiated`, mid-body) starts from zero enclosing scopes rather than
    // inheriting the calling function's own scope stack.
    fn reset_var_scopes(&mut self) {
        var seed = vec_of(self.vars);
        self.var_scopes = Vec { data: seed.data, len: 0 };
        var mseed = vec_of(self.moved);
        self.moved_scopes = Vec { data: mseed.data, len: 0 };
        self.moved = registry_new("__init__", false);
        var mcseed = vec_of(self.mut_capable);
        self.mut_capable_scopes = Vec { data: mcseed.data, len: 0 };
        self.mut_capable = registry_new("__init__", false);
    }

    // Enter a new lexical scope: the current scope (`self.vars`) is pushed onto `self.var_scopes`
    // (innermost-last) and `self.vars` becomes a fresh, empty table -- so anything registered from
    // here until the matching `pop_scope` is invisible once this scope ends, and shadows (rather
    // than overwrites) any same-named binding from an enclosing scope in the meantime. `self.moved`/
    // `self.moved_scopes` are pushed/popped in EXACT lockstep (same index depth as `var_scopes`) --
    // see `TyChecker.moved`'s own header for why moved-state needs the same per-scope-frame split.
    fn push_scope(&mut self) {
        self.var_scopes.push(self.vars);
        self.vars = registry_new("__init__", TyUnknown);
        self.moved_scopes.push(self.moved);
        self.moved = registry_new("__init__", false);
        self.mut_capable_scopes.push(self.mut_capable);
        self.mut_capable = registry_new("__init__", false);
    }

    // Leave the scope most recently opened by `push_scope`, discarding every binding it registered
    // and restoring the enclosing scope as `self.vars` (and `self.moved`/`self.mut_capable`).
    fn pop_scope(&mut self) {
        self.vars = self.var_scopes.pop();
        self.moved = self.moved_scopes.pop();
        self.mut_capable = self.mut_capable_scopes.pop();
    }

    // Marks `name` moved-out-of, in whichever scope frame actually DECLARES it (current `self.
    // moved` if `name` is a local in the CURRENT scope, else whichever `var_scopes` frame owns it)
    // -- walked in lockstep with `lookup_var`'s own resolution order, so the flag lands on the same
    // frame a later `is_moved` read for the same name will find. A name this checker doesn't
    // recognize as a local at all (already resolved to `TyUnknown`/a static/a typo elsewhere) is
    // silently a no-op -- nothing to mark.
    fn mark_moved(&mut self, name: String) {
        let found = self.vars.get(name);
        if let Some { value: _v } = found {
            self.moved.register(name, true);
            return;
        }
        var i = self.var_scopes.length() - 1;
        while i >= 0 {
            let scope = self.var_scopes.get(i);
            let found2 = scope.get(name);
            if let Some { value: _v2 } = found2 {
                let frame = self.moved_scopes.get(i);
                frame.register(name, true);
                return;
            }
            i = i - 1;
        }
    }

    // Reassigning `x` (`x = ...;`) rebinds it to a fresh value -- clears any prior moved-flag, same
    // scope-walk as `mark_moved`.
    fn unmark_moved(&mut self, name: String) {
        let found = self.vars.get(name);
        if let Some { value: _v } = found {
            self.moved.register(name, false);
            return;
        }
        var i = self.var_scopes.length() - 1;
        while i >= 0 {
            let scope = self.var_scopes.get(i);
            let found2 = scope.get(name);
            if let Some { value: _v2 } = found2 {
                let frame = self.moved_scopes.get(i);
                frame.register(name, false);
                return;
            }
            i = i - 1;
        }
    }

    // Is `name` currently moved-out-of? Same resolution order as `lookup_var`/`mark_moved` --
    // checks the scope frame that actually owns the declaration, not just the current one.
    fn is_moved(&mut self, name: String) -> Bool {
        let found = self.vars.get(name);
        if let Some { value: _v } = found {
            let mf = self.moved.get(name);
            if let Some { value: m } = mf { return m; }
            return false;
        }
        var i = self.var_scopes.length() - 1;
        while i >= 0 {
            let scope = self.var_scopes.get(i);
            let found2 = scope.get(name);
            if let Some { value: _v2 } = found2 {
                let frame = self.moved_scopes.get(i);
                let mf2 = frame.get(name);
                if let Some { value: m2 } = mf2 { return m2; }
                return false;
            }
            i = i - 1;
        }
        return false;
    }

    // Records whether `name` can have its own fields assigned into / be re-borrowed `&mut` --
    // set once, at the point `name` is registered as a local (a `Let`, a fn/method param, or
    // `self`), never mutated afterward. Same owning-frame scope-walk as `mark_moved`.
    fn mark_mut_capable(&mut self, name: String, val: Bool) {
        let found = self.vars.get(name);
        if let Some { value: _v } = found {
            self.mut_capable.register(name, val);
            return;
        }
        var i = self.var_scopes.length() - 1;
        while i >= 0 {
            let scope = self.var_scopes.get(i);
            let found2 = scope.get(name);
            if let Some { value: _v2 } = found2 {
                let frame = self.mut_capable_scopes.get(i);
                frame.register(name, val);
                return;
            }
            i = i - 1;
        }
    }

    // Can `name` currently have its own fields assigned into / be re-borrowed `&mut`? True for a
    // `var` local, an `&mut`-declared param, or `self` in an `&mut self` method; false for
    // everything else (a `let` local, a plain by-value param, a `&`-borrowed param, `self` in a
    // `&self`/bare-`self` method) -- see `TyChecker.mut_capable`'s own header for the full rule
    // set. Same resolution order as `is_moved`.
    fn is_mut_capable(&mut self, name: String) -> Bool {
        let found = self.vars.get(name);
        if let Some { value: _v } = found {
            let mf = self.mut_capable.get(name);
            if let Some { value: m } = mf { return m; }
            return false;
        }
        var i = self.var_scopes.length() - 1;
        while i >= 0 {
            let scope = self.var_scopes.get(i);
            let found2 = scope.get(name);
            if let Some { value: _v2 } = found2 {
                let frame = self.mut_capable_scopes.get(i);
                let mf2 = frame.get(name);
                if let Some { value: m2 } = mf2 { return m2; }
                return false;
            }
            i = i - 1;
        }
        return false;
    }

    // Phase 3 borrow checking's own two call-site rules, both from README's own "`&mut` vs `&`"
    // section: (1) an argument landing on an `&mut` param must itself be mutably accessible (a
    // `var` local, an `&mut` param, or `&mut self` -- reusing `is_mut_capable`, the same property
    // that gates field assignment); (2) WITHIN one call's argument list (the implicit receiver of
    // a method call included -- callers pass it as element 0), the same name can't appear more
    // than once if at least one of those positions is `&mut` (`foo(&mut p, &p)` and `foo(&mut p,
    // &mut p)` are both rejected; `foo(&p, &p)` is fine). `names`/`is_mut`/`present` are three
    // parallel Vecs, one entry per argument POSITION (`present` is false for anything that isn't
    // a bare `Ident` -- a computed expression has no named binding to check either rule against,
    // and is silently trusted, same "only a bare Ident" scope cut `move_check_consumed` already
    // discloses).
    fn check_borrow_rules(&mut self, names: Vec<String>, is_mut: Vec<Bool>, present: Vec<Bool>, context: String) {
        var i = 0;
        while i < names.length() {
            if present.get(i) && is_mut.get(i) {
                if !self.is_mut_capable(names.get(i)) {
                    self.error("cannot borrow '" + names.get(i) + "' as mutable in " + context + " -- it's not a 'var' local, an '&mut'-borrowed param, or '&mut self'");
                }
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        var j = 0;
        while j < names.length() {
            if present.get(j) {
                var k = j + 1;
                while k < names.length() {
                    if present.get(k) && names.get(j) == names.get(k) {
                        if is_mut.get(j) || is_mut.get(k) {
                            self.error("cannot borrow '" + names.get(j) + "' as mutable more than once, or alongside another borrow of it, within the same " + context);
                        }
                    }
                    k = k + 1;
                }
            }
            j = j + 1;
        }
    }

    // Move-checks `e` for the SPECIFIC "this position consumes its whole value" contexts --
    // `let`/`x = ...`/`return`/a struct-literal field value/a by-value call argument -- called
    // AFTER `check_expr(e)` has already run (so an already-moved read was already flagged there,
    // see `check_ident`'s own header). Only a bare `Ident` of a non-Copy type actually moves;
    // anything else (a field access, a fresh struct literal, a call's return value, ...) produces
    // a value with no OLD binding to invalidate.
    fn move_check_consumed(&mut self, e: &Expr, ty_name0: String) {
        if is_copy_ty_name_chk(ty_name0) { return; }
        match e {
            Ident { name } => { self.mark_moved(name); }
            _ => {}
        }
    }

    // A real, independent snapshot of the FULL moved-state (current scope + every enclosing one),
    // for `check_stmt`'s own `If` arm: since only ONE of `then`/`else` actually runs, checking both
    // branches against the SAME live `self.moved`/`self.moved_scopes` would let one branch's moves
    // incorrectly "leak" into the other's checking (e.g. `if c { consume(x); } else { use(x); }` --
    // perfectly fine, since only one arm runs, but would wrongly flag `use(x)` if `then`'s move was
    // still visible while checking `else`). Restore with `restore_moved_state`; merge the two
    // branches' real effects afterward with `union_moved_into_current`.
    fn snapshot_moved_state(&mut self) -> Vec<Registry<Bool>> {
        var out = vec_of(clone_bool_registry_chk(self.moved));
        var i = 0;
        while i < self.moved_scopes.length() {
            out.push(clone_bool_registry_chk(self.moved_scopes.get(i)));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }

    // Restores exactly what `snapshot_moved_state` captured. Assumes the scope DEPTH hasn't
    // changed since the snapshot was taken (true for `If`'s own then/else, which each open and
    // close their own `check_block` scope symmetrically before this ever runs).
    fn restore_moved_state(&mut self, snap: Vec<Registry<Bool>>) {
        self.moved = snap.get(0);
        var i = 1;
        while i < snap.length() {
            self.moved_scopes.set(i - 1, snap.get(i));
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    // Unions every `true` flag from a PREVIOUSLY-captured snapshot (typically `then`'s own
    // resulting state, snapshotted right after checking it) into the CURRENT moved-state (typically
    // `else`'s resulting state, or the original pre-`if` state when there's no `else` at all) --
    // the "moved in either branch" merge `check_stmt`'s own `If` arm needs.
    fn union_moved_into_current(&mut self, other: Vec<Registry<Bool>>) {
        or_true_entries_chk(self.moved, other.get(0));
        var i = 0;
        while i < self.moved_scopes.length() {
            or_true_entries_chk(self.moved_scopes.get(i), other.get(i + 1));
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    // The real merge decision for an `If`'s two branches, accounting for divergence -- a branch
    // that unconditionally `return`s/`throw`s never reaches whatever code comes after the whole
    // `if`, so ITS moves must not affect that code either (the single most common false positive
    // move-checking's own branch-merge would otherwise produce: `if special_case { ...moves x...;
    // return y; } return x;` -- see `stmt_list_diverges_chk`'s own header). Call sequence at each
    // `If`-arm call site: snapshot before either branch, check `then`, snapshot again as `then_
    // snap`, restore to the pre-`if` snapshot, check `else` (if present), then call this with both
    // snapshots and both branches' divergence -- it leaves `self.moved`/`self.moved_scopes` as the
    // correct final state.
    fn merge_if_moved(&mut self, then_snap: Vec<Registry<Bool>>, then_diverges: Bool, else_diverges: Bool) {
        if then_diverges && else_diverges {
            // Both paths diverge -- nothing after the `if` is reachable through either one, so
            // the resulting state is moot; leave it as `else`'s (already current), same as the
            // ordinary union would produce anyway in the vast majority of real cases.
            return;
        }
        if then_diverges {
            // Only `else` (or the implicit "skipped the `if` entirely" path when there's no real
            // `else`) ever reaches code after this -- `self.moved`/`self.moved_scopes` are already
            // exactly that state (restored to the pre-`if` snapshot, then `else` checked on top of
            // it, if any) -- nothing to merge in from `then`.
            return;
        }
        if else_diverges {
            // Only `then` ever reaches code after this -- discard whatever `else` did (it's
            // already the live state) and use `then`'s own resulting snapshot directly instead of
            // unioning the two.
            self.restore_moved_state(then_snap);
            return;
        }
        // Neither diverges -- the real conservative union: moved in EITHER branch counts as moved
        // afterward.
        self.union_moved_into_current(then_snap);
    }

    // The one true read path for a local variable's type, innermost scope first -- see
    // `TyChecker.var_scopes`'s own header. `check_ident`/`check_assign`/anything else that used to
    // read `self.vars.get(name)` directly now goes through this instead, so a read from inside a
    // nested block still finds a binding from an enclosing one.
    fn lookup_var(&mut self, name: String) -> Option<Ty> {
        let found = self.vars.get(name);
        if let Some { value: t } = found {
            return Option<Ty>::Some { value: t };
        }
        var i = self.var_scopes.length() - 1;
        while i >= 0 {
            let scope = self.var_scopes.get(i);
            let f2 = scope.get(name);
            if let Some { value: t2 } = f2 {
                return Option<Ty>::Some { value: t2 };
            }
            i = i - 1;
        }
        return Option<Ty>::None {};
    }

    fn check_block(&mut self, stmts: Vec<Stmt>) {
        self.push_scope();
        var i = 0;
        while i < stmts.length() {
            self.check_stmt(stmts.get(i));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        self.pop_scope();
    }

    fn check_arg_list(&mut self, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Vec<Ty> {
        if args.length() == 0 {
            return empty_ty_vec();
        }
        var out = vec_of(self.check_expr(args.get(0)));
        var i = 1;
        while i < args.length() {
            out.push(self.check_expr(args.get(i)));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }

    // === Top level ===

    // Called once per impl-block method (by the driver, before any fn/method body is checked --
    // mirrors `fn_sigs`'/`register_struct`'s own forward-registration).
    fn register_method(&mut self, struct_name: String, f: FnDecl) {
        let key = struct_name + "#" + f.name;
        self.methods.register(key, FnSig { param_types: param_types_of(f.params), ret_type: ty_from_name(opt_string_or2(f.ret_type, "Int")) });
        self.method_param_is_ref.register(key, param_is_ref_of(f.params));
        self.method_param_is_mut_ref.register(key, param_is_mut_ref_of(f.params));
    }

    // Same as `register_method`, but into `static_methods` -- no `self` to worry about, plain
    // params like a top-level fn's own `fn_sigs` registration.
    fn register_static_method(&mut self, struct_name: String, f: FnDecl) {
        let key = struct_name + "#" + f.name;
        self.static_methods.register(key, FnSig { param_types: param_types_of(f.params), ret_type: ty_from_name(opt_string_or2(f.ret_type, "Int")) });
        self.static_method_param_is_mut_ref.register(key, param_is_mut_ref_of(f.params));
        self.static_method_param_is_ref.register(key, param_is_ref_of(f.params));
    }

    // Like `check_fn`, but also binds `self` (the struct's own type) into the variable
    // environment before checking params/body -- `self` isn't itself an entry in `f.params` (see
    // `Ast.hc`'s `ImplDecl` header).
    fn check_method(&mut self, struct_name: String, f: FnDecl) {
        // Fresh scope per fn/method -- `Registry<T>.get()` returns the *first* match (see this
        // file's own header), so without resetting here, a param name reused across two
        // functions with *different* types (two different fns both taking a param called `b`,
        // say) would silently resolve the second fn's own `b` to the first fn's leftover type.
        // Real bug, caught by the generics demo -- `combo`'s own `b: Int` was shadowed by an
        // earlier-checked fn's unrelated `b: Box$Int` until this fix.
        self.vars = registry_new("__init__", TyUnknown);
        self.reset_var_scopes();
        self.vars.register("self", ty_from_name(struct_name));
        // Phase 3 borrow checking: `self.field = ...` inside this method's own body is only
        // allowed when the receiver was declared `&mut self` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `FnDecl.
        // self_is_mut` header.
        self.mark_mut_capable("self", f.self_is_mut);
        self.cur_ret_ty = opt_string_or2(f.ret_type, "Unit");
        var i = 0;
        while i < f.params.length() {
            let p = f.params.get(i);
            // A ranged-Int param (`Int<0..=100>`, see `IDEAS.md`'s own "Numeric range bounds"
            // entry) is registered as plain `"Int"` for the fn/method's OWN body -- same
            // "behaves as an ordinary Int everywhere else once the check passes" promise `find_
            // field_type` already keeps for struct fields (real bug found running a real
            // example: without this, `amount - 1` inside the body itself failed with "'-' needs
            // matching Int, ... operands, got Int and Int<0..=100>", since ordinary arithmetic
            // type-checking compares exact type-name strings).
            var pty = p.type_name;
            if is_ranged_int_type_chk(pty) { pty = "Int"; }
            self.vars.register(p.name, ty_from_name(pty));
            self.mark_mut_capable(p.name, p.is_mut_ref);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        self.check_block(f.body);
    }

    fn check_fn(&mut self, f: FnDecl) {
        self.vars = registry_new("__init__", TyUnknown); // fresh scope per fn -- see check_method's own comment
        self.reset_var_scopes();
        self.cur_ret_ty = opt_string_or2(f.ret_type, "Unit");
        var i = 0;
        while i < f.params.length() {
            let p = f.params.get(i);
            // A ranged-Int param (`Int<0..=100>`, see `IDEAS.md`'s own "Numeric range bounds"
            // entry) is registered as plain `"Int"` for the fn/method's OWN body -- same
            // "behaves as an ordinary Int everywhere else once the check passes" promise `find_
            // field_type` already keeps for struct fields (real bug found running a real
            // example: without this, `amount - 1` inside the body itself failed with "'-' needs
            // matching Int, ... operands, got Int and Int<0..=100>", since ordinary arithmetic
            // type-checking compares exact type-name strings).
            var pty = p.type_name;
            if is_ranged_int_type_chk(pty) { pty = "Int"; }
            self.vars.register(p.name, ty_from_name(pty));
            self.mark_mut_capable(p.name, p.is_mut_ref);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        self.check_block(f.body);
    }

    // Canonical "what real JVM type is this, for signature-matching purposes" key for a DECLARED
    // (HC-level) type name -- mirrors the real compiler's own `realTyKey` (see `JavaReflect.kt`'s
    // header comment for why this port exists: the self-hosted subset had NO real classpath
    // reflection at all, only "trust the declared signature" honesty). `"?"` means
    // "unrepresentable for this comparison" (an HC-native struct/enum, matching the real
    // compiler's own `null` sentinel there) -- `verify_extern_signature` skips the whole check
    // when it sees this, rather than risk a false-positive mismatch for a shape this port doesn't
    // understand. `self.externs` binary names are stored EXACTLY as declared (dot or slash form
    // alike -- only `Codegen.hotc`'s own `dot_to_slash_cg` normalizes them, at bytecode-emission
    // time, not here), so this converts dot-to-slash itself before building the key, to compare
    // correctly against `real_method_key`'s own always-slash-form (`Class.getName()` normalized).
    fn declared_type_to_key(&mut self, type_name0: String) -> String {
        // A nullable type reflects/verifies identically to its non-null form -- the real JVM has
        // no separate "nullable" signature shape at all, so stripping this here (rather than
        // falling through to the "?" = "unrepresentable, skip the check" catch-all below) means a
        // nullable extern method signature still gets genuinely verified, not silently trusted.
        var type_name = type_name0;
        if is_nullable_type_name(type_name) { type_name = strip_nullable(type_name); }
        if type_name == "Int" { return "I"; }
        if type_name == "Long" { return "J"; }
        if type_name == "Float" { return "F"; }
        if type_name == "Double" { return "D"; }
        if type_name == "Bool" { return "Z"; }
        if type_name == "Char" { return "C"; }
        if type_name == "Byte" { return "B"; }
        if type_name == "Unit" { return "V"; }
        if type_name == "String" { return "Ljava/lang/String;"; }
        if is_array_type_name(type_name) {
            return "[" + self.declared_type_to_key(array_elem_type_name(type_name));
        }
        let bin_found = self.externs.get(type_name);
        if let Some { value: bin } = bin_found {
            return "L" + dot_to_slash_reflect(bin) + ";";
        }
        return "?";
    }

    // Real gap found trying to give `Checker.hotc` real classpath reflection at all: an extern
    // method's DECLARED signature was never checked against the real class it names -- a wrong
    // return type or param type compiled clean and only surfaced as a runtime `NoSuchMethodError`.
    // Deliberately narrower than the real compiler's own `verifyExternSignatures`: only the
    // explicit-signature form (this subset's ONLY extern-method form -- no `use { ... }`, no lazy
    // resolution, no `Ty.Dyn` bound-interface params) needs porting at all. Still NOT wired into
    // `register_extern`'s own automatic flow (that would need `Checker`'s own construction site
    // threaded a classpath, which none of `run_bootstrap_demo`/`run_selfcompile_*`/`run_cli` do --
    // a bigger, separate change to every one of those call sites) -- callable directly wherever a
    // caller already has a real classpath available. `classpath` used to be implicit (always the
    // context classloader, real, disclosed gap) -- now a real `Vec<String>` of jar/dir paths,
    // resolved via `build_classpath_loader` below, same as the real compiler's own
    // `ClasspathReflector` constructor. An empty `Vec` keeps the old context-classloader behavior
    // (see `class_exists_reflect`'s own header for why that path still exists separately). Taken
    // by REFERENCE (`&Vec<String>`), not by value -- `Vec` isn't `Copy` (move-only, same as every
    // other non-`Copy` type in this subset), and this fn needs to pass it to BOTH
    // `class_exists_reflect` and `resolve_real_methods` below; by-value would move it into the
    // first call and leave the second a compile error ("use of moved value"), a real mistake found
    // running this exact code through the REAL compiler's own move-checker (see `Codegen.hotc`'s
    // own `descriptor_of_type`/`extern_descriptor_of_type` for the established `&Vec<String>`
    // reborrow-and-forward convention this mirrors).
    fn verify_extern_signature(&mut self, alias_name: String, binary_name: String, method_name: String, param_type_names: Vec<String>, ret_type_name: String, is_static: Bool, is_ctor: Bool, classpath: &Vec<String>) {
        if !class_exists_reflect(binary_name, &classpath) { return; } // not on this classpath -- nothing to check against
        let real_methods = resolve_real_methods(binary_name, method_name, is_ctor, &classpath);
        if real_methods.length() == 0 {
            self.error("extern class '" + alias_name + "': no public member '" + method_name + "' found on '" + binary_name + "' on the configured classpath");
            return;
        }
        var declared_keys = empty_str_vec2();
        var any_unknown = false;
        var i = 0;
        while i < param_type_names.length() {
            let k = self.declared_type_to_key(param_type_names.get(i));
            if k == "?" { any_unknown = true; }
            declared_keys.push(k);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        var ret_key = "V";
        if !is_ctor {
            ret_key = self.declared_type_to_key(ret_type_name);
            if ret_key == "?" { any_unknown = true; }
        }
        // Any unrepresentable type means this declaration could never correspond to a real Java
        // member anyway -- skip rather than guess, same "degrade to no check" reasoning
        // `declared_type_to_key`'s own header already uses.
        if any_unknown { return; }
        var matched = false;
        var j = 0;
        while j < real_methods.length() {
            let rm = real_methods.get(j);
            if rm.is_static == is_static && str_vec_eq_reflect(&rm.param_keys, &declared_keys) {
                if is_ctor {
                    matched = true;
                } else if rm.ret_key == ret_key {
                    matched = true;
                }
            }
            j = j + 1;
        }
        if !matched {
            self.error("extern class '" + alias_name + "': declared '" + method_name + "' doesn't match any real overload of '" + binary_name + "." + method_name + "' on the configured classpath");
        }
    }
}

// === Real classpath reflection, ported from `JavaReflect.kt`'s `ClasspathReflector` ===
// The self-hosted subset had NO real classpath reflection at all before this -- `extern class`
// declarations were pure "trust me" honesty, matching this file's own long-standing header.
// `try`/`catch` (see `Ast.hc`'s own `Try` header) exists specifically to make this possible:
// `Class.forName` genuinely throws for a missing class, and there's no other way to turn that
// into "return false/empty" instead of crashing the whole compiler.

extern class JClassRefX = "java.lang.Class" {
    static fn forName(name: String) -> JClassRefX;
    fn getName(&self) -> String;
    fn isArray(&self) -> Bool;
    fn isPrimitive(&self) -> Bool;
    fn getComponentType(&self) -> JClassRefX;
    fn getMethods(&self) -> [JMethodRefX];
    fn getConstructors(&self) -> [JConstructorRefX];
}
extern class JMethodRefX = "java.lang.reflect.Method" {
    fn getName(&self) -> String;
    fn getParameterTypes(&self) -> [JClassRefX];
    fn getReturnType(&self) -> JClassRefX;
    fn getModifiers(&self) -> Int;
}
extern class JConstructorRefX = "java.lang.reflect.Constructor" {
    fn getParameterTypes(&self) -> [JClassRefX];
    fn getModifiers(&self) -> Int;
}
extern class JModifierRefX = "java.lang.reflect.Modifier" {
    static fn isPublic(mod: Int) -> Bool;
    static fn isStatic(mod: Int) -> Bool;
}
extern class JClassNotFoundRefX = "java.lang.ClassNotFoundException" {}

// A real `--classpath` (a list of jar/dir paths), resolved the same way the real compiler's own
// `ClasspathReflector` constructor does: each path -> `File` -> `URI` -> `URL`, fed to a real
// `URLClassLoader`. `URLClassLoader(URL[])` (no explicit parent) already delegates to the system
// classloader first, so this still finds ordinary JDK classes (`java.lang.String`, etc) even when
// nothing in `classpath` provides them, same as the plain `Class.forName` path always did.
extern class JFileRefX = "java.io.File" {
    fn new(path: String) -> Self;
    fn toURI(&self) -> JURIRefX;
}
extern class JURIRefX = "java.net.URI" {
    fn toURL(&self) -> JURLRefX;
}
extern class JURLRefX = "java.net.URL" {}
extern class JURLClassLoaderRefX = "java.net.URLClassLoader" {
    fn new(urls: [JURLRefX]) -> Self;
    fn loadClass(&self, name: String) -> JClassRefX;
}

// One real, reflected overload -- `param_keys`/`ret_key` already in the same canonical-key form
// `declared_type_to_key` produces, so `verify_extern_signature` can compare them directly with a
// plain string/vec equality check.
pub struct ReflectedMethod {
    is_static: Bool,
    param_keys: Vec<String>,
    ret_key: String,
}

// Builds the real classloader for a non-empty `classpath`. `[value; count]` (`ArrayRepeat`) needs a
// real seed VALUE, not just a length, to build the `[JURLRefX]` array `URLClassLoader::new` takes
// (this subset has no "build a native array from a runtime-length list" construct otherwise) --
// `classpath.get(0)` is always safe to use as that seed here since every call site below only
// reaches this fn when `classpath.length() > 0` (checked first, see `class_exists_reflect`'s own
// header for why the empty case is handled as a wholly separate path instead of going through an
// empty/dummy `URLClassLoader`).
pub fn build_classpath_loader(classpath: &Vec<String>) -> JURLClassLoaderRefX {
    let seed_file = JFileRefX::new(classpath.get(0));
    let seed_uri = seed_file.toURI();
    var urls = [seed_uri.toURL(); classpath.length()];
    var i = 0;
    while i < classpath.length() {
        let f = JFileRefX::new(classpath.get(i));
        let uri = f.toURI();
        urls[i] = uri.toURL();
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return JURLClassLoaderRefX::new(urls);
}

// `classpath` empty keeps the ORIGINAL behavior (plain `Class.forName`, i.e. the context
// classloader only) rather than building a real-but-pointless zero-entry `URLClassLoader` --
// `URLClassLoader(URL[0])` would actually behave identically (it still delegates to the system
// classloader first), but skipping it avoids the ceremony for the common no-classpath call sites
// (every one of `run_bootstrap_demo`'s own bootstrap demos, which never pass a classpath at all).
// `pub` (unlike `resolve_real_methods`/`build_classpath_loader` below, both only ever called from
// WITHIN this file) -- `Driver.hotc`'s own `run_classpath_verify_probe` calls this directly, and a
// plain top-level `fn` called cross-file compiles cleanly under this bootstrap's flat source-level
// namespace but fails at class-LOAD time with a real `IllegalAccessError` (the actual generated
// bytecode puts it as a non-public static method on THIS file's own class -- unlike an `impl`
// block's own methods, e.g. `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_fn`, which stay callable cross-file even
// without `pub`; only bare top-level fns need it). Found running this exact probe.
pub fn class_exists_reflect(binary_name: String, classpath: &Vec<String>) -> Bool {
    try {
        var c = JClassRefX::forName("java.lang.Object"); // dummy, guaranteed-safe init -- see below
        if classpath.length() > 0 {
            let loader = build_classpath_loader(&classpath);
            c = loader.loadClass(binary_name);
        } else {
            c = JClassRefX::forName(binary_name);
        }
        return true;
    } catch (e: JClassNotFoundRefX) {
        return false;
    }
}

// `"dyn Name"` -- see `Parser.hotc`'s own `type_name_ref` header. `_chk`-suffixed (same
// dodge-the-flat-namespace convention as `Codegen.hotc`'s own `_cg` duplicates) since
// `Codegen.hotc` needs its own identical pair (`is_dyn_type_name_cg`/`dyn_interface_name_cg`) and
// this whole subset's flat top-level function namespace would otherwise collide the two.
pub fn is_dyn_type_name_chk(name: String) -> Bool {
    if name.length() < 4 { return false; }
    return name.substring(0, 4) == "dyn ";
}

pub fn dyn_interface_name_chk(name: String) -> String {
    return name.substring(4, name.length());
}

// `"arena Name"` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `ArenaNew` header.
pub fn is_arena_type_name_chk(name: String) -> Bool {
    if name.length() < 6 { return false; }
    return name.substring(0, 6) == "arena ";
}

extern class JIntegerChk = "java.lang.Integer" {
    static fn parseInt(s: String) -> Int;
}

// `"Int<0..100>"` / `"Int<0..=100>"` -- numeric range bounds, see `Parser.hotc`'s own `type_
// name_ref` header for the full design and `IDEAS.md`'s own "Numeric range bounds" entry.
// `_chk`-suffixed for the same reason `is_dyn_type_name_chk` right above is: `Codegen.hotc` needs
// its own identical mirror (`_cg`-suffixed) and this subset's flat top-level namespace would
// otherwise collide the two.
pub fn is_ranged_int_type_chk(name: String) -> Bool {
    if name.length() < 5 { return false; }
    return name.substring(0, 4) == "Int<";
}

pub fn ranged_int_lo_chk(name: String) -> Int {
    let inner = name.substring(4, name.length() - 1);
    let dd = inner.indexOf("..");
    return JIntegerChk::parseInt(inner.substring(0, dd));
}

pub fn ranged_int_hi_chk(name: String) -> Int {
    let inner = name.substring(4, name.length() - 1);
    let dd = inner.indexOf("..");
    var hi_start = dd + 2;
    if inner.substring(dd + 2, dd + 3) == "=" { hi_start = dd + 3; }
    return JIntegerChk::parseInt(inner.substring(hi_start, inner.length()));
}

pub fn ranged_int_inclusive_chk(name: String) -> Bool {
    let inner = name.substring(4, name.length() - 1);
    let dd = inner.indexOf("..");
    return inner.substring(dd + 2, dd + 3) == "=";
}

pub fn arena_struct_name_chk(name: String) -> String {
    return name.substring(6, name.length());
}

// Same duplicated-helper pattern `Codegen.hotc`'s own `dot_to_slash_cg` already established
// (`_cg`-suffixed there to dodge the flat cross-module function namespace, `_reflect`-suffixed
// here for the same reason) -- `self.externs`' own binary names are stored exactly as declared
// (dot or slash form alike), but `real_method_key`'s own keys are always slash-form
// (`Class.getName()`, normalized the same way), so `declared_type_to_key` needs this too.
pub fn dot_to_slash_reflect(name: String) -> String {
    var out = "";
    var i = 0;
    while i < name.length() {
        let c = name.substring(i, i + 1);
        if c == "." {
            out = out + "/";
        } else {
            out = out + c;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// By reference, not by value -- a real, disclosed latent bug found alongside the classpath port
// (masked until now: every previous caller only ever hit ONE real overload per `member_name`, so
// `declared_keys` being moved into the FIRST loop iteration's call never got reused by a second).
// `verify_extern_signature`'s own `while j < real_methods.length()` loop calls this once per real
// overload -- for a genuinely overloaded method (more than one match), a by-value `declared_keys`
// would move on the first iteration and fail to compile ("use of moved value") on the second.
pub fn str_vec_eq_reflect(a: &Vec<String>, b: &Vec<String>) -> Bool {
    if a.length() != b.length() { return false; }
    var i = 0;
    while i < a.length() {
        if a.get(i) != b.get(i) { return false; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// Mirrors the real compiler's own `javaClassToTyOrNull` + `realTyKey`, fused into one step since
// this port skips the intermediate `Ty` reconstruction entirely. `Float`/`Double` are now handled
// (this subset ported them -- see `Types.hotc`'s own header) -- `isPrimitive()` without a name
// match below still falls through to `"?"` for `byte`/`short`/`char`, matching the real compiler's
// own "unrepresentable, skip" treatment for exactly those. `Class.getName()` (not `==` against
// `Integer.TYPE`-style constants,
// which would need real static-FIELD reflection this subset doesn't have) is the only signal
// available for telling primitives apart -- it returns the primitive's own bare name ("int",
// "long", ...) for exactly those, so string-comparing it is both simpler and sufficient.
pub fn real_method_key(c: JClassRefX) -> String {
    if c.isArray() {
        let comp = c.getComponentType();
        return "[" + real_method_key(comp);
    }
    let name = c.getName();
    if name == "int" { return "I"; }
    if name == "long" { return "J"; }
    if name == "float" { return "F"; }
    if name == "double" { return "D"; }
    if name == "boolean" { return "Z"; }
    if name == "void" { return "V"; }
    if name == "java.lang.String" { return "Ljava/lang/String;"; }
    if c.isPrimitive() { return "?"; }
    return "L" + dot_to_slash_reflect(name) + ";";
}

pub fn empty_reflected_method_vec() -> Vec<ReflectedMethod> {
    var v = vec_of(ReflectedMethod { is_static: false, param_keys: empty_str_vec2(), ret_key: "?" });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

// `member_name` is ignored for a constructor lookup (`is_ctor`) -- every public constructor is a
// candidate, same as the real compiler's own `memberName == "new"` branch. The lone `catch`
// clause is never actually expected to fire here (`verify_extern_signature` already confirmed
// `class_exists_reflect(binary_name, classpath)` before calling this, with the SAME `classpath`),
// but `try` still requires at least one -- same restriction this subset's own grammar puts on it,
// mirroring the real compiler's.
pub fn resolve_real_methods(binary_name: String, member_name: String, is_ctor: Bool, classpath: &Vec<String>) -> Vec<ReflectedMethod> {
    var out = empty_reflected_method_vec();
    try {
        var c = JClassRefX::forName("java.lang.Object"); // dummy -- see `class_exists_reflect`'s own header
        if classpath.length() > 0 {
            let loader = build_classpath_loader(&classpath);
            c = loader.loadClass(binary_name);
        } else {
            c = JClassRefX::forName(binary_name);
        }
        if is_ctor {
            let ctors = c.getConstructors();
            var i = 0;
            while i < ctors.length {
                let ctor = ctors[i];
                let mods = ctor.getModifiers();
                if JModifierRefX::isPublic(mods) {
                    let ptypes = ctor.getParameterTypes();
                    var pkeys = empty_str_vec2();
                    var j = 0;
                    while j < ptypes.length {
                        pkeys.push(real_method_key(ptypes[j]));
                        j = j + 1;
                    }
                    out.push(ReflectedMethod { is_static: false, param_keys: pkeys, ret_key: "V" });
                }
                i = i + 1;
            }
        } else {
            let methods = c.getMethods();
            var i2 = 0;
            while i2 < methods.length {
                let m = methods[i2];
                let mods2 = m.getModifiers();
                if m.getName() == member_name && JModifierRefX::isPublic(mods2) {
                    let ptypes2 = m.getParameterTypes();
                    var pkeys2 = empty_str_vec2();
                    var j2 = 0;
                    while j2 < ptypes2.length {
                        pkeys2.push(real_method_key(ptypes2[j2]));
                        j2 = j2 + 1;
                    }
                    let rkey = real_method_key(m.getReturnType());
                    out.push(ReflectedMethod { is_static: JModifierRefX::isStatic(mods2), param_keys: pkeys2, ret_key: rkey });
                }
                i2 = i2 + 1;
            }
        }
    } catch (e2: JClassNotFoundRefX) {
    }
    return out;
}

